window.AAYAT_POSTS=[{"id":"1.1.a","kind":"pre","title":"Psychological tactics (intimidation, urgency, authority, scarcity)","topic":"1.1","href":"unit-1/1.1/1.1.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.1/1.1.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Social engineering is the art of manipulating people into taking an action that benefits an\nattacker: clicking a link, revealing a password, wiring money, or holding a door open. Unlike a\nsoftware exploit, which targets a flaw in code, social engineering targets predictable flaws in\n<strong>human decision-making</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Here's the mental model worth keeping for the whole unit: every social-engineering message is an attempt to make you skip your own internal verification step. Urgency skips it by racing the clock. Authority skips it by outsourcing your judgment to someone else's. Scarcity skips it by turning verification into a cost (\"if I check, I'll miss the deal\"). Intimidation skips it by making <em>not</em> complying feel dangerous. Once you see all four as variations on one move, disable your slow, careful thinking, you stop needing to memorize scam templates, because the templates change every year but the move never does.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Only careless people fall for it.\" → Anyone under stress, time pressure, or distraction is vulnerable; tactics target universal psychology, not intelligence.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify the four core psychological pressure tactics in a social-engineering message and explain <em>why</em> each one lowers a victim's guard.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.1.a","kind":"post","title":"Psychological tactics (intimidation, urgency, authority, scarcity)","topic":"1.1","href":"unit-1/1.1/1.1.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.1/1.1.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Notice that not one of these four tactics required the attacker to know anything about computers. That's the whole point of starting the course here: before you learn to harden a server or read a packet capture, you need to internalize that the cheapest, most reliable attack surface in any organization is a human being's nervous system under pressure. Every technical control you'll learn for the rest of this course, MFA, segmentation, detection rules, exists downstream of an attacker successfully doing what we practiced today. Getting this instinct right early pays off in every unit that follows.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Social engineering</strong>: manipulating people (not machines) into actions that aid an attacker.</li>\n<li><strong>Urgency</strong>: manufactured time pressure that suppresses careful verification.</li>\n<li><strong>Authority</strong>: borrowed legitimacy (boss, IT, government) used to compel compliance.</li>\n<li><strong>Scarcity</strong>: implied limited availability that triggers fear of missing out.</li>\n<li><strong>Intimidation</strong>: threats that create fear so the target complies to remove the threat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Only careless people fall for it.\" → Anyone under stress, time pressure, or distraction is vulnerable; tactics target universal psychology, not intelligence.</li>\n<li>\"If it has a logo, it's real.\" → Logos and sender names are trivially spoofed; verify the <em>request</em>, not the branding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.1.A, 1.1.B</p>"},{"id":"1.1.b","kind":"pre","title":"Elicitation; malicious links and files","topic":"1.1","href":"unit-1/1.1/1.1.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.1/1.1.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Social engineering has two operational phases. The first is pressure, the psychological levers\ncovered in 1.1.a. The second is extraction and delivery: getting the victim to surrender\ninformation through conversation, or to open something dangerous.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Think of elicitation and malicious links as two different keys to the same lock. Elicitation gets the attacker information; links and files get them execution. A useful intuition: elicitation is <em>slow and social</em>, it works over minutes or days of conversation, and each fragment feels harmless in isolation, which is exactly why it's dangerous. Link and file attacks are <em>fast and technical</em>, one click, one moment of inattention, and it's over. Defending against the first requires a habit of mind (need-to-know discipline); defending against the second requires a habit of hand (hover before you click, check the extension before you open).</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"HTTPS means the site is safe.\" → HTTPS only means the connection is encrypted. Attackers routinely obtain free TLS certificates for malicious domains; the padlock proves encryption, not legitimacy.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Describe how elicitation extracts sensitive information through conversation, and you can identify the technical indicators that distinguish a malicious link or file from a legitimate one.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.1.b","kind":"post","title":"Elicitation; malicious links and files","topic":"1.1","href":"unit-1/1.1/1.1.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.1/1.1.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The right-to-left URL-reading rule is one of the highest-leverage five seconds you'll spend in this entire course. Almost every lookalike-domain attack banks on the reader's eye landing on the trusted brand name wherever it appears in the string, rather than immediately before the first slash. Once that habit is automatic, an astonishing fraction of phishing links stop working on you, not because you got smarter about attacks in general, but because you fixed one specific, mechanical reading error that every browser and every attacker already knows humans make by default.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elicitation</strong>: covert extraction of information through conversation, without the target realizing that sensitive details are being gathered.</li>\n<li><strong>Typosquatting</strong>: registering a domain visually similar to a trusted brand to deceive users.</li>\n<li><strong>Homograph attack</strong>: using visually identical characters from different scripts in a domain name to impersonate a legitimate domain.</li>\n<li><strong>IP-literal URL</strong>: a URL using a raw IP address instead of a hostname, often signaling deception because legitimate services rarely use this form in communications.</li>\n<li><strong>Mismatched link</strong>: a hyperlink whose visible anchor text differs from its actual href destination.</li>\n<li><strong>Dangerous extension</strong>: a file extension (.exe, .scr, .ps1, .vbs, etc.) indicating the file can execute arbitrary code when opened.</li>\n<li><strong>Double extension</strong>: a filename like <code>report.pdf.exe</code> used to disguise an executable as a harmless file type by exploiting OS defaults that hide the final extension.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"HTTPS means the site is safe.\" → HTTPS only means the connection is encrypted. Attackers routinely obtain free TLS certificates for malicious domains; the padlock proves encryption, not legitimacy.</li>\n<li>\"Antivirus will catch every malicious file.\" → Novel malware and fileless payloads frequently evade signature-based detection. Recognizing dangerous extensions adds a layer that does not depend on vendor updates.</li>\n<li>\"I would notice a fake domain.\" → Homograph and typosquatting attacks are specifically designed to defeat casual visual inspection. Always read URLs from right to left, methodically.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.1.A, 1.1.C</p>"},{"id":"1.1.c","kind":"pre","title":"Email, text, and social media attacks","topic":"1.1","href":"unit-1/1.1/1.1.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.1/1.1.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> The tactics covered in 1.1.a (urgency, authority, scarcity, intimidation) and the link and file\nindicators from 1.1.b are the content of social-engineering attacks. This skill examines the\ndelivery channels (the pipes through which that content reaches victims) and why each channel\nhas its own set of exploitable trust assumptions.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> A useful way to hold email, SMS, and social media together in your head: each channel is effective for a <em>different</em> reason, not the same reason wearing a different costume. Email works because it looks institutional. SMS works because it feels personal and urgent, a text landing on the phone in your pocket carries a different weight than an email in a crowded inbox. Social media works because it borrows your existing trust in a specific person or brand you already follow. An attacker who has been rebuffed on one channel doesn't give up; they just switch to a channel where your guard is calibrated differently.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"I can always tell a phishing email by its grammar.\" → Spear phishing and AI-generated phishing (1.4.b) can be grammatically perfect and contextually accurate. Grammar is an unreliable signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify the distinguishing features of phishing attacks delivered over email, SMS, and social media, and explain how channel-specific trust cues make each delivery method effective.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.1.c","kind":"post","title":"Email, text, and social media attacks","topic":"1.1","href":"unit-1/1.1/1.1.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.1/1.1.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The multi-channel \"pre-warning\" pattern described here, a text that says \"expect an email from us\" followed by the actual phishing email, is worth sitting with, because it's a small example of a much bigger idea you'll see again in this course: attackers build <em>social proof</em> across independent-seeming signals to defeat verification. The single defense that generalizes across every channel is the same one from 1.1.a: verify through a path the message itself didn't give you. Look up the number yourself. Don't use the one in the text.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phishing</strong>: a mass social-engineering attack via email (or another channel) that impersonates a trusted entity to harvest credentials or deliver malware.</li>\n<li><strong>Spear phishing</strong>: a targeted phishing attack crafted using intelligence about a specific person or organization.</li>\n<li><strong>Whaling</strong>: spear phishing directed at high-value targets such as executives.</li>\n<li><strong>Smishing</strong>: phishing delivered via SMS (text message).</li>\n<li><strong>Social media impersonation</strong>: creating a fake account that closely mimics a real person or organization to deceive their contacts.</li>\n<li><strong>Business email compromise (BEC)</strong>: an attack in which the attacker impersonates an executive or business partner to authorize fraudulent transfers or disclose sensitive data.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"I can always tell a phishing email by its grammar.\" → Spear phishing and AI-generated phishing (1.4.b) can be grammatically perfect and contextually accurate. Grammar is an unreliable signal.</li>\n<li>\"Only email matters; texts are safe.\" → Smishing is growing rapidly and often bypasses the security awareness training that focuses on email.</li>\n<li>\"Checking the sender name is enough.\" → Display names are trivially spoofed. Always inspect the actual sending address, not just what appears in the \"From:\" field.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.1.A, 1.1.C</p>"},{"id":"1.2.a","kind":"pre","title":"Password-attack signs (failed attempts, unusual times, unknown devices)","topic":"1.2","href":"unit-1/1.2/1.2.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.2/1.2.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> When an attacker wants access to an account they do not own, the simplest path is to guess the\npassword. Modern attackers do not guess slowly and manually, they use automated tools that can\nsubmit hundreds or thousands of login attempts per minute. The digital fingerprints those attempts\nleave in authentication logs are predictable, and recognizing them is one of the most fundamental\nskills in defensive security.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The professor's shortcut for this whole atom: a single anomaly is a coincidence; a stack of anomalies is an incident. Five failed logins alone might just be someone who forgot their password twice and fat-fingered it three more times. Five failed logins from a device you've never seen, at 3 a.m., ending in a success? Each individual fact is explainable on its own, it's the <em>conjunction</em> that collapses the space of innocent explanations. This is the same correlation principle real SIEM systems are built around, and you'll see it again every time this course asks you to combine weak signals into a strong verdict.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A few failed logins are not a big deal.\" → A few in an hour is noise. Five in sixty seconds is a strong indicator of automation; the rate, not the raw count, is what matters.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Read an authentication log, identify the three key signals of a password attack (repeated failed attempts, logins at anomalous times, and access from unknown devices), and write a rule that flags brute-force attempts automatically.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.2.a","kind":"post","title":"Password-attack signs (failed attempts, unusual times, unknown devices)","topic":"1.2","href":"unit-1/1.2/1.2.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.2/1.2.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Notice something quietly important buried in \"what happens after detection\": a lockout stops future guesses, but it does nothing to undo a success that already happened before the threshold tripped. That's why the response checklist ends with \"review the logs to determine whether a successful login occurred before the lockout\", detection isn't the finish line, it's the trigger for an investigation. Carry that distinction with you into every later detection topic in this course: catching the pattern and confirming the blast radius are two separate jobs.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Brute-force attack</strong>: automated, exhaustive attempt to guess a credential by trying many possible values.</li>\n<li><strong>Dictionary attack</strong>: brute-force variant that tries passwords from a wordlist rather than every possible character combination.</li>\n<li><strong>Credential stuffing</strong>: using username/password pairs from a known breach to attack other services, exploiting password reuse.</li>\n<li><strong>Authentication failure spike</strong>: a rapid burst of failed login events for the same account, a primary indicator of automated attack.</li>\n<li><strong>Behavioral baseline</strong>: the normal pattern of activity for an account; deviations from it trigger anomaly-based alerts.</li>\n<li><strong>Correlation</strong>: combining multiple signals to reduce false positives in security detection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A few failed logins are not a big deal.\" → A few in an hour is noise. Five in sixty seconds is a strong indicator of automation; the rate, not the raw count, is what matters.</li>\n<li>\"The attacker needs to know my username to try passwords.\" → Credential stuffing uses lists of real usernames from breaches. On Wi-Fi routers the admin username (<code>admin</code>) is often public knowledge from the manufacturer's manual.</li>\n<li>\"A locked account means I am safe.\" → Lockout stops the brute-force, but if a success event appears in the log before the lockout triggered, the account may already be compromised.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.2.A</p>"},{"id":"1.2.b","kind":"pre","title":"Weak authentication patterns; adversary dictionary construction","topic":"1.2","href":"unit-1/1.2/1.2.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.2/1.2.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> A password is only as strong as the assumptions an attacker has to defeat in order to guess it.\nWhen an attacker sits down to brute-force an account, they do not usually start at <code>aaaaaaa</code> and\ntry every possible combination, that is computationally expensive when the password space is\nlarge. Instead, they start with what works: a dictionary.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Here's the intuition that makes the whole math of password cracking click: an attacker is not trying to search the entire universe of possible passwords, they're trying to search the tiny universe of passwords a <em>human</em> would actually pick. Humans are bad random-number generators, we gravitate toward names, dates, keyboard patterns, and \"clever\" substitutions that feel unpredictable to us but are the very first thing every mangling rule tries. The dictionary attack isn't a brute-force fallback; for most real accounts, it's the <em>primary</em> attack, because it exploits human psychology, not computational limits.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Adding a number and symbol makes a password secure.\" → If the base word is in the dictionary, mangling rules will find the variant. Entropy comes from unpredictability and length.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Evaluate a password against the core weakness criteria (length, dictionary membership, character variety, and sequential/repeated patterns) and explain how adversaries build and use wordlists to attack weak passwords.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.2.b","kind":"post","title":"Weak authentication patterns; adversary dictionary construction","topic":"1.2","href":"unit-1/1.2/1.2.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.2/1.2.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The passphrase-versus-complexity argument is one of the few places in security where the math is genuinely counterintuitive on first hearing and then completely obvious once you see it: length multiplies the search space, an extra character class only adds one factor to it. A four-word random passphrase you can actually remember beats an eight-character <code>P@ssw0rd!</code> you can barely remember and that every mangling engine tries in its first ten thousand guesses. If you remember nothing else from this skill in five years, remember that length beats cleverness.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dictionary attack</strong>: brute-force strategy that tries passwords from a wordlist rather than exhaustive character combinations.</li>\n<li><strong>Wordlist / credential dictionary</strong>: file of candidate passwords ordered by likelihood, built from breach data, common patterns, and target-specific words.</li>\n<li><strong>Mangling rules</strong>: transformations (capitalize, substitute, append) applied to wordlist entries to generate variants.</li>\n<li><strong>Credential stuffing</strong>: reusing username/password pairs from one breach to attack other services; made effective by password reuse.</li>\n<li><strong>Password entropy</strong>: measure of how unpredictable a password is; increases with length and character variety, decreases with dictionary membership and patterns.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Adding a number and symbol makes a password secure.\" → If the base word is in the dictionary, mangling rules will find the variant. Entropy comes from unpredictability and length.</li>\n<li>\"Longer passwords are harder to remember, so shorter complex ones are better.\" → A four-word random passphrase is both longer and more memorable than <code>P@ssw0rd!</code>. Length wins on math.</li>\n<li>\"The attacker needs the password file to run a dictionary attack.\" → Online dictionary attacks (directly against a login form) do not need the hash. Offline attacks on stolen hashes are faster, but the dictionary approach applies to both.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.2.B</p>"},{"id":"1.2.c","kind":"pre","title":"Strengthening authentication: length/complexity, randomness, MFA","topic":"1.2","href":"unit-1/1.2/1.2.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.2/1.2.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> The previous atom showed what makes a password weak. This skill asks the harder question: what\nmakes authentication genuinely strong? The answer has three layers, length, randomness, and a\nsecond factor, and understanding all three changes how you design and evaluate authentication\nsystems.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> It's worth pausing on why TOTP specifically, not SMS, not email, became the default second factor: the whole scheme works <em>offline</em>, with zero network trust required at verification time. Both the phone and the server independently compute the same 30-second-windowed function of a shared secret and the clock. No code ever crosses a network where it could be intercepted, spoofed, or SIM-swapped. That's a fundamentally different security posture than SMS codes, which do travel over an inherently interceptable carrier network, and it's why this skill asks you to build TOTP from <code>hmac</code>/<code>hashlib</code> yourself: once you've computed a code by hand, \"the app just knows\" stops being magic and becomes math you own.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"MFA is unbreakable.\" → TOTP codes can be phished in real-time (the attacker relays the code immediately). MFA raises the bar dramatically but does not eliminate social engineering.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain why password length and randomness are the primary drivers of authentication strength, describe how Time-based One-Time Passwords work as a second factor, and implement a TOTP verifier using only the standard library.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.2.c","kind":"post","title":"Strengthening authentication: length/complexity, randomness, MFA","topic":"1.2","href":"unit-1/1.2/1.2.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.2/1.2.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The real lesson underneath the RFC 6238 mechanics is this: strong authentication isn't one strong thing, it's two <em>independent</em> weak things stacked so an attacker has to defeat both simultaneously through completely different means. A phished password and a phished TOTP code both individually compromise a factor, but an attacker who only got one of the two still has nothing. That's the actual security property MFA buys you, and it's why phishing-resistant factors (hardware keys) matter more than which specific app you use: the goal is factors that fail independently, not factors that feel different but fail together.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication)</strong>: requiring two or more factors from distinct categories (know/have/are) before granting access.</li>\n<li><strong>TOTP (Time-based One-Time Password)</strong>: a 6-digit code derived from HMAC-SHA1(secret, time_step), valid for one 30-second window, defined in RFC 6238.</li>\n<li><strong>HMAC (Hash-based Message Authentication Code)</strong>: a construction that uses a cryptographic hash function and a secret key to produce an authentication tag.</li>\n<li><strong>Time step</strong>: the integer floor(unix_timestamp / 30); changes every 30 seconds and synchronizes the server and authenticator without communication.</li>\n<li><strong>Dynamic truncation</strong>: the RFC 4226 method of extracting 4 bytes from a 20-byte HMAC-SHA1 output to produce a numeric OTP.</li>\n<li><strong>Entropy</strong>: measure of unpredictability; in passwords, increases with length and randomness; in TOTP, comes from the secret key and the short validity window.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"MFA is unbreakable.\" → TOTP codes can be phished in real-time (the attacker relays the code immediately). MFA raises the bar dramatically but does not eliminate social engineering.</li>\n<li>\"SMS codes and TOTP are the same security level.\" → SMS codes can be intercepted via SIM swapping; TOTP codes are computed locally and never sent over the phone network.</li>\n<li>\"A password manager makes passwords weaker because one breach exposes everything.\" → A good password manager encrypts the vault with a master password; losing the vault file without the master key reveals nothing. The tradeoff is worthwhile for the unique-per-site passwords it enables.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.2.C</p>"},{"id":"1.3.a","kind":"pre","title":"Adversary skill levels (low-skilled vs high-skilled / zero-days); motivation","topic":"1.3","href":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Not every attacker who shows up in a coffee shop with a laptop is equally dangerous. Understanding\nthe spectrum of adversary capability, from the curious teenager running a downloaded script to the\nnation-state operator wielding a custom radio exploit, helps you calibrate risk realistically rather\nthan either dismissing it or treating every threat as equally catastrophic.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Resist the instinct to imagine every attacker as a hooded figure in a dark room running custom exploits. The honest picture is a long-tailed distribution: an enormous number of low-skilled opportunists running free tools against anyone in range, and a small number of well-resourced, patient operators aiming precisely at specific high-value targets. The threat you actually face on a given afternoon at a coffee shop is overwhelmingly the first kind, which is good news, because the defenses against low-skilled, high-volume attacks (a VPN, basic skepticism, avoiding open networks) are cheap and effective, unlike defending against a funded zero-day operator.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Hackers on public Wi-Fi are all elite geniuses.\" → Most opportunistic attacks use free, widely available tools. The ceiling is high, but the floor is very low.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Classify a wireless attacker by skill tier based on the tools and techniques they use, and explain how motivation shapes which public Wi-Fi threats you face in practice.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.3.a","kind":"post","title":"Adversary skill levels (low-skilled vs high-skilled / zero-days); motivation","topic":"1.3","href":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Pairing skill tier with motivation is what turns this from trivia into a genuinely useful risk-assessment tool: the <em>same</em> observable behavior (someone scanning nearby Wi-Fi networks) means something completely different depending on whether the actor wants your credit card, your company's trade secrets, or just the thrill of proving they can do it. Every later topic in this unit, evil twins, jamming, war driving, is easier to reason about once you've internalized that the attacker's <em>goal</em> predicts their <em>method</em>, and the method predicts what evidence they'll leave behind.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low-skilled adversary</strong>: attacker who uses existing public tools without understanding the underlying mechanics; threat is broad but shallow.</li>\n<li><strong>High-skilled adversary</strong>: attacker who creates or modifies tools, understands full protocol stacks, and can adapt; threat is precise and deep.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero-day vulnerability</strong>: a security flaw unknown to the vendor, giving the defender zero days of advance warning; typically exploited only by high-skilled actors.</li>\n<li><strong>Motivation</strong>: the attacker's goal (financial gain, espionage, disruption, curiosity) that shapes which targets and methods they choose.</li>\n<li><strong>Script kiddie</strong>: informal term for a low-skilled attacker running tools they did not write and do not fully understand.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Hackers on public Wi-Fi are all elite geniuses.\" → Most opportunistic attacks use free, widely available tools. The ceiling is high, but the floor is very low.</li>\n<li>\"I have nothing worth stealing, so I am safe.\" → Low-skilled attackers often resell any captured credential in bulk; they do not prescreen victims.</li>\n<li>\"Zero-days only matter to governments.\" → Zero-day exploits are bought and sold commercially and have been used against journalists, lawyers, and activists.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.3.A</p>"},{"id":"1.3.b","kind":"pre","title":"Evil twin attack","topic":"1.3","href":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> When you connect to Wi-Fi in a cafe, airport, or hotel, your device searches for a network name\n(SSID) it recognizes or that you select from a list. It does not, by default, verify that the\naccess point broadcasting that name is the legitimate one.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The evil twin attack is a great case study in a pattern you'll see across this entire course: an attacker exploiting a gap between what a system <em>displays</em> and what it actually <em>verifies</em>. Your device shows you an SSID, a plain-text label anyone can broadcast, and calls that \"the network.\" It never asks the deeper question: which piece of hardware, identified by which BSSID, is actually on the other end? The name is theater; the BSSID is the closest thing to ground truth available, and even that only works if someone maintained an approved list to check it against.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"If I see HTTPS in my browser, the connection is safe even on public Wi-Fi.\" → SSL stripping can remove HTTPS before your browser receives the page. Always check for HTTPS and be suspicious if it is absent where you expected it.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how an evil twin attack works at the network level, identify the indicators that reveal a rogue access point in a wireless scan, and apply those indicators to detect one programmatically.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.3.b","kind":"post","title":"Evil twin attack","topic":"1.3","href":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Notice how directly this connects back to 1.3.a: setting up an evil twin requires no zero-day, no custom exploit, nothing beyond a laptop's built-in hotspot feature, which is exactly why it's a low-skilled attacker's favorite tool despite sounding technically sophisticated. That gap between \"sounds scary\" and \"costs nothing to execute\" is worth remembering: the attacks worth worrying about most on public Wi-Fi are rarely the exotic ones, they're the cheap, high-yield ones that exploit a blind spot everyone shares.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SSID (Service Set Identifier)</strong>: the human-readable name of a Wi-Fi network (e.g., \"CafeGuest\").</li>\n<li><strong>BSSID (Basic Service Set Identifier)</strong>: the hardware MAC address of the radio broadcasting the network; each physical access point has a unique BSSID.</li>\n<li><strong>Evil twin</strong>: a rogue access point broadcasting the same SSID as a legitimate network to intercept victim traffic.</li>\n<li><strong>Man-in-the-middle (MitM)</strong>: an attack where the adversary secretly relays and can read or modify communications between two parties.</li>\n<li><strong>SSL stripping</strong>: a MitM technique that downgrades an HTTPS connection to plain HTTP without the user noticing, exposing traffic to eavesdropping.</li>\n<li><strong>Captive portal</strong>: a web page shown to new network users, often requiring authentication; attackers use fake versions to harvest credentials.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"If I see HTTPS in my browser, the connection is safe even on public Wi-Fi.\" → SSL stripping can remove HTTPS before your browser receives the page. Always check for HTTPS and be suspicious if it is absent where you expected it.</li>\n<li>\"The same SSID appearing twice means my device is glitching.\" → Multiple BSSIDs for one SSID is normal in enterprise Wi-Fi, but an unexpected BSSID for a small public network is a strong indicator of an evil twin.</li>\n<li>\"Evil twin attacks need sophisticated equipment.\" → Any device that can broadcast Wi-Fi, including a smartphone, can be configured as an evil twin in minutes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.3.B</p>"},{"id":"1.3.c","kind":"pre","title":"Jamming / denial-of-service attack","topic":"1.3","href":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every Wi-Fi access point communicates by broadcasting radio waves on a shared spectrum.\nThe 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands used by Wi-Fi are unlicensed, meaning any device is legally\nallowed to transmit on them, subject to power limits set by the FCC or equivalent regulator.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Jamming is the one attack in this unit that isn't really about deception at all, there's no fake network, no stolen credential, nothing to trick you into trusting. It's brute physical denial: flood the shared medium with noise until CSMA/CA politeness forces every legitimate device to back off. The intuition worth keeping is that Wi-Fi's cooperative design, devices listening before they transmit so they don't collide, is precisely the mechanism a jammer weaponizes. The same courtesy that makes Wi-Fi work efficiently among cooperating devices makes it exploitable by one that refuses to cooperate.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"High noise always means jamming.\" → Transient interference from household devices is common on 2.4 GHz. A jamming attack is characterized by <em>sustained</em>, not momentary, elevated noise across several consecutive samples.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how radio-frequency jamming causes a wireless denial-of-service, describe what a sustained jamming event looks like in signal-strength monitoring data, and implement a detector that flags sustained interference in a sample series.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.3.c","kind":"post","title":"Jamming / denial-of-service attack","topic":"1.3","href":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The distinction between a jamming attack and ordinary microwave-oven interference is a small, concrete example of a detection principle that recurs constantly in security: it's rarely the presence of an anomaly that matters, it's the <em>duration</em>. A brief spike is noise; a sustained plateau is an event. You'll meet this exact reasoning again, a single failed login versus a sustained burst (1.2.a), a single loud sound versus continuous elevated noise here, because \"sustained over a threshold\" is one of the most broadly reusable detection heuristics in the entire field.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Jamming</strong>: deliberate transmission of radio-frequency noise to prevent legitimate wireless communication on a channel.</li>\n<li><strong>Denial-of-service (DoS)</strong>: an attack that makes a resource unavailable to legitimate users without necessarily compromising data.</li>\n<li><strong>RSSI (Received Signal Strength Indicator)</strong>: a measurement of the power level of a received radio signal, expressed in dBm (always negative for Wi-Fi).</li>\n<li><strong>Noise floor</strong>: the level of background radio noise on a channel; jamming raises this, reducing the usable signal-to-noise ratio.</li>\n<li><strong>CSMA/CA (Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Avoidance)</strong>, the access-control protocol Wi-Fi uses; devices back off when they detect noise, making them vulnerable to deliberate jamming.</li>\n<li><strong>Sustained anomaly</strong>: a condition maintained across multiple consecutive measurement samples, distinguishing attacks from transient interference.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"High noise always means jamming.\" → Transient interference from household devices is common on 2.4 GHz. A jamming attack is characterized by <em>sustained</em>, not momentary, elevated noise across several consecutive samples.</li>\n<li>\"Jamming is only a problem for military networks.\" → Civilian Wi-Fi is equally vulnerable; jammers are used in corporate espionage, exam-hall cheating, and as a physical-intrusion aid.</li>\n<li>\"Switching channels defeats a jammer.\" → It can help if the jammer targets a specific channel, but wideband jammers can cover all 2.4 GHz channels simultaneously.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.3.B</p>"},{"id":"1.3.d","kind":"pre","title":"War driving; protecting yourself (verify SSIDs, avoid open networks, VPN)","topic":"1.3","href":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.d/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.d/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> <strong>War driving</strong> is the practice of driving or walking through a geographic area with a\nlaptop, smartphone, or dedicated wireless scanner to discover, log, and map Wi-Fi networks.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> War driving is worth understanding less as an attack in itself and more as <em>reconnaissance infrastructure</em>, the map an attacker builds before deciding where to strike. What makes it unsettling is how little it costs: the tool that once required a car, a directional antenna, and real dedication is now a free app on a phone already in everyone's pocket. The barrier to <em>finding</em> a target has collapsed even where the barrier to <em>exploiting</em> one hasn't.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A password-protected network means I am safe.\" → WPA2 encrypts the air interface, but the access point operator can still read unencrypted traffic, and a compromised or malicious AP can intercept HTTPS via SSL stripping. A VPN provides an additional layer.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain what war driving is and why it enables targeted wireless attacks, evaluate a Wi-Fi network against a set of risk criteria, and apply a systematic risk-flagging process to decide whether connecting is safe.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.3.d","kind":"post","title":"War driving; protecting yourself (verify SSIDs, avoid open networks, VPN)","topic":"1.3","href":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.d/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.3/1.3.d/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The three-step protocol, verify, avoid open networks, VPN, is deliberately ordered, and the ordering itself teaches something: each step is a fallback for when the previous one fails. Verifying the SSID doesn't help if you can't actually verify it. Avoiding open networks doesn't help if the only option is open. The VPN is the last line specifically because it's the only one of the three that still protects you even when everything upstream of it went wrong, the same defense-in-depth logic you'll see formalized later in this course as a named principle, first encountered here as plain common sense.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>War driving</strong>: moving through a geographic area with a wireless scanner to discover and log Wi-Fi networks; used in legitimate research and as an attack-planning technique.</li>\n<li><strong>Open network</strong>: a Wi-Fi network requiring no password; all traffic is unencrypted at the wireless layer, visible to any device that joins or captures the channel.</li>\n<li><strong>VPN (Virtual Private Network)</strong>: software that creates an encrypted tunnel from your device to a trusted server before your traffic reaches the internet; protects against interception on hostile networks.</li>\n<li><strong>SSID verification</strong>: confirming the correct network name with venue staff or an official source before connecting, to avoid accidentally joining an evil twin or lookalike network.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk flag</strong>: a specific, nameable property of a network that raises the probability of interception or attack (e.g., \"open-network\", \"unverified-ssid\", \"no-vpn\").</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A password-protected network means I am safe.\" → WPA2 encrypts the air interface, but the access point operator can still read unencrypted traffic, and a compromised or malicious AP can intercept HTTPS via SSL stripping. A VPN provides an additional layer.</li>\n<li>\"I can tell if a network is legitimate by its name.\" → SSIDs are trivially spoofed. Only direct verification with venue staff is reliable.</li>\n<li>\"War driving is only done by sophisticated attackers.\" → Modern war-driving apps run on smartphones and require no technical skill; the barrier to reconnaissance is extremely low.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.3.B, 1.3.C</p>"},{"id":"1.4.a","kind":"pre","title":"Voice/image cloning for impersonation","topic":"1.4","href":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> For decades, the phrase \"I heard his voice, so I knew it was him\" carried real weight. A voice on\nthe phone, a face on a video call, or a signature on an image felt like trustworthy proof of\nidentity. That assumption is now dangerously fragile.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The unsettling reframe this skill asks you to make: for your entire life, hearing a familiar voice or seeing a familiar face has been <em>evidence</em> of identity, strong enough evidence that we built our entire social trust system on it. That evidence just became forgeable at consumer-hardware cost. The professor's intuition here is to stop asking \"does this sound/look real to me?\" (a losing arms race against improving models) and start asking \"does this interaction include something a clone <em>couldn't</em> have access to?\", a shared secret the model was never trained on, a callback to a number you already had.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"I can always tell if a voice is fake.\" → Current cloning quality fools trained humans the majority of the time. Perceptual detection is not a reliable control.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify the signals that suggest a voice or image has been AI-cloned and describe why deepfakes make identity verification harder for defenders.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.4.a","kind":"post","title":"Voice/image cloning for impersonation","topic":"1.4","href":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The phrase \"double-stack\" in this skill's closing paragraph is worth remembering as a template you'll reapply constantly: a cloned voice defeats your <em>recognition</em> heuristic, and manufactured urgency defeats your <em>verification</em> heuristic, and they're deployed together specifically because each one is disarming a different defense. Whenever an attack combines a trust exploit with a time-pressure exploit, assume it's not an accident, it's the attacker covering both of your natural safety checks at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Deepfake</strong>: synthetic media (audio, image, or video) in which AI replaces or fabricates a person's appearance or voice.</li>\n<li><strong>Voice cloning</strong>: fine-tuning a speech-synthesis model on a speaker's audio to produce new utterances in that speaker's voice.</li>\n<li><strong>Vishing</strong>: voice-based phishing; often combined with cloned audio.</li>\n<li><strong>Shared secret</strong>: a pre-agreed code word used to verify identity out-of-band, defeating audio/video impersonation.</li>\n<li><strong>Out-of-band confirmation</strong>: approval over a channel separate from the one being manipulated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"I can always tell if a voice is fake.\" → Current cloning quality fools trained humans the majority of the time. Perceptual detection is not a reliable control.</li>\n<li>\"Deepfakes require a lot of source material.\" → Three to ten seconds of audio is sufficient for modern voice cloning; a few public photos suffice for image synthesis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.4.A</p>"},{"id":"1.4.b","kind":"pre","title":"Generative-AI phishing; prompt injection against LLM apps","topic":"1.4","href":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Traditional phishing campaigns have always had a tell: volume. Sending ten thousand emails\nrequired a template, and templates left seams, awkward phrasing, inconsistent grammar, generic\ngreetings, mismatched signatures. Spam filters learned to score those signals.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The core economic insight here is worth sitting with because it explains an entire generation of security tooling becoming obsolete overnight: spam filters were never detecting <em>maliciousness</em> directly, they were detecting the <em>fingerprints of mass production</em>, awkward phrasing, generic greetings, inconsistent grammar. An LLM doesn't just write better phishing emails, it removes the fingerprint the filter was actually keying on. When the cost of a personalized, fluent, contextually accurate message drops to zero, \"this reads a little off\" stops being a signal you can rely on at all.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"AI-generated phishing is easy to spot because it sounds robotic.\" → Current LLMs produce text that is more fluent than many humans' writing. Stylistic detection is not reliable.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify prompt-injection patterns in user input and explain how generative AI lowers the cost of targeted phishing by bypassing grammar-based detection.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.4.b","kind":"post","title":"Generative-AI phishing; prompt injection against LLM apps","topic":"1.4","href":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Prompt injection deserves a mental model distinct from ordinary phishing: you're no longer defending a human from persuasion, you're defending a <em>system</em> that cannot tell the difference between an instruction from its owner and an instruction hiding inside data it was asked to process. That's a genuinely new category of vulnerability, not a repackaged old one, and it's why the strongest defenses here (privilege separation, human-in-the-loop) don't try to make the model smarter about spotting injections. They accept the model will sometimes be fooled and limit the blast radius of that failure instead, the same posture you'll see recur under the name \"assume breach\" later in this course.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Generative-AI phishing</strong>: phishing content produced by an LLM, which bypasses grammar-based filters by generating fluent, targeted, contextually appropriate text.</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt injection</strong>: an attack that smuggles adversarial instructions into LLM input so the model acts on attacker goals rather than operator goals.</li>\n<li><strong>Direct injection</strong>: the attacker controls the user input field of an LLM app directly.</li>\n<li><strong>Indirect injection</strong>: adversarial instructions are embedded in data the LLM processes on behalf of a legitimate user (emails, documents, web pages).</li>\n<li><strong>Jailbreak</strong>: an adversarial prompt designed to override the safety constraints or system-prompt instructions an LLM operator has established.</li>\n<li><strong>System prompt</strong>: operator-supplied instructions that define an LLM app's role, constraints, and behavior; provided before user interaction begins.</li>\n<li><strong>Privilege separation</strong>: confining the LLM to the minimum permissions required for its task so that even a successful injection cannot take high-impact actions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"AI-generated phishing is easy to spot because it sounds robotic.\" → Current LLMs produce text that is more fluent than many humans' writing. Stylistic detection is not reliable.</li>\n<li>\"Prompt injection only works if the attacker can type directly into the chatbot.\" → Indirect injection embeds instructions in external data (emails, PDFs, web pages) the LLM processes autonomously, with no direct attacker access to the interface.</li>\n<li>\"Adding 'you must not reveal your system prompt' to the system prompt prevents leakage.\" → Adversarial prompts routinely bypass such instructions. Structural controls (privilege separation, output validation) are more reliable than natural-language prohibitions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.4.A</p>"},{"id":"1.4.c","kind":"pre","title":"Prompt extraction of sensitive info","topic":"1.4","href":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> When a company deploys a chatbot, it typically writes a system prompt, a block of operator-supplied instructions that shapes the model's behavior before the user ever types a word. That system prompt might read: \"You are SecureBot for Meridian Bank. Never discuss competitor products.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The single sentence to hold onto from this whole atom: \"confidential\" is a policy the model is asked to follow, not a technical wall it cannot cross. Every token in the conversation, the system prompt, the user's message, the model's own prior output, sits in the same context window with no structural fence between \"instructions\" and \"content.\" Asking a model to keep a secret is closer to asking a very obedient employee to keep a secret than to putting that secret in a locked safe. Obedient employees can still be talked into things.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"The system prompt is hidden from the model.\", The model reads and reasons over the full context window; \"confidential\" is a policy instruction the model tries to follow, not a technical barrier that prevents access. The model can be asked to repeat what it knows, and may comply.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify output patterns that indicate an LLM has leaked sensitive configuration or credentials, and explain what data classes are at risk and why a technical barrier alone cannot prevent extraction.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.4.c","kind":"post","title":"Prompt extraction of sensitive info","topic":"1.4","href":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> It's worth noticing the shape of the arms race this skill sets up: regex output filters catch what you anticipated, and a motivated adversary evades exactly that by encoding, splitting, or paraphrasing the secret. This is the same static-analysis-versus-creative-adversary tension you'll meet again in 1.5.b (code scanning) and 5.1.a (SQL injection detection), pattern matching is cheap, fast, and a genuinely useful first layer, but it is never, on its own, a complete defense against an adversary who knows the patterns you're matching against.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prompt extraction</strong>: eliciting an LLM's confidential context (system prompt, credentials) through adversarial queries that cause the model to quote or summarize what it was told to keep private</li>\n<li><strong>System prompt</strong>: operator-supplied instructions placed before user input in the context window; sets the model's persona, rules, and capabilities; often intended to be confidential</li>\n<li><strong>Output filter</strong>: a post-processing layer that inspects LLM output for policy violations (credential patterns, forbidden content) before the response is delivered to the end user</li>\n<li><strong>API key</strong>: a secret credential that grants programmatic access to a service; its presence in LLM output is always a leak and often exploitable in minutes</li>\n<li><strong>Data class</strong>: a category of sensitive information (credential, personal data, internal configuration) treated uniformly by a detection or redaction policy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"The system prompt is hidden from the model.\", The model reads and reasons over the full context window; \"confidential\" is a policy instruction the model tries to follow, not a technical barrier that prevents access. The model can be asked to repeat what it knows, and may comply.</li>\n<li>\"Regex can catch all leaks.\", Regex catches structured patterns you anticipated. A creative adversary can evade simple patterns through encoding, paraphrasing, or splitting the sensitive token. Regex is a useful layer, not a complete defense.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.4.A</p>"},{"id":"1.4.d","kind":"pre","title":"False information in LLM training sets (data poisoning)","topic":"1.4","href":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.d/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.d/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Machine learning models learn entirely from their training data. If that data is wrong, the model will be wrong in exactly the way the attacker intended. Data poisoning is the deliberate act of inserting mislabeled, corrupted, or specially crafted samples into a training dataset so that the resulting model behaves in a way the attacker chooses.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Data poisoning asks you to think about security in a place most people never consider a threat surface at all: the raw material a model learns from, before the model even exists. The intuition that makes this click is realizing a model has no concept of \"training\" versus \"deployment\" separate from the numbers it was shown, if the numbers say hostile language correlates with the label \"positive,\" the model doesn't know that's wrong, it just knows that's what the data said. Garbage in isn't just garbage out; carefully crafted garbage in is a <em>chosen</em> out.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Poisoning requires access to the model.\" → Poisoning targets the training data, which may be collected from public sources, crowd-sourced platforms, or user submissions, no model access needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify indicators of data poisoning in a labeled training dataset and explain how poisoned data degrades or manipulates AI model behavior.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.4.d","kind":"post","title":"False information in LLM training sets (data poisoning)","topic":"1.4","href":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.d/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.d/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The distinction between availability attacks (break it for everyone) and integrity/backdoor attacks (break it only in one specific, attacker-chosen way, while looking perfectly healthy everywhere else) is the part worth remembering longest, because backdoor attacks are unsettling precisely for how <em>invisible</em> they stay under normal evaluation. A model can pass every accuracy benchmark you throw at it and still contain a landmine that only detonates on a trigger the evaluator never thought to test. That's a sobering preview of why AI systems need the same kind of adversarial, not just functional, testing you'll apply to code throughout this course.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Data poisoning</strong>: intentionally corrupting training data to influence a model's behavior</li>\n<li><strong>Backdoor attack</strong>: embedding a trigger pattern in training data so the model misclassifies inputs containing that trigger</li>\n<li><strong>Label/content mismatch</strong>: a sample whose label contradicts the observable content</li>\n<li><strong>Outlier marker</strong>: a sample statistically anomalous relative to the rest of the dataset</li>\n<li><strong>Training integrity</strong>: the guarantee that training data has not been tampered with</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Poisoning requires access to the model.\" → Poisoning targets the training data, which may be collected from public sources, crowd-sourced platforms, or user submissions, no model access needed.</li>\n<li>\"A small number of poisoned samples cannot change a large model.\" → Targeted backdoors have been demonstrated with less than 1% poisoning rate; availability attacks can succeed with slightly higher rates but still well under 10%.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.4.A</p>"},{"id":"1.4.e","kind":"pre","title":"AI reconnaissance and enhanced malware coding; defenses (shared secrets, MFA, verify output)","topic":"1.4","href":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.e/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.e/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Before an attacker exploits a system, they study it. The study phase is called reconnaissance, and it has always been the most time-consuming part of an attack. An adversary who wants to break into a bank's network must first discover which subdomains exist, which software versions are running, which employees might be deceived, and which CVEs match those software versions.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The honest framing for this skill: AI hasn't invented new attack categories here, port scans, credential stuffing, and polymorphic malware all predate any of this, what changed is the unit economics. Reconnaissance that used to cost a skilled human a full day now costs a script a few minutes, and it scales to hundreds of targets in parallel. When you see \"847 DNS queries in 2 seconds,\" the point isn't the specific number, it's that automated speed itself becomes the detectable signature: no human browsing behavior looks like that, regardless of what the human's intent might have been.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Only skilled attackers use AI for recon.\", AI tools lower the skill floor substantially. Script-level actors can now conduct reconnaissance that previously required professional penetration testers. The threat model must account for low-sophistication attackers with AI-assisted capabilities.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Analyze a log of security events, detect AI-assisted reconnaissance patterns, and recommend targeted defenses.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.4.e","kind":"post","title":"AI reconnaissance and enhanced malware coding; defenses (shared secrets, MFA, verify output)","topic":"1.4","href":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.e/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.4/1.4.e/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Layer the four defenses here, shared secrets, MFA, verify output, behavioral anomaly detection, against the two attack phases, and a pattern emerges you'll see as a recurring design principle in security architecture: no single control addresses the whole kill chain, because reconnaissance, credential attack, and deception each require a <em>structurally different</em> countermeasure. Shared secrets defeat impersonation because a clone can't know something it was never trained on. MFA defeats credential theft because a password alone is insufficient. Behavioral detection defeats scale because automation leaves a statistical footprint no human replicates. Layered defense isn't redundancy for its own sake, each layer is answering a different question.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AI reconnaissance</strong>: using AI tools or LLMs to automate target profiling, OSINT aggregation, subdomain enumeration, and vulnerability enumeration at speeds and scales that exceed human capability</li>\n<li><strong>Polymorphic malware</strong>: malware that rewrites its own code on each execution to produce a novel binary signature, defeating signature-based antivirus detection; AI assistance reduces the expertise needed to generate such payloads</li>\n<li><strong>Shared secret</strong>: a value pre-agreed out-of-band between two parties that serves as proof of identity; effective against AI-generated impersonation because the AI cannot know information it was never trained on</li>\n<li><strong>MFA (multi-factor authentication)</strong>: authentication requiring two or more independent factors (something you know, something you have, something you are); blocks credential-stuffing attacks even when passwords are correct</li>\n<li><strong>Behavioral anomaly</strong>: a pattern of system events that deviates from the normal baseline in a statistically significant way; used by SIEM and IDS systems to flag automated recon activity</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Only skilled attackers use AI for recon.\", AI tools lower the skill floor substantially. Script-level actors can now conduct reconnaissance that previously required professional penetration testers. The threat model must account for low-sophistication attackers with AI-assisted capabilities.</li>\n<li>\"MFA stops all AI-driven attacks.\", MFA stops credential-stuffing and password-spray attacks. It does not stop attackers who exploit unpatched software vulnerabilities, who conduct prompt-bombing to trick users into approving a push notification, or who hijack an already-authenticated session. MFA is one layer in a stack, not a complete defense.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.4.A, 1.4.B</p>"},{"id":"1.5.a","kind":"pre","title":"AI reviewing security configurations","topic":"1.5","href":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every server, network device, and cloud service is controlled by a configuration file, a plain-text\ndocument that specifies exactly how the system behaves. Get that file right and you have a hardened\nsystem. Get it wrong and you have an open door.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The professor's frame for the whole AI-in-defense half of this unit: every tool from here on out follows the same pattern, AI does the exhaustive, mechanical, tireless part (does this directive match a known-bad pattern, across every line, with zero fatigue) and a human does the contextual judgment part (is this exception documented, is this a legitimate compensating control). Keep asking, for every AI-assisted tool you meet from here forward, \"what exactly is the machine automating, and what judgment is still reserved for the human?\", that question is the fastest way to actually understand what a tool does rather than just what it's called.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Default settings are safe because the vendor tested them.\" → Defaults are tuned for compatibility and ease of setup, not for security. Hardening requires deliberate reconfiguration.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Describe how AI-assisted tools audit security configuration files for common misconfigurations, identify specific insecure settings in a sample config, and explain why automated review catches issues that manual checklists often miss.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.5.a","kind":"post","title":"AI reviewing security configurations","topic":"1.5","href":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The rule-engine-versus-graph-model distinction quietly previews something you'll rediscover throughout this course: simpler, transparent detection logic (a fixed rule list) is often the <em>right</em> choice for a learning environment even when more sophisticated ML-based approaches exist, precisely because you can audit exactly why a finding fired. A security tool you can't explain the reasoning of is a tool you can't fully trust, no matter how accurate it tests.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Configuration file</strong>: a plain-text file specifying system behavior; the primary control surface for hardening.</li>\n<li><strong>Misconfiguration</strong>: a setting that deviates from security best practice, intentionally or not.</li>\n<li><strong>Rule engine</strong>: a component that checks each config directive against a database of known-bad patterns and emits findings.</li>\n<li><strong>PermitRootLogin</strong>: SSH directive controlling whether the root account can authenticate directly; should be <code>no</code> on any production system.</li>\n<li><strong>Hardening</strong>: the process of reducing a system's attack surface by disabling unnecessary services and correcting insecure defaults.</li>\n<li><strong>Shift-left security</strong>: moving security checks earlier in the development cycle, catching issues before they reach production.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Default settings are safe because the vendor tested them.\" → Defaults are tuned for compatibility and ease of setup, not for security. Hardening requires deliberate reconfiguration.</li>\n<li>\"Flagging a setting means it must be changed.\" → A finding is a question, not a command. The human reviewer decides whether a compensating control or documented exception is acceptable.</li>\n<li>\"AI config review replaces a security engineer.\" → It replaces the mechanical checklist step, not the judgment step. The engineer still interprets context, approves exceptions, and owns the outcome.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.5.A</p>"},{"id":"1.5.b","kind":"pre","title":"AI analyzing code for vulnerabilities","topic":"1.5","href":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Writing software that is both functional and secure is hard. Developers focus on making code work;\nsecurity analysis is often an afterthought. For decades, the answer was manual code review, a\nsenior engineer reading every line looking for bugs.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Static analysis is worth understanding by contrast with what it <em>isn't</em>: it never runs your code, it just pattern-matches the text against a list of historically dangerous constructs. That's simultaneously its superpower (catches bugs before a single test case ever executes, scales to millions of lines in milliseconds) and its ceiling (a <code>pickle.loads</code> call is flagged the same way whether it's processing untrusted user uploads or a trusted internal config file, the tool has no idea which). The six patterns here aren't an arbitrary list to memorize; each one is a place where a <em>string</em> can become <em>code</em> or a <em>choice</em> can become a <em>weakness</em> (MD5), and once you see that unifying shape, you'll start recognizing the pattern in code you've never seen flagged before.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Static analysis finds all vulnerabilities.\" → Static analysis finds known patterns; novel logic bugs, authentication flaws, and business-logic errors usually require human review or dynamic testing.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how AI-powered static analysis scans source code for known vulnerability patterns, identify specific risky constructs in Python code, and articulate why each flagged pattern creates a security risk.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.5.b","kind":"post","title":"AI analyzing code for vulnerabilities","topic":"1.5","href":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> It's worth explicitly connecting this skill back to 1.4.c's regex output filters: both are pattern-matching layers sitting between untrusted input and a dangerous action, and both share the exact same limitation, they catch what the defender anticipated. The difference is what's being scanned (source code here, model output there) but the underlying philosophy, and its blind spot, is identical. Recognizing that a defense technique repeats across contexts is a genuinely transferable skill, more valuable long-term than memorizing any single rule list.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Static analysis</strong>: examining source code without executing it to detect bugs and security vulnerabilities by pattern matching or data-flow modeling.</li>\n<li><strong>Code injection</strong>: a class of attacks where an attacker supplies input that is interpreted as code by the application (e.g., via <code>eval</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>OS command injection</strong>: an attack where user input is passed to a shell, allowing the attacker to append arbitrary shell commands (<code>os.system</code>, <code>subprocess shell=True</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Deserialization vulnerability</strong>: a bug where loading serialized data from an untrusted source executes attacker-controlled code (e.g., <code>pickle.loads</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Broken cryptography</strong>: use of algorithms (MD5, SHA-1) that are no longer considered secure for the intended purpose (passwords, signatures).</li>\n<li><strong>Hardcoded credential</strong>: a password or key embedded directly in source code, making it accessible to anyone who can read the file.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Static analysis finds all vulnerabilities.\" → Static analysis finds known patterns; novel logic bugs, authentication flaws, and business-logic errors usually require human review or dynamic testing.</li>\n<li>\"MD5 is only a problem for passwords.\" → MD5 is broken for any security use: file integrity, signatures, or tokens. It is fine for non-security checksums (e.g., cache keys).</li>\n<li>\"subprocess is safe if I use it instead of os.system.\" → subprocess with <code>shell=True</code> has the same injection risk as os.system. The safe usage is passing a list of arguments with <code>shell=False</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.5.A</p>"},{"id":"1.5.c","kind":"pre","title":"AI suggesting detection rules","topic":"1.5","href":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> A security operations center (SOC) can receive millions of log events per day from firewalls,\nservers, identity providers, and endpoint agents. No human team can read every line. The first\nline of defense is a set of <em>detection rules</em>: machine-readable conditions that trigger an alert\nwhen log events match a pattern known to be suspicious.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Detection rules are where the abstract idea of \"pattern recognition\" from earlier atoms becomes a concrete, deployable artifact: a condition, a threshold, and an action, expressed in a format a machine can act on without a human re-reading every log line. The brute-force rule, five failures per minute, is worth remembering not for the specific number but for what the number represents: someone, at some point, tuned that threshold against real data to balance catching real attacks against drowning analysts in false alarms. Every threshold you'll ever configure in security tooling is fighting that same precision-recall tradeoff.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"More rules = better security.\" → More rules means more alerts. If rules are not precise, analysts are buried in noise and real threats get lost. Quality and tuning matter more than quantity.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how AI-assisted tools generate detection rules from log data, implement a rule-generation algorithm that converts repeated suspicious events into structured rule strings, and evaluate what makes a generated rule operationally useful versus noisy.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.5.c","kind":"post","title":"AI suggesting detection rules","topic":"1.5","href":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> \"AI proposes, humans approve\" is a phrase worth holding onto as a template for how AI assistance should be integrated almost everywhere in security operations, not just here. The moment an organization lets AI-<em>generated</em> rules deploy without human review, it has traded one risk (missing real threats) for a different, sneakier one (rules that are technically accurate but legally, operationally, or contextually wrong, blocking a business partner's IP, for instance). Speed and judgment are not substitutes for each other; the best pipelines get both by keeping a human explicitly in the loop at the approval gate, not eliminating the loop.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Detection rule</strong>: a formal condition-and-threshold statement that triggers an alert when a log pattern matches; the fundamental unit of automated threat detection.</li>\n<li><strong>SIEM</strong>: Security Information and Event Management; a platform that aggregates logs from many sources, applies detection rules, and surfaces alerts to analysts.</li>\n<li><strong>Brute-force attack</strong>: an attack that tries many credential combinations in rapid succession to guess a password or key.</li>\n<li><strong>Threshold</strong>: the minimum count of events required before a detection rule fires; tuned to balance precision and recall.</li>\n<li><strong>Alert fatigue</strong>: the desensitization of analysts when too many low-quality alerts are generated, causing real threats to be missed.</li>\n<li><strong>Detection engineering</strong>: the discipline of writing, testing, and maintaining detection rules for a security operations environment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"More rules = better security.\" → More rules means more alerts. If rules are not precise, analysts are buried in noise and real threats get lost. Quality and tuning matter more than quantity.</li>\n<li>\"An AI-generated rule is ready to deploy as-is.\" → AI proposes candidates; a human analyst must review each rule for precision, compliance, and context before it goes live.</li>\n<li>\"One failed login is suspicious.\" → A single failure is normal. The pattern of repeated failures from the same source in a short window is the signal. Context and threshold are everything.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.5.A</p>"},{"id":"1.5.d","kind":"pre","title":"Faster, more accurate threat detection and response","topic":"1.5","href":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.d/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.d/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> A security operations center is always racing against the clock. Every minute an attacker\nspends inside a network is another minute of data exfiltration, lateral movement, or\ncredential harvesting. The time from the moment an incident is detected to the moment it is\nresolved, Mean Time to Respond, or MTTR, is one of the most-watched metrics in cyber\ndefense.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> MTTR is the metric that makes an abstract idea, \"attackers race against defenders\", measurable and manageable. The intuition worth internalizing: every second an incident sits unactioned isn't neutral time, it's time an attacker is actively spending on lateral movement or exfiltration. Decomposing MTTR into detect/triage/contain/eradicate isn't just accounting, it tells you exactly <em>where</em> to invest, because AI helps each sub-metric through a different mechanism (continuous monitoring shrinks detection time; severity scoring shrinks triage time; automated playbooks shrink containment time).</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Lower MTTR always means better security.\" → MTTR must be balanced against accuracy. A system that auto-resolves everything has a very low MTTR but will also close real incidents prematurely. Precision matters.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how AI reduces Mean Time to Respond (MTTR) in a security operations center, implement an incident-triage algorithm that prioritizes incidents by severity and computes response-time metrics, and articulate the difference between automated resolution and human-escalated response.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"1.5.d","kind":"post","title":"Faster, more accurate threat detection and response","topic":"1.5","href":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.d/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-1/1.5/1.5.d/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The tiered-response model, automate the high-confidence, reversible cases; escalate the ambiguous, high-stakes ones, is this unit's cleanest example of a principle that will reappear constantly for the rest of this course: automation is not about replacing judgment, it's about being ruthlessly selective about <em>which</em> decisions actually require it. A SOC that tries to automate everything creates a new failure mode (auto-resolving a real incident prematurely); a SOC that automates nothing drowns its analysts in routine work. The skill this course is actually building in you, across every AI-defense atom in this unit, is the judgment to draw that line correctly.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MTTR (Mean Time to Respond)</strong>: the average time from incident detection to resolution; a primary KPI for SOC effectiveness.</li>\n<li><strong>Triage</strong>: the process of prioritizing incidents by severity so the most dangerous are handled first.</li>\n<li><strong>Severity score</strong>: a numeric rating assigned to an incident based on its potential impact and the criticality of the affected asset.</li>\n<li><strong>Auto-resolution</strong>: automated closure of an incident by an AI playbook without human intervention, reserved for high-confidence cases with safe responses.</li>\n<li><strong>Tiered response</strong>: a model where AI handles routine, high-confidence incidents automatically and escalates ambiguous or high-stakes cases to human analysts.</li>\n<li><strong>False positive</strong>: an alert that fires for a benign event; high false-positive rates waste analyst time and cause alert fatigue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Lower MTTR always means better security.\" → MTTR must be balanced against accuracy. A system that auto-resolves everything has a very low MTTR but will also close real incidents prematurely. Precision matters.</li>\n<li>\"AI replaces SOC analysts.\" → AI handles scale and speed; analysts handle judgment, ethics, and novel threats. The SOC becomes a human-AI team, not a human-free system.</li>\n<li>\"All incidents should be auto-resolved to minimize MTTR.\" → Auto-resolution is only safe for incidents that are both high-confidence true positives and have reversible response actions. Incorrectly auto-resolving a critical incident can make it worse.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 1.5.B</p>"},{"id":"2.1.a","kind":"pre","title":"CIA triad: confidentiality, integrity, availability","topic":"2.1","href":"unit-2/2.1/2.1.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.1/2.1.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every security decision in cybersecurity traces back to three questions: Is this information being kept from those who should not see it? Has this information been changed without authorization? Can the people and systems that need this information actually reach it?</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Here's the mental model worth carrying through the rest of the course: whenever you read about a security incident, don't ask \"how bad was it,\" ask \"which pillar did it break.\" Confidentiality, integrity, and availability aren't three items on a checklist, they're three completely different failure modes that call for three completely different fixes. A confidentiality fix (encrypt it) does nothing for an integrity problem (verify it hasn't changed), and neither does anything for an availability problem (make sure it's still reachable). Once you sort an incident into the right bucket, the right defense almost picks itself.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Security is mainly about keeping things secret.\" → Confidentiality is only one pillar; many attacks target integrity or availability and cause equal or greater harm without stealing any data.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Name and define each pillar of the CIA triad, give a concrete example and a countermeasure for each, and explain how the three pillars can come into conflict.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.1.a","kind":"post","title":"CIA triad: confidentiality, integrity, availability","topic":"2.1","href":"unit-2/2.1/2.1.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.1/2.1.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The tension buried at the end of this skill is the one worth remembering longest: the three pillars don't just coexist, they actively fight each other. Lock a system down hard enough to guarantee confidentiality and you've probably made it slower or harder to reach, an availability cost. That's not a flaw in the framework; that's the framework doing its job, forcing you to make the tradeoff on purpose instead of by accident. Every unit from here forward, physical controls, network defenses, incident response, is really just this same triage question asked in a new context: which pillar am I protecting, and what am I willing to spend on the other two to do it?</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CIA triad</strong>: the three foundational security properties: confidentiality, integrity, and availability.</li>\n<li><strong>Confidentiality</strong>: the property that information is accessible only to authorized parties.</li>\n<li><strong>Integrity</strong>: the property that information and systems have not been altered without authorization.</li>\n<li><strong>Availability</strong>: the property that systems and data are accessible to authorized users when needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Encryption</strong>: a process that transforms data into an unreadable form, protecting confidentiality.</li>\n<li><strong>Cryptographic hash</strong>: a fixed-length fingerprint of data used to detect unauthorized modification.</li>\n<li><strong>DDoS (Distributed Denial-of-Service)</strong>: an attack that floods a system with traffic to make it unavailable.</li>\n<li><strong>Redundancy</strong>: duplicating critical systems or data so that a failure in one does not cause an outage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Security is mainly about keeping things secret.\" → Confidentiality is only one pillar; many attacks target integrity or availability and cause equal or greater harm without stealing any data.</li>\n<li>\"Encrypting everything makes a system maximally secure.\" → Strong encryption protects confidentiality but can reduce availability (key loss, decryption latency) and does nothing to guarantee integrity on its own.</li>\n<li>\"Availability is an IT problem, not a security problem.\" → Ransomware, DDoS, and sabotage are deliberate security attacks that target availability; it is explicitly part of the security mandate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.1.A</p>"},{"id":"2.1.b","kind":"pre","title":"Threat actors and their motivations","topic":"2.1","href":"unit-2/2.1/2.1.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.1/2.1.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> When Meridian University's security team arrived on Monday morning, they found three incident tickets waiting. Someone had encrypted the registrar's database and left a ransom note. An anonymous post on a public forum included thousands of student records.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The single habit this skill is trying to build: before you design a defense, name the attacker. \"Someone might attack us\" is not a threat model, \"a script kiddie scanning for unpatched software\" and \"a nation-state after our research data\" are, and they lead to opposite spending decisions. A patch cycle stops the first and barely slows the second. Get in the habit of asking who and why before you ask how, because the six categories in this skill aren't trivia to memorize, they're a sorting mechanism that tells you where your budget actually belongs.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"All hackers are criminals out for money\", Hacktivists and nation-state actors have entirely different goals; conflating them leads to misaligned defenses.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify the six major threat actor categories, explain what motivates each one, and use motivation to recommend the most appropriate defensive measure for a given attack scenario.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.1.b","kind":"post","title":"Threat actors and their motivations","topic":"2.1","href":"unit-2/2.1/2.1.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.1/2.1.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Meridian University's Monday morning is a small case study in a big idea: the same organization can be targeted by wildly different actors in the same 24 hours, and treating them identically wastes the response. A ransomware note calls for law enforcement and offline backups. A public data leak calls for an access-log audit aimed at insiders. A defaced website calls for credential hygiene and a PR plan. Notice, too, that APT isn't a seventh category, it's a description of <em>style</em> (patient, stealthy, custom-tooled) that mostly, but not always, points back to a nation-state actor. Keep that distinction sharp; it's one of the AP exam's favorite places to test whether you actually understand the categories or just memorized their names.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Threat actor</strong>: any individual, group, or organization that intentionally or accidentally causes harm to information systems or data.</li>\n<li><strong>Nation-state actor</strong>: a threat actor operating on behalf of or with the support of a national government.</li>\n<li><strong>APT (Advanced Persistent Threat)</strong>: a long-duration, stealthy, high-capability intrusion campaign, typically state-sponsored.</li>\n<li><strong>Cybercriminal</strong>: a threat actor motivated by financial gain, using attacks such as ransomware, fraud, or credential theft.</li>\n<li><strong>Hacktivist</strong>: an attacker motivated by political or ideological goals who uses hacking as a form of protest or public pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Insider threat</strong>: a threat originating from someone who already has authorized access to an organization's systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Script kiddie</strong>: a low-skill attacker who uses pre-built tools without deep technical understanding.</li>\n<li><strong>Motivation</strong>: the underlying reason a threat actor chooses to attack; drives target selection, tactics, and persistence level.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"All hackers are criminals out for money\", Hacktivists and nation-state actors have entirely different goals; conflating them leads to misaligned defenses.</li>\n<li>\"APT always means nation-state\", APT describes a campaign style (advanced and persistent) that is usually but not always government-backed; well-funded criminal groups can run APT-style campaigns too.</li>\n<li>\"Insider threats are rare\", Accidental insider incidents (negligent users) are among the most common causes of data exposure; they receive less press than ransomware but are just as damaging.</li>\n<li>\"Patching stops sophisticated attackers\", Patching is essential against script kiddies, but nation-state actors regularly exploit zero-days and misconfigurations that have no patch yet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.1.B</p>"},{"id":"2.1.c","kind":"pre","title":"Risk language: likelihood, impact, and security goals","topic":"2.1","href":"unit-2/2.1/2.1.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.1/2.1.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every organization faces threats. A threat is a potential negative event (like a hard drive failing, a database leak, or a hurricane). A vulnerability is a weakness in the system that makes the threat possible (like using an outdated operating system or storing servers in a basement).</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Hold onto this one plain fact: risk is not the same thing as threat, and the difference is the entire point of this skill. A threat is the bad thing that could happen. Risk is how much you should actually worry about it, likelihood times impact, a number you can compare against other numbers. That multiplication is what turns security from a vague sense of dread into something you can put a budget against, because a security team that tries to defend against every threat equally is a security team that has already lost the argument for resources.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"We must eliminate all risk.\" → Impossible. Security has a cost, and organizations must accept some level of risk (known as risk appetite) to function.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Define likelihood, impact, and asset value in a cybersecurity context, calculate a risk score, and select and justify the most appropriate risk treatment (Mitigate, Accept, Transfer, Avoid) for a given scenario.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.1.c","kind":"post","title":"Risk language: likelihood, impact, and security goals","topic":"2.1","href":"unit-2/2.1/2.1.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.1/2.1.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The four-treatment vocabulary, mitigate, accept, transfer, avoid, is worth memorizing cold, because it's the AP exam's cleanest test of whether you can reason about a scenario rather than just recite a definition. The trap most students fall into is assuming \"mitigate\" is always the right answer, since it sounds the most responsible. It isn't. Spending $20,000 to protect a $10,000 asset is a worse decision than accepting the risk outright, and buying insurance (transfer) is often smarter than trying to engineer your way to zero risk. The organizations that manage risk well aren't the ones that eliminate it, they're the ones that spend their limited defense budget on the risks where a dollar of mitigation actually buys a dollar (or more) of reduced expected loss.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Risk</strong>: the probability that a threat will exploit a vulnerability and cause harm.</li>\n<li><strong>Likelihood</strong>: the probability of a threat occurring (scored 1-5).</li>\n<li><strong>Impact</strong>: the severity of the consequences if a threat occurs (scored 1-5).</li>\n<li><strong>Mitigate</strong>: reducing risk by implementing security controls.</li>\n<li><strong>Accept</strong>: retaining risk without active mitigation when it is low-impact or too expensive to fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Transfer</strong>: sharing risk with third parties (like insurance or cloud hosting).</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid</strong>: eliminating risk by stopping the risky activity altogether.</li>\n<li><strong>Control function</strong>: when a control acts: preventive, detective, corrective, deterrent, or compensating.</li>\n<li><strong>Control category</strong>: how a control is implemented: technical, administrative, or physical.</li>\n<li><strong>Defense in depth</strong>: layering independent controls so that the failure of one does not expose the asset.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"We must eliminate all risk.\" → Impossible. Security has a cost, and organizations must accept some level of risk (known as risk appetite) to function.</li>\n<li>\"Cyber insurance replaces technical security.\" → Insurance covers financial damage but does not prevent reputational loss, operational downtime, or legal liability.</li>\n<li>\"Risk is the same as threat.\" → A threat is the actor or event (e.g., malware); risk includes how likely it is to happen to <em>us</em> and how bad it will be.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.1.C, 2.1.D, 2.1.E, 2.1.F, 2.1.G</p>"},{"id":"2.2.a","kind":"pre","title":"Unauthorized physical access (tailgating, piggybacking, lock bypass)","topic":"2.2","href":"unit-2/2.2/2.2.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.2/2.2.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every cybersecurity textbook covers firewalls and encryption, but the most damaging breaches often begin with an attacker simply walking through the front door. Physical access controls, locked doors, badge readers, security checkpoints, are the first layer of defense for servers, wiring closets, and workstations.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Notice the throughline from Unit 1: tailgating works for the exact same psychological reason phishing works, it exploits the awkwardness of demanding verification from someone who seems legitimate. A locked door is a technical control, but the moment a human decides whether to hold it open, you're back in social-engineering territory, just wearing a badge instead of an email. That's why this skill belongs right after Unit 1 rather than off in its own silo: physical security and social engineering aren't two separate topics, they're the same vulnerability, human courtesy under mild social pressure, showing up in a different setting.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Tailgating only works in movies.\" → It is one of the most frequently reported physical-penetration vectors in professional red-team assessments; social courtesy is a real and exploitable vulnerability.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify tailgating, piggybacking, and lock-bypass techniques, explain how each circumvents physical access controls, and flag suspicious entry-log records that indicate an unauthorized access attempt.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.2.a","kind":"post","title":"Unauthorized physical access (tailgating, piggybacking, lock bypass)","topic":"2.2","href":"unit-2/2.2/2.2.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.2/2.2.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The access-log heuristics here are worth sitting with because they're your first real taste of <em>anomaly detection logic</em>, a skill this course keeps returning to in more technical forms later on. A single badge swipe at two doors 13 seconds apart isn't proof of anything by itself, it's a physically implausible pattern that a human reviewing logs by eye would probably miss, but a simple rule (time gap too short for travel distance) catches automatically. Keep that idea in your pocket: piggybacking and tailgating look identical in the log, and the only thing that changes is intent, which is precisely why you can't rely on trust or recognition (\"I know that guy\") as a substitute for a system that verifies every credential every time.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tailgating</strong>: following an authorized person through a secured entry without using one's own credentials; the authorized person is unaware or does not consent.</li>\n<li><strong>Piggybacking</strong>: an authorized employee knowingly allows an unverified individual through a secured entry; the risk is identical to tailgating despite the consent.</li>\n<li><strong>Lock picking</strong>: manipulating a lock's internal mechanism with specialized tools to open it without the correct key.</li>\n<li><strong>Bump key</strong>: a specially cut key used with a striking blow to momentarily bypass pin-tumbler lock pins.</li>\n<li><strong>RFID badge cloning</strong>: capturing the radio signal of an access badge and writing the data to a blank credential to gain unauthorized entry.</li>\n<li><strong>Mantrap</strong>: a physical security control consisting of two interlocked doors; only one can be open at a time, preventing a second person from following through.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Tailgating only works in movies.\" → It is one of the most frequently reported physical-penetration vectors in professional red-team assessments; social courtesy is a real and exploitable vulnerability.</li>\n<li>\"A locked door means the room is secure.\" → Locks are a delay mechanism, not an absolute barrier; lock-bypass techniques, credential cloning, and tailgating all circumvent them without leaving obvious evidence.</li>\n<li>\"Piggybacking is fine for people I recognize.\" → Recognition is not authorization; a familiar face may be a former employee, a social-engineering attacker who built rapport, or a contractor whose access has been revoked.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.2.A, 2.2.B</p>"},{"id":"2.2.b","kind":"pre","title":"Environmental vulnerabilities (power, HVAC, fire, water)","topic":"2.2","href":"unit-2/2.2/2.2.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.2/2.2.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Locks and badge readers defend against a person who wants to walk into your server room. But a\nserver room can be destroyed just as thoroughly by things that are never \"unauthorized\" in the\naccess-control sense at all: a power outage, an overheated rack, a fire, or a burst pipe.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The intuition worth keeping: nature doesn't care about your threat model. A ransomware gang wants something from you; a burst pipe doesn't want anything, it just happens, and the server room goes offline exactly the same way either way. That's the whole reason this skill exists in a cybersecurity course at all: if your risk analysis only counts threats with intent behind them, you've quietly excluded some of the most common causes of real outages, and \"but nobody attacked us\" is cold comfort to a hospital that just lost patient records to a flooded basement.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Environmental risk isn't really a cybersecurity topic.\" → A power, cooling, fire, or water failure produces the same availability (and sometimes integrity) loss as a targeted cyberattack; AP Cybersecurity's Analyze Risk skill treats all threats to CIA equally regardless of whether an adversary is involved.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify how power loss, HVAC (heating, ventilation, and air conditioning) failure, fire, and water/flood events threaten the availability and integrity of physical IT infrastructure, and you can evaluate whether a facility's environmental sensor readings indicate a developing incident.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.2.b","kind":"post","title":"Environmental vulnerabilities (power, HVAC, fire, water)","topic":"2.2","href":"unit-2/2.2/2.2.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.2/2.2.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The detail that should stick with you is the fire-suppression paradox: the default tool for putting out a fire (water) is also one of the most effective tools for permanently destroying the exact hardware you're trying to save. That's a perfect example of a control designed for one context becoming a threat in another, which is a pattern you'll see again and again in security. The fix (clean-agent suppression) isn't a fancier version of a sprinkler, it's a completely different mechanism chosen because the ordinary solution actively backfires here. When you evaluate any control in this course, always ask: does this control assume a context that doesn't apply here?</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Uninterruptible power supply (UPS)</strong>: a battery-backed unit that provides short-term power during an outage or dip, bridging the gap until generators start or power is restored.</li>\n<li><strong>N+1 redundancy</strong>: a design principle where at least one extra unit of a critical system (power, cooling) exists beyond the minimum required, so a single failure does not cause an outage.</li>\n<li><strong>HVAC (heating, ventilation, and air conditioning)</strong>: the system that regulates temperature and humidity to keep hardware within safe operating conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>Clean-agent fire suppression</strong>: a fire-extinguishing system (e.g., FM-200, inert gas) that removes oxygen or interrupts combustion without damaging electronics, unlike water sprinklers.</li>\n<li><strong>Raised flooring</strong>: elevated flooring in a data center that both routes cabling/cooling air and protects equipment from shallow flooding at slab level.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Environmental risk isn't really a cybersecurity topic.\" → A power, cooling, fire, or water failure produces the same availability (and sometimes integrity) loss as a targeted cyberattack; AP Cybersecurity's Analyze Risk skill treats all threats to CIA equally regardless of whether an adversary is involved.</li>\n<li>\"Sprinklers are fine for a server room like anywhere else in the building.\" → Water-based sprinklers actively destroy electronics; data centers use clean-agent suppression specifically because a false alarm or a real fire should not itself become a total-loss event.</li>\n<li>\"As long as there's a backup generator, power is a solved problem.\" → A generator that has not been load-tested and does not have fuel contracts in place is a false sense of security; the UPS-to-generator handoff window and generator runtime under real load must be validated regularly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.2.A</p>"},{"id":"2.2.c","kind":"pre","title":"Device theft and tampering","topic":"2.2","href":"unit-2/2.2/2.2.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.2/2.2.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Unauthorized physical access (2.2.a) gets an attacker into a building. Once inside, or once a\ndevice leaves the building on an employee's backpack, the attacker's goal shifts to the device\nitself. <strong>Device theft</strong> is the straightforward case: a laptop, phone, external drive, or backup\ntape is physically taken.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The distinction to hold onto: a stolen laptop is a loss you already know about; a tampered laptop is a loss you don't know has happened yet. Theft interrupts your day. Tampering doesn't interrupt anything, it just quietly turns a device you trust into one you shouldn't, while it keeps behaving normally in every way you'd notice. That asymmetry is why tamper-evident seals and inventory audits matter even when nothing looks wrong: the whole point of a good tampering attack is that nothing <em>looks</em> wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"If a laptop is password-protected, a thief can't get the data.\" → A login password protects the operating system's normal boot path, but an attacker with physical possession can often boot from external media and read the disk directly unless the disk itself is encrypted.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Distinguish device theft from device tampering, identify the attack techniques each enables (offline password reset, disk imaging, hardware implants, evil-maid attacks), and evaluate whether a given device's configuration (encryption, boot protections, asset tracking) would contain the damage if the device were stolen or tampered with.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.2.c","kind":"post","title":"Device theft and tampering","topic":"2.2","href":"unit-2/2.2/2.2.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.2/2.2.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The evil-maid attack is worth remembering not because you'll personally be targeted by one, but because it's the cleanest illustration in this unit of a principle that recurs constantly in security: a control can be cryptographically perfect and still fail because of <em>when</em> it's applied. Full-disk encryption is genuinely strong, but only from the moment it's engaged. An attacker who gets brief unsupervised access before you've entered your password that session isn't breaking the encryption at all; they're intercepting the one narrow window where the protection hasn't started yet. That's a lesson worth generalizing: don't just ask \"is this control strong,\" ask \"is there a gap in time or scope where it isn't running.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Device theft</strong>: the physical removal of a device (laptop, phone, drive, tape) from its authorized location or owner.</li>\n<li><strong>Device tampering</strong>: a covert, typically brief physical alteration of a device (hardware, firmware, or peripheral) intended to compromise it without the owner's knowledge.</li>\n<li><strong>Evil-maid attack</strong>: a tampering attack in which an attacker with brief unsupervised access to a device (e.g., in a hotel room) modifies it, often to capture credentials on next use, then restores its appearance and leaves.</li>\n<li><strong>Full-disk encryption</strong>: encryption of an entire storage volume so its contents are unreadable without the decryption key, even if the physical drive is removed.</li>\n<li><strong>Tamper-evident seal</strong>: a sticker or mechanism that visibly and irreversibly shows evidence (tearing, a \"VOID\" pattern, discoloration) if a device enclosure has been opened.</li>\n<li><strong>Remote wipe</strong>: a mobile-device-management capability that erases a device's data remotely, typically once it is reported lost or stolen and reconnects to a network.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"If a laptop is password-protected, a thief can't get the data.\" → A login password protects the operating system's normal boot path, but an attacker with physical possession can often boot from external media and read the disk directly unless the disk itself is encrypted.</li>\n<li>\"Tampering only matters for spy-movie scenarios.\" → Evil-maid attacks and rogue USB implants are documented techniques used against real targets (executives, journalists, researchers) whenever a device is left briefly unattended in a hotel, shared office, or conference venue.</li>\n<li>\"A locked laptop bag is enough physical security.\" → A cable lock or bag deters casual, opportunistic theft but does nothing against a prepared attacker with cutting tools, and does nothing at all against tampering, which does not require removing the device from the room.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.2.B, 2.2.C</p>"},{"id":"2.3.a","kind":"pre","title":"Physical access controls (locks, badges, biometrics, mantraps)","topic":"2.3","href":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every firewall rule, encryption key, and intrusion-detection alert becomes irrelevant if an attacker can walk through the front door, plug a USB drive into a server, or tailgate an employee into a restricted corridor. Physical security is not a legacy concern; it is the outermost layer of a layered defense model.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The four controls in this skill, locks, badges, biometrics, mantraps, aren't a menu where you pick the \"best\" one. They're a stack, and each layer exists specifically to cover a gap the layer before it can't. A lock works with no power and no network, but leaves no record of who used it. A badge leaves a record and can be revoked instantly, but only proves someone had the credential, not that they're the right person. A biometric proves identity, but can't be swapped out if it's compromised the way a badge can. Ask \"what does this layer fail to catch\" for every control you meet in this course, because that question is what tells you what the <em>next</em> layer needs to do.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A badge reader is enough; locks are old-fashioned.\", Locks provide power-independent fallback. If the badge system loses power or is misconfigured, a lock prevents the door from becoming unsecured.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify the purpose of each physical access control layer, locks, badge readers, biometrics, and mantraps, explain how they combine into a defense-in-depth strategy, and pinpoint gaps when one or more controls are absent.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.3.a","kind":"post","title":"Physical access controls (locks, badges, biometrics, mantraps)","topic":"2.3","href":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The seven-layer facility list at the end of this skill is really a compressed version of defense-in-depth, and it's worth noticing that it ends with a person, not a piece of technology. Every electronic and mechanical control on that list handles a predictable case well and an unpredictable case badly, and a guard is the one layer capable of noticing something is wrong even when every rule-based system says everything is fine. False acceptance and false rejection rates are the same idea in miniature: no single control, however well-tuned, gets it right 100% of the time, which is precisely the argument for stacking several imperfect controls rather than searching for one perfect one.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Keyed lock</strong>: a mechanical barrier that requires a physical key to disengage; provides no audit trail.</li>\n<li><strong>RFID badge</strong>: a credential token that communicates with a reader via radio frequency; supports audit logging and remote revocation.</li>\n<li><strong>Biometric</strong>: an identity-verification method based on a measurable physical characteristic unique to a person (fingerprint, iris, etc.).</li>\n<li><strong>Mantrap</strong>: a two-door vestibule with interlocked operation that prevents tailgating by allowing only one entry cycle at a time.</li>\n<li><strong>Tailgating</strong>: following an authorized person through a secured door without authenticating, without that person's consent; when the authorized person knowingly lets them in it is called piggybacking.</li>\n<li><strong>False acceptance rate (FAR)</strong>: the probability a biometric system incorrectly grants access to an unauthorized subject.</li>\n<li><strong>False rejection rate (FRR)</strong>: the probability a biometric system incorrectly denies access to an authorized subject.</li>\n<li><strong>Defense in depth</strong>: a security strategy that layers multiple independent controls so that the failure of one does not expose the asset.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A badge reader is enough; locks are old-fashioned.\", Locks provide power-independent fallback. If the badge system loses power or is misconfigured, a lock prevents the door from becoming unsecured.</li>\n<li>\"Biometrics are infallible.\", Every biometric system has FAR and FRR greater than zero. Fingerprints can be lifted and spoofed; some facial recognition systems are defeated by photographs. Biometrics should be one factor in a multi-factor scheme, not the only one.</li>\n<li>\"A mantrap is only for spy movies.\", Any data center or evidence room handles this threat seriously. Tailgating is one of the simplest physical attacks and requires no technical skill.</li>\n<li>\"CCTV prevents intrusion.\", CCTV is a detective control, not a preventive one. It records what happened; it does not stop an attacker from entering.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.3.A</p>"},{"id":"2.3.b","kind":"pre","title":"Surveillance systems (CCTV, monitoring)","topic":"2.3","href":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> If locks, badges, and biometrics control <em>who</em> enters a space, surveillance systems tell us <em>what</em> happens in and around that space. Surveillance is a dual-use control. It functions as a <strong>detective control</strong> by recording events and alerting operators to incidents in progress.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The framing to hold onto: a camera is a detective control wearing a preventive control's reputation. People install CCTV and feel safer, but a camera has never once physically stopped anyone from doing anything, it only makes it more likely they'll be identified afterward, and the deterrent effect only works if the attacker believes that matters to them. Keep that distinction sharp, because the AP exam loves testing whether students can tell a preventive control from a detective one, and \"we have cameras\" is one of the most common wrong answers to \"how did you prevent this.\"</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"CCTV prevents physical security breaches.\", CCTV is fundamentally a <strong>detective</strong> control, not a preventive one. A camera cannot stop an intruder from climbing a fence; it only records the action or alerts a guard. To prevent breaches, cameras must be paired with preventive controls (like fences and locks) and active response protocols.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Describe the components and layout of physical surveillance systems, explain the security differences between active and passive monitoring, and analyze vulnerabilities in surveillance networks to recommend mitigations.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.3.b","kind":"post","title":"Surveillance systems (CCTV, monitoring)","topic":"2.3","href":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The analog-versus-IP tradeoff in this skill is a small, concrete example of a huge pattern in modern security: every time you add intelligence and connectivity to a device, you also add an attack surface that didn't exist before. An analog coaxial camera can be physically cut but can't be hacked over the internet; an IP camera can be watched from anywhere in the world by its owner, and, if the default password was never changed, by anyone else too. That tradeoff (capability versus attack surface) isn't unique to cameras. You'll meet it again with every \"smart\" device in this course, and the right response is never \"don't use the smart version,\" it's \"secure the smart version as seriously as you'd secure a server, because that's what it now is.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CCTV (Closed-Circuit Television)</strong>: A video system where signals are transmitted privately to a specific set of monitors and recording units.</li>\n<li><strong>IP Camera</strong>: A digital video camera that sends and receives data over an IP network.</li>\n<li><strong>Active Monitoring</strong>: The real-time viewing of live video feeds by security personnel to detect and respond to incidents as they occur.</li>\n<li><strong>Passive Monitoring</strong>: Recording video feeds for storage and forensic retrieval after an incident.</li>\n<li><strong>Blind Spot</strong>: An unmonitored area within a facility's layout where an intruder can avoid camera coverage.</li>\n<li><strong>Thermal Imaging</strong>: A camera technology that detects infrared radiation (heat) rather than visible light, allowing detection in complete darkness or harsh weather.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"CCTV prevents physical security breaches.\", CCTV is fundamentally a <strong>detective</strong> control, not a preventive one. A camera cannot stop an intruder from climbing a fence; it only records the action or alerts a guard. To prevent breaches, cameras must be paired with preventive controls (like fences and locks) and active response protocols.</li>\n<li>\"Networked IP cameras are inherently more secure than old analog systems.\", While IP cameras provide better image quality and analytics, they also introduce a cyber-attack surface. An analog coaxial system cannot be hacked over the internet; an IP camera connected to an insecure network can.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.3.A</p>"},{"id":"2.3.c","kind":"pre","title":"Security guards and layered defense-in-depth","topic":"2.3","href":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> No single security control protects a facility completely. A lock can be picked. A camera can be obscured.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The idea to internalize here is that layers don't add security, they <em>multiply</em> the attacker's cost, and that distinction matters more than it sounds like it should. If a facility has five independent control categories, an attacker doesn't need to be five times as capable; they need to succeed at all five simultaneously, and failing any single one ends the attempt. That's a fundamentally different kind of hard than \"climb a slightly taller fence.\" It's also why the depth score in this skill counts <em>categories</em>, not raw control count, three guards covering the same job as one guard adds cost without closing a single new gap.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"More guards means more security\", Redundant personnel in the same role add cost without closing new gaps; the benefit comes from covering additional categories, not duplicating existing ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how security guards function as an active personnel control and how combining multiple control categories creates a defense-in-depth posture that raises the cost of physical attacks on a facility.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.3.c","kind":"post","title":"Security guards and layered defense-in-depth","topic":"2.3","href":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Notice what a guard can do that nothing else in this unit can: reason. A badge reader logs a swipe; a guard notices that the person swiping doesn't match their badge photo, or that their explanation for being in the building doesn't add up. That's the throughline connecting this skill back to social engineering in Unit 1, technology is consistent and tireless but has no judgment, and humans have judgment but get tired and make exceptions. A mature security posture doesn't pick one over the other; it deliberately pairs them so each one covers the other's blind spot. That pairing principle is worth remembering every time this course asks you to evaluate whether a security plan is \"strong, adequate, or weak.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Defense-in-depth</strong>: a security architecture that uses multiple independent control layers so that no single failure is sufficient for an attacker to reach an objective</li>\n<li><strong>Personnel control</strong>: a security measure involving human actors, including guards, receptionists, escorts, and visitor-log administrators</li>\n<li><strong>Tailgating</strong>: the practice of following an authorized person through a controlled entry without presenting one's own credential (called piggybacking when the authorized person knowingly allows it)</li>\n<li><strong>Mantrap</strong>: a physical access control consisting of two interlocking doors that prevent more than one person from entering at a time</li>\n<li><strong>Visitor log</strong>: a record of all non-employee entrants, including sponsor, arrival time, and departure time, used for access accountability</li>\n<li><strong>Escort policy</strong>: a facility rule requiring that every visitor be accompanied by an authorized employee at all times</li>\n<li><strong>Depth score</strong>: a metric counting the number of distinct control categories active in a facility; higher scores indicate stronger layered posture</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"More guards means more security\", Redundant personnel in the same role add cost without closing new gaps; the benefit comes from covering additional categories, not duplicating existing ones.</li>\n<li>\"Technology replaces guards\", Automated controls and human controls cover different failure modes; removing guards to rely solely on electronics eliminates judgment, response, and social-engineering detection.</li>\n<li>\"A visitor log stops attacks\", A log is a deterrent and an audit tool, not a barrier; it is effective as part of a layered program that also includes escort policies and physical access controls.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.3.B</p>"},{"id":"2.3.d","kind":"pre","title":"Environmental controls (HVAC, fire suppression, power)","topic":"2.3","href":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.d/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.d/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Physical hardware is the foundation of every digital system. A server does not care whether its\ndata was stolen by a hacker or destroyed because a room hit 50 degrees Celsius, the outcome is\nthe same: the data is gone and the service is unavailable.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The line worth remembering from this skill: a server doesn't know or care whether it went offline because of a hacker or a broken thermostat, the outcome is identical, so your risk analysis should weigh both equally. That's the entire justification for treating HVAC, fire suppression, and power as cybersecurity topics rather than \"facilities problems.\" If your job is protecting availability, the source of the threat is irrelevant; only the probability and the consequence matter, and a badly maintained cooling system can take down more systems in an afternoon than most attackers manage in a year.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> Sprinkler systems are fine for data centers -&gt; Water damages electronics on contact and even a false activation can destroy an entire room; clean-agent suppression is the appropriate choice for most data center spaces.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how HVAC, fire suppression, and power systems protect physical infrastructure and describe the alert thresholds that define safe operating ranges.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.3.d","kind":"post","title":"Environmental controls (HVAC, fire suppression, power)","topic":"2.3","href":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.d/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.3/2.3.d/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The detail worth carrying forward is that every environmental control in this skill is really a <em>timing</em> problem in disguise. A UPS doesn't need to run for hours, it needs to bridge milliseconds until a generator, which itself needs seconds to stabilize, takes over. A VESDA system's entire value is in giving minutes of warning instead of seconds. Clean-agent suppression only works if the room stays sealed for the length of the discharge. None of these controls are about brute strength; they're about buying exactly enough time for the next layer to activate before the last one runs out, which is defense-in-depth expressed as a countdown clock instead of a wall.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HVAC</strong>: Heating, Ventilation, and Air Conditioning; the system that maintains temperature and humidity within safe operating ranges for hardware.</li>\n<li><strong>Hot aisle / cold aisle</strong>: A data center rack layout that separates cool intake air from hot exhaust air to maximize cooling efficiency and prevent hot spots.</li>\n<li><strong>Clean agent</strong>: A gaseous fire suppressant (such as FM-200 or NOVEC 1230) that extinguishes fire without leaving residue or damaging electronic equipment.</li>\n<li><strong>UPS (Uninterruptible Power Supply)</strong>: A battery-backed device that provides immediate clean power during outages or power quality events, bridging the gap until a generator starts.</li>\n<li><strong>PDU (Power Distribution Unit)</strong>: A device that distributes electrical power to multiple rack components; managed PDUs support remote monitoring and outlet control.</li>\n<li><strong>VESDA</strong>: Very Early Smoke Detection Apparatus; a system that continuously samples air and detects smoke at extremely low concentrations, providing early warning before a fire develops.</li>\n<li><strong>Brownout</strong>: A condition where utility voltage drops below nominal, causing hardware instability without a full outage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sprinkler systems are fine for data centers -&gt; Water damages electronics on contact and even a false activation can destroy an entire room; clean-agent suppression is the appropriate choice for most data center spaces.</li>\n<li>UPS units are only needed for long outages -&gt; A UPS is most critical for the first few milliseconds to seconds of an outage, before a generator can start; it also filters everyday power quality problems that would otherwise cause silent corruption.</li>\n<li>Cold is always safer than warm for servers -&gt; Temperatures below 15 C increase condensation risk and ESD from dry air, and can mechanically stress some components; the safe window has a lower bound as well as an upper bound.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.3.C</p>"},{"id":"2.4.a","kind":"pre","title":"Access logs and monitoring","topic":"2.4","href":"unit-2/2.4/2.4.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.4/2.4.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Physical security controls, badge readers, key-card doors, biometric scanners, generate a continuous stream of timestamped records every time someone attempts or succeeds at entering a controlled area. That stream is called a <strong>physical access log</strong>, and it is one of the most powerful detective tools a security team has.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The reframe worth making here: an access log isn't a record of what happened, it's a record of what a badge did, and the gap between those two things is where every interesting anomaly lives. A valid badge swipe only proves a credential was presented; it says nothing about who actually presented it. Once you internalize that distinction, the detection rules in this skill stop looking like arbitrary thresholds and start looking obvious: you're not looking for invalid credentials, you're looking for valid credentials behaving in ways a single human physically couldn't.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A valid badge swipe means the entry was authorized.\" → Badges can be cloned, stolen, or shared; a valid swipe only proves a credential was presented, not that the right person presented it. Context, time, location, frequency, is what determines legitimacy.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Interpret physical access log entries to identify anomalous patterns, after-hours entries, repeated failures, and tailgating gaps, and explain what each pattern signals to a security analyst.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.4.a","kind":"post","title":"Access logs and monitoring","topic":"2.4","href":"unit-2/2.4/2.4.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.4/2.4.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Every rule in this skill is really the same move applied three times: define what normal looks like, then flag whatever falls outside it. After-hours access is abnormal <em>timing</em>. A denial burst is abnormal <em>frequency</em>. A location inconsistency is abnormal <em>geography</em>, the same badge in two places faster than a person could travel between them. That's the core logic of every detection system you'll meet for the rest of this course, physical or digital: you can't spot an anomaly without first being precise about what normal actually is, which is why \"establish a baseline\" always comes before \"write the alert rule,\" never after.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Physical access log</strong>: a timestamped record of credential presentations at controlled entry points, including outcome (granted or denied).</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-passback</strong>: a rule that prevents a credential from granting entry if it has not yet been used to exit, detecting shared or re-entered badges.</li>\n<li><strong>After-hours access</strong>: any entry to a controlled area outside its defined authorized schedule, which warrants automatic alerting regardless of credential validity.</li>\n<li><strong>Repeated denial burst</strong>: a cluster of ACCESS DENIED events for a single credential within a short time window, analogous to a brute-force attack.</li>\n<li><strong>Location inconsistency</strong>: a single credential appearing at two geographically separated readers within an interval too short for legitimate travel.</li>\n<li><strong>SIEM</strong>: Security Information and Event Management; a platform that aggregates and correlates security event data from multiple sources for real-time analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A valid badge swipe means the entry was authorized.\" → Badges can be cloned, stolen, or shared; a valid swipe only proves a credential was presented, not that the right person presented it. Context, time, location, frequency, is what determines legitimacy.</li>\n<li>\"Logs are only useful after an incident.\" → Real-time rule evaluation against a live log stream is an active detective control; modern SIEM rules alert within seconds of a threshold crossing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.4.A</p>"},{"id":"2.4.b","kind":"pre","title":"Alarm systems","topic":"2.4","href":"unit-2/2.4/2.4.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.4/2.4.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> A lock keeps someone out. A camera records that they got in anyway. An <strong>alarm system</strong> sits in\nbetween: it is a <strong>detective control</strong> whose job is to notice an intrusion attempt in progress and\ntrigger a response fast enough to matter.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The single most important sentence in this skill is the one about what an alarm is <em>not</em>: nothing about a siren physically stops anyone from walking through a broken window. An alarm is a detective control that happens to be loud, not a barrier, and confusing the two is the single most common mistake in physical security planning, because \"we have an alarm\" feels like it should mean \"this door can't be opened\" when it actually just means \"someone will eventually find out it was.\"</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"An armed alarm prevents a break-in.\" → An alarm is a detective and response control, not a preventive one; it can only notice and react to an intrusion that has already started, not physically stop it. Preventive controls are locks, barriers, and access control.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify the components of an intrusion detection alarm system, explain how arm states and entry/exit delay affect whether a sensor trip is a real event, and classify alarm events (true alarm, false alarm, suppressed) to decide which should escalate to central-station dispatch.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.4.b","kind":"post","title":"Alarm systems","topic":"2.4","href":"unit-2/2.4/2.4.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.4/2.4.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The exit/entry delay problem is a small, elegant example of a hard engineering tradeoff you'll see again throughout this course: how do you tell a real event apart from an expected, harmless one that looks identical on a single sensor? The system can't out-think the ambiguity, it resolves it with time, giving the legitimate occupant a window to prove they're legitimate by disarming before the trip counts. Cross-zone verification solves the same ambiguity a different way: don't trust one sensor, require two independent ones to agree. Both are the same underlying lesson, a single signal is cheap to fake or misread, but two independent signals agreeing is much harder to produce by accident, which is exactly why the panic button is allowed to skip all of this: a human deliberately pressing a button is already about as high-confidence as a signal gets.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Door/window contact sensor</strong>: a two-part reed switch (frame + door/window) that reports an open circuit when the door or window is opened while the zone is armed.</li>\n<li><strong>Glass-break sensor</strong>: a sensor tuned to the acoustic or vibration signature of breaking glass, reducing false trips from generic loud noises.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit/entry delay</strong>: a timed grace period after arming (exit) or before a trip escalates (entry) that lets a legitimate occupant leave or disarm without triggering a false alarm.</li>\n<li><strong>Panic/duress button</strong>: a manually-triggered alert for personal safety or an in-progress emergency, independent of arm state and sensor zones, that escalates straight to dispatch.</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-zone verification</strong>: requiring two independent sensors/zones to trip within a short time window before treating an event as high-confidence, to cut down false dispatch.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring / central station</strong>: a staffed facility that receives alarm signals, attempts verification, and dispatches police when warranted.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"An armed alarm prevents a break-in.\" → An alarm is a detective and response control, not a preventive one; it can only notice and react to an intrusion that has already started, not physically stop it. Preventive controls are locks, barriers, and access control.</li>\n<li>\"A silent/panic alarm is always about a burglary.\" → A panic or duress button is most often used for personal safety, a medical emergency, a threat of violence, a robbery in progress, and is deliberately designed to bypass the arm-state and verification logic that governs ordinary intrusion sensors, because a human pressing it is already high-confidence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.4.B</p>"},{"id":"2.4.c","kind":"pre","title":"Motion detectors and sensor-based detection","topic":"2.4","href":"unit-2/2.4/2.4.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.4/2.4.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> A locked door stops someone from walking in cleanly. A camera records what happens if they don't.\nBut neither a lock nor a camera actively notices an intruder who is already inside a room after\nhours, a badge reader only fires when a badge is presented, and nobody is watching every camera\nfeed in real time.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The framing worth keeping: a motion sensor answers exactly one narrow question, did something change in this zone, and that narrowness is a feature, not a limitation. It doesn't know who moved, why, or whether they belong there; it hands a single yes/no signal to the alarm system covered in the last atom for a decision-maker (human or rule engine) to interpret with context the sensor itself doesn't have. Once you see the sensor as a narrow, fast tripwire rather than a smart observer, PIR's biggest quirk, that a person standing perfectly still can go undetected, stops being a flaw and starts being exactly what \"detects change\" should mean.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A motion sensor sees everything in the room.\" → Motion sensors have a limited detection cone and blind spots just like cameras; furniture, doorways, and mounting angle can all hide an intruder's movement from a single sensor.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how PIR, microwave, and dual-technology motion sensors detect intrusions, evaluate sensor placement and sensitivity tradeoffs, and classify a given sensor trigger event as a true detection or a false positive.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"2.4.c","kind":"post","title":"Motion detectors and sensor-based detection","topic":"2.4","href":"unit-2/2.4/2.4.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-2/2.4/2.4.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Dual-technology sensors are this skill's best worked example of a principle worth generalizing across the whole course: two independent detectors are dramatically stronger together than either is alone, precisely <em>because</em> their false-positive causes don't overlap. Sunlight fools a PIR element but produces no Doppler shift; HVAC airflow fools a microwave element but produces no meaningful heat delta. Requiring both to agree (AND-logic) filters out almost every nuisance trigger that would fool either one individually, the same cross-zone-verification idea from alarm systems, one atom earlier, just implemented inside a single sensor housing instead of across a room. And the misconception about sensitivity is worth remembering on its own: cranking sensitivity to maximum doesn't make a system more secure, it makes it noisier, and a system that cries wolf constantly is a system whose real alerts eventually get ignored, alarm fatigue is a security failure, not a technical inconvenience.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Passive infrared (PIR) sensor</strong>: a motion sensor that detects a <em>change</em> in infrared (heat) radiation across segments of its field of view, without emitting any energy itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Doppler shift</strong>: the change in frequency of a reflected wave (microwave or ultrasonic) caused by a moving object, used by active sensors to detect motion.</li>\n<li><strong>Dual-technology sensor</strong>: a sensor combining two detection technologies (typically PIR + microwave) that must both trigger together (AND-logic) before an alert fires, reducing false positives.</li>\n<li><strong>Detection cone / coverage pattern</strong>: the limited field of view a motion sensor can actually see; anything outside it, or behind an obstruction, is a blind spot.</li>\n<li><strong>Sensitivity tuning</strong>: adjusting a sensor's trigger threshold to trade off missed detections (too insensitive) against false-positive alerts (too sensitive).</li>\n<li><strong>Zoned / layered detection</strong>: deploying multiple sensors across distinct perimeter and interior zones with overlapping coverage, so an alert both localizes an intrusion and provides defense in depth if one layer is bypassed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A motion sensor sees everything in the room.\" → Motion sensors have a limited detection cone and blind spots just like cameras; furniture, doorways, and mounting angle can all hide an intruder's movement from a single sensor.</li>\n<li>\"Higher sensitivity is always better security.\" → Sensitivity is a tradeoff, not a one-directional dial: maximum sensitivity catches pets, insects, HVAC airflow, and sunlight glare as false positives, which trains staff to ignore alerts (alarm fatigue) and can get a sensor's alerts deprioritized or disabled entirely, the opposite of better security.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 2.4.B, 2.4.C</p>"},{"id":"3.1.a","kind":"pre","title":"TCP/IP fundamentals and network topology flaws","topic":"3.1","href":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every network attack starts with a target: a device, a service, or a data stream that an attacker\nwants to reach, disrupt, or spy on. To understand how attacks work, and how defenses fail, you\nfirst need to understand the underlying plumbing. That plumbing is <strong>TCP/IP</strong>, a family of\nprotocols that governs how data moves between any two points on the internet or a private network.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Hold onto this framing before the lecture even starts: TCP/IP was built for a network of people who already trusted each other, and every structural weakness this skill covers is just that original assumption of trust refusing to die. Nobody added authentication to IP addresses or ARP because nobody in 1975 imagined a stranger would want to lie about who they were. That's not a bug that slipped through code review, it's the entire internet's foundation being built for a world that no longer exists. Once you see the stack that way, IP spoofing and ARP spoofing stop looking like clever hacks and start looking like the completely predictable consequence of never having asked \"but what if the sender lies?\"</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Switches make sniffing impossible.\" → Switches reduce passive sniffing but MAC flooding and ARP spoofing let attackers redirect traffic through their own machine even on switched networks.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Describe how the TCP/IP protocol stack works, identify structural weaknesses in common network topologies, and explain how those weaknesses create attack surfaces that adversaries exploit.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.1.a","kind":"post","title":"TCP/IP fundamentals and network topology flaws","topic":"3.1","href":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The move worth carrying forward out of this skill is asking, for any attack, \"which layer does this operate at, and which layer must the defense operate at?\" That single question is what turns the four-layer stack from a memorization exercise into an actual diagnostic tool: IP spoofing gets fought at the internet layer, ARP spoofing (3.1.c) at the network access layer, DNS poisoning (3.1.b) at the application layer, and a flat topology's blast radius gets fought by segmenting the network itself rather than patching any single layer at all. Notice too that this skill quietly previews the entire rest of Unit 3, DNS attacks, MITM, packet sniffing, VLANs, firewalls, IDS/IPS are all just this skill's four weaknesses (unauthenticated IPs, predictable handshakes, trusting ARP, forgeable DNS) getting their own dedicated defense in later atoms. If you understand why TCP/IP trusts everyone, you already understand why the rest of this unit exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>TCP/IP</strong>: the suite of protocols governing internet communication, organized into four layers: application, transport, internet, network access.</li>\n<li><strong>Encapsulation</strong>: each layer wraps data from the layer above in its own header, creating a protocol data unit (frame, packet, segment, data).</li>\n<li><strong>IP address</strong>: a numerical label assigned to a device at the internet layer; identifies source and destination of packets. IPv4 = 32-bit; IPv6 = 128-bit.</li>\n<li><strong>MAC address</strong>: a 48-bit hardware identifier used at the network access layer for communication within a single local segment.</li>\n<li><strong>Port number</strong>: a 16-bit number at the transport layer identifying which application service should handle incoming data.</li>\n<li><strong>IP spoofing</strong>: forging the source IP address in a packet header to hide the sender's true identity or impersonate another host.</li>\n<li><strong>Three-way handshake</strong>: the TCP connection setup sequence: SYN (client), SYN-ACK (server), ACK (client).</li>\n<li><strong>Network topology</strong>: the arrangement (physical or logical) of devices and links in a network; shape determines broadcast domains and attack surface.</li>\n<li><strong>Network segmentation</strong>: dividing a network into isolated subnets or VLANs to limit lateral movement after a breach.</li>\n<li><strong>MAC flooding</strong>: sending large numbers of fake MAC entries to overflow a switch's table, causing it to broadcast all frames.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Switches make sniffing impossible.\" → Switches reduce passive sniffing but MAC flooding and ARP spoofing let attackers redirect traffic through their own machine even on switched networks.</li>\n<li>\"IP addresses prove identity.\" → IP addresses are easily forged; they are routing labels, not authentication tokens. Actual identity verification requires higher-layer mechanisms (TLS certificates, authenticated protocols).</li>\n<li>\"A firewall on the perimeter is enough.\" → Flat internal networks let a compromised internal host reach everything; segmentation is needed to contain breaches inside the perimeter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.1.A</p>"},{"id":"3.1.b","kind":"pre","title":"DNS attacks (spoofing, cache poisoning)","topic":"3.1","href":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> The Domain Name System (DNS) is the directory service of the internet, translating human-friendly names (like <code>example.com</code>) into computer-routable IP addresses (like <code>192.0.2.1</code>). Because almost every internet connection starts with a DNS lookup, compromising this process allows an attacker to redirect traffic silently and globally.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The intuition to carry in: DNS is a race, and the attacker doesn't need to be strong, just fast and lucky enough times in a row. Because DNS runs over UDP with no session and no built-in verification, a forged reply that arrives before the real one wins outright, not because it's more convincing, but because the resolver has no way to tell a real answer from a fake one except \"which one showed up first.\" That's a genuinely uncomfortable fact to sit with: an entire layer of internet infrastructure that billions of devices depend on every day is, at its core, a guessing game about a 16-bit number.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how the Domain Name System (DNS) is vulnerable to interception and spoofing, trace the mechanics of a DNS cache poisoning attack, and identify modern cryptographic and architectural controls used to protect name resolution.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.1.b","kind":"post","title":"DNS attacks (spoofing, cache poisoning)","topic":"3.1","href":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The throughline worth remembering is that DNS poisoning and ARP spoofing (3.1.c, next atom) are the same underlying vulnerability, a device blindly trusting an unauthenticated reply, expressed at two different layers of the stack you just met in 3.1.a. DNS poisons the mapping from name to IP before a connection even starts; ARP spoofing poisons the mapping from IP to MAC once you're already on the local network. Keep that parallel sharp, because it's exactly the kind of connection the AP exam rewards: recognizing that \"forge the trusted-but-unauthenticated reply\" is a reusable attack pattern, not a one-off DNS quirk. And don't let the misconception slide by, HTTPS staying green in your browser tells you nothing about whether DNS lied to you; it only tells you the certificate matched whatever server you actually reached, which is precisely why a spoofed DNS answer pointing you to an attacker's server usually fails loudly (a cert warning) rather than silently, and why that failure is a feature of the system working, not proof DNS spoofing didn't happen.</p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.1.A, 3.1.B</p>"},{"id":"3.1.c","kind":"pre","title":"Man-in-the-middle and ARP spoofing","topic":"3.1","href":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> A <strong>man-in-the-middle (MITM)</strong> attack is any attack where an adversary secretly positions itself\nbetween two communicating parties, relaying (and often reading or altering) traffic while both\nsides believe they are talking directly to each other.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The mental model worth having before lecture: ARP is a network's equivalent of everyone in a room shouting their own name whenever asked, with zero ID check, and everyone else just believing the loudest recent answer. That's not a metaphor stretched to fit, it's literally how a gratuitous ARP reply works, an answer nobody even asked for that devices accept anyway. Once you've internalized that ARP has no authentication at all, ARP spoofing isn't a clever exploit, it's just... using the protocol exactly as specified, except lying.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"HTTPS fully protects you from a man-in-the-middle.\" → HTTPS encrypts and authenticates the content of the connection, preventing the attacker from reading or silently modifying it without detection, but it does not stop the attacker from occupying the MITM position in the first place, seeing which servers you connect to, or degrading service.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how the Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) can be abused to insert an attacker between two hosts on a local network, trace the mechanics of an ARP spoofing attack step by step, and identify the technical controls that detect or prevent it.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.1.c","kind":"post","title":"Man-in-the-middle and ARP spoofing","topic":"3.1","href":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The detail worth keeping long after this skill: a man-in-the-middle position is not the same thing as a broken connection, and that distinction is what makes MITM attacks so dangerous. Both the victim and the gateway keep working normally the entire time the attacker is silently relaying their traffic, nothing breaks, nothing looks wrong, which is exactly why detection tools like Dynamic ARP Inspection and ARP-watch software matter: the attack is invisible from inside the conversation and only visible from outside it, by noticing a MAC address suddenly claiming an IP it never owned before. Also worth locking in: HTTPS limits what a MITM attacker can <em>read</em>, but does nothing to stop them from <em>occupying the position</em> in the first place, that's a subtlety this course keeps returning to (you'll see it again with DNS in 3.1.b and packet sniffing in 3.1.d): encryption protects the payload, not the position, and those are two completely different problems with two completely different fixes.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Man-in-the-middle (MITM)</strong>: an attack where an adversary secretly relays and can observe or alter traffic between two parties who believe they are communicating directly.</li>\n<li><strong>ARP (Address Resolution Protocol)</strong>: the protocol that maps IP addresses to MAC addresses on a local network; has no built-in authentication.</li>\n<li><strong>ARP spoofing / ARP cache poisoning</strong>: sending forged ARP replies to make a victim's device (or the gateway) associate a legitimate IP address with the attacker's MAC address.</li>\n<li><strong>Gratuitous ARP</strong>: an unsolicited ARP reply, which devices accept and cache even though they never asked the question, enabling spoofing.</li>\n<li><strong>Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI)</strong>: a switch-level control that validates ARP packets against a trusted binding table and drops forged ones.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"HTTPS fully protects you from a man-in-the-middle.\" → HTTPS encrypts and authenticates the content of the connection, preventing the attacker from reading or silently modifying it without detection, but it does not stop the attacker from occupying the MITM position in the first place, seeing which servers you connect to, or degrading service.</li>\n<li>\"ARP spoofing requires access to the router.\" → It requires only that the attacker be on the same local network/broadcast domain as the victim (e.g. the same Wi-Fi or switch segment), no router or gateway access is needed, since ARP replies are sent directly between hosts on the LAN.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.1.B</p>"},{"id":"3.1.d","kind":"pre","title":"Packet capture and traffic sniffing","topic":"3.1","href":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.d/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.d/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> <strong>Packet capture</strong> (often called \"sniffing\") is the process of intercepting and logging network\ntraffic as it passes across a network interface. Every packet that a network interface card (NIC)\nsees can, in principle, be copied and inspected, this is exactly what tools like <strong>Wireshark</strong>\n(graphical) and <strong>tcpdump</strong> (command-line) do.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The idea to hold before this lecture: a sniffer is not itself the attack, the attack is whatever let the sniffer see traffic it shouldn't, and whatever that traffic exposed once seen. Wireshark and tcpdump are the exact same tools a network administrator runs every single day to debug a slow connection; nothing about the tool is malicious. That reframing matters because it means the real security question is never \"is packet capture happening,\" it's \"who has the position to capture, and is the payload readable once they do\", which is precisely the two-part filter this skill hands you.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"You need to be an attacker to use Wireshark.\" → Packet capture is a standard, legitimate network-troubleshooting and security-monitoring tool; it becomes an attack only through unauthorized access or misuse of legitimate positioning (e.g. a misconfigured SPAN port granted to the wrong party).</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how packet capture tools intercept network traffic, apply capture and display filters to isolate relevant packets, and analyze a small packet log to identify credentials or indicators of compromise sent in cleartext.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.1.d","kind":"post","title":"Packet capture and traffic sniffing","topic":"3.1","href":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.d/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.1/3.1.d/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> This skill is the payoff for everything Unit 3 has built up to this point: ARP spoofing (3.1.c) earns an attacker the <em>position</em> to sniff traffic that a switch would otherwise keep hidden from them, and this skill shows you what that position is actually worth, cleartext credentials on HTTP, Telnet, and FTP sitting there in plain readable bytes, versus HTTPS and SSH traffic that shows up as unreadable binary even to an attacker sitting directly in the traffic's path. That's the cleanest demonstration in the whole unit of a principle worth generalizing: you often cannot stop an attacker from reaching a position to listen, so the real defense is making sure whatever they hear is useless to them. Keep the capture-filter-versus-display-filter distinction straight for the exam too, a capture filter decides what's ever recorded, a display filter only decides what you're currently looking at within what was already recorded, and that's an irreversible-versus-reversible distinction the exam likes to probe.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Packet capture (sniffing)</strong>: intercepting and logging network traffic passing a network interface.</li>\n<li><strong>Promiscuous mode</strong>: a NIC setting that accepts all frames it physically receives, not just those addressed to its own MAC.</li>\n<li><strong>Port mirroring / SPAN port</strong>: a switch feature that copies traffic from one or more ports to a monitoring port for legitimate analysis (or, if misused, for eavesdropping).</li>\n<li><strong>Capture filter</strong>: a filter applied before capture that determines which packets are recorded at all.</li>\n<li><strong>Display filter</strong>: a filter applied after capture to narrow which already-captured packets are shown.</li>\n<li><strong>Cleartext protocol</strong>: a protocol (HTTP, Telnet, FTP, unencrypted SMTP/POP3/IMAP) that sends payload data, including credentials, without encryption.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"You need to be an attacker to use Wireshark.\" → Packet capture is a standard, legitimate network-troubleshooting and security-monitoring tool; it becomes an attack only through unauthorized access or misuse of legitimate positioning (e.g. a misconfigured SPAN port granted to the wrong party).</li>\n<li>\"A switched network makes sniffing impossible.\" → A switch limits sniffing compared to a hub, but ARP spoofing, port mirroring misuse, and compromised network taps all still allow an attacker to see traffic they should not on a fully switched network.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.1.B, 3.1.C</p>"},{"id":"3.2.a","kind":"pre","title":"Wi-Fi basics and wireless security protocols (WPA2/WPA3)","topic":"3.2","href":"unit-3/3.2/3.2.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.2/3.2.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every modern office, school, and home relies on Wi-Fi, the family of wireless networking\nstandards collectively known as IEEE 802.11. Radio signals are convenient, but they are also\ninherently public: anyone within range can receive the same frames your device sends. Without a\nstrong security protocol layered on top, an attacker sitting in a parking lot can eavesdrop on\nevery packet.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The frame worth carrying in: Wi-Fi has no cable, which means Wi-Fi has no privacy by default, every frame you send radiates in every direction to anyone with an antenna in range, full stop. WPA2 and WPA3 aren't optional polish on top of a private medium; they're the entire reason Wi-Fi is usable for anything sensitive at all, because without them you'd be broadcasting your traffic to the parking lot the same way you'd be shouting a phone call across a crowded room. Keep that mental picture, broadcasting into public space, and the rest of the atom's design decisions (why authenticate, why encrypt, why the handshake matters) stop being arbitrary standards and start being the obvious response to a genuinely public medium.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Hiding the SSID makes the network secure.\" → A hidden SSID is still broadcast in probe responses; any passive scanner finds it in seconds. SSID hiding is not a security control.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Compare WPA2 and WPA3 wireless security protocols, explain the vulnerabilities each addresses, and evaluate a Wi-Fi network configuration against current best practice.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.2.a","kind":"post","title":"Wi-Fi basics and wireless security protocols (WPA2/WPA3)","topic":"3.2","href":"unit-3/3.2/3.2.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.2/3.2.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The single upgrade worth remembering by name is what WPA3's SAE handshake actually fixes: WPA2's four-way handshake can be captured and taken offline, where an attacker with unlimited time and no risk of detection can grind through a password dictionary at their leisure; SAE's zero-knowledge proof means the captured handshake gives an offline attacker nothing to grind against at all. That's a different <em>kind</em> of security improvement than \"use a longer key\", it's closing off an entire attack strategy, not just making it slower. Notice too how this skill quietly reinforces 3.2.b's point before you've even gotten there: WPA3 is a technical control, but it only protects you if the <em>managerial</em> decision to disable WPA2 fallback and enforce 20-character random passwords actually gets made and enforced. A network can have the strongest wireless protocol on Earth and still be wide open because the policy layer around it never caught up, the protocol is necessary, but on its own it is not sufficient.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IEEE 802.11</strong>: the family of wireless networking standards defining Wi-Fi.</li>\n<li><strong>Access point (AP)</strong>: the device that bridges wireless clients onto a wired network.</li>\n<li><strong>WPA2 (Wi-Fi Protected Access 2)</strong>: the 2004 wireless security standard using AES-CCMP; current minimum acceptable.</li>\n<li><strong>WPA3</strong>: the 2018 successor; uses SAE to prevent offline dictionary attacks and provides forward secrecy.</li>\n<li><strong>Pre-Shared Key (PSK)</strong>: a shared password used in WPA2/WPA3 Personal mode to derive session keys.</li>\n<li><strong>SAE (Simultaneous Authentication of Equals)</strong>: WPA3's zero-knowledge handshake that resists offline attacks.</li>\n<li><strong>Forward secrecy</strong>: property ensuring that compromise of one session key cannot decrypt past sessions.</li>\n<li><strong>CCMP (AES-CCMP)</strong>: the AES-based cipher suite providing confidentiality and integrity in WPA2/WPA3.</li>\n<li><strong>TKIP</strong>: deprecated RC4-based cipher used in WPA (v1); do not use.</li>\n<li><strong>Four-way handshake</strong>: WPA2's session-key negotiation; capturable and subject to offline attack.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Hiding the SSID makes the network secure.\" → A hidden SSID is still broadcast in probe responses; any passive scanner finds it in seconds. SSID hiding is not a security control.</li>\n<li>\"WPA2 with a strong password is good enough for enterprise.\" → Personal mode shares one key across all users; a departing employee who knew the PSK retains access until the password is changed for everyone. Enterprise mode (802.1X) is required for individual accountability.</li>\n<li>\"WPA3 is automatically used if the router supports it.\" → Mixed-mode deployments allow WPA2-only clients to connect using WPA2; an attacker can force a downgrade. For maximum protection, disable WPA2 compatibility on networks where all clients support WPA3.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.2.A</p>"},{"id":"3.2.b","kind":"pre","title":"Managerial controls and security policies","topic":"3.2","href":"unit-3/3.2/3.2.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.2/3.2.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Security controls are commonly grouped into three families, and this course expects you to be\nable to sort any given control into the right one. <strong>Technical (logical) controls</strong> are implemented\nin hardware or software and enforce policy automatically, without a person deciding case-by-case, a firewall rule, WPA3 encryption (3.2.a), or an access-control list are all technical controls.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The habit worth building before this lecture: whenever you meet a new control anywhere in this course, ask \"is this a piece of hardware or software enforcing something automatically, or is this a document telling a human what they're supposed to do?\" That one question is the entire managerial-versus-technical-versus-physical sort, and it matters because this skill is explicitly calling out a bias most students (and most real organizations) have without realizing it: technical controls feel like \"real security\" and policy feels like paperwork, when in practice policy is usually what determines whether the technical controls even stay configured correctly.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A policy is not a real control unless it's enforced by software.\" → Managerial controls are a distinct, legitimate control family (alongside technical and physical). A signed AUP, documented access-review cadence, or completed awareness training all count as controls in their own right, even though none of them are software.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Distinguish managerial (administrative) controls from technical and physical controls, describe the purpose of core security policy documents (AUP, least privilege, security awareness training), and match a given organizational scenario to the correct control type and policy.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.2.b","kind":"post","title":"Managerial controls and security policies","topic":"3.2","href":"unit-3/3.2/3.2.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.2/3.2.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The sentence worth keeping from this skill is the one about the relationship, not either family alone: a policy without enforcement is close to useless, but a technical control without a policy explaining its purpose gets quietly circumvented by frustrated employees who don't understand why it's there. That's the same insight Unit 2 built around physical and technical controls stacking to cover each other's blind spots, here it's the managerial layer doing that job for the technical and physical layers instead. Keep least privilege and privilege creep especially sharp for the exam: the <em>technical</em> enforcement is an ACL denying a specific file read, but the <em>managerial</em> control is the recurring review process that catches when someone's access should have been revoked months ago and wasn't, miss that distinction and you'll misclassify half of this skill's practice scenarios. This skill is also the hinge that connects Unit 3's very technical first half (protocols, spoofing, sniffing) to 3.2.c's layered-defense model, where you'll see the managerial layer named explicitly as the one that spans and reinforces every other layer rather than sitting in its own separate lane.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Managerial (administrative) control</strong>: a documented policy, procedure, or process governing human behavior and organizational process, enforced through training, review, and consequences rather than automatically by hardware/software.</li>\n<li><strong>Technical (logical) control</strong>: a control implemented in hardware or software that enforces policy automatically (e.g., a firewall, encryption, an access-control list).</li>\n<li><strong>Physical control</strong>: a control that restricts physical access to a space or device (e.g., a locked door, a badge reader).</li>\n<li><strong>Acceptable Use Policy (AUP)</strong>: a policy defining permitted and prohibited use of organizational systems and data.</li>\n<li><strong>Least privilege policy</strong>: a policy requiring that access be limited to the minimum necessary for a role, with a documented request/review/revocation process.</li>\n<li><strong>Security awareness training</strong>: a recurring program that teaches employees to recognize threats (like social engineering) and follow security policy.</li>\n<li><strong>Privilege creep</strong>: the gradual, unreviewed accumulation of unnecessary access rights over time, which least-privilege review policies are designed to catch and correct.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A policy is not a real control unless it's enforced by software.\" → Managerial controls are a distinct, legitimate control family (alongside technical and physical). A signed AUP, documented access-review cadence, or completed awareness training all count as controls in their own right, even though none of them are software.</li>\n<li>\"Security awareness training is just a compliance checkbox.\" → When implemented well (recurring, reinforced with realistic phishing simulations, tied to measurable behavior change), awareness training measurably reduces successful social-engineering attacks, it is functioning as a control, not merely satisfying an audit requirement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.2.A, 3.2.B</p>"},{"id":"3.2.c","kind":"pre","title":"Layered network defense overview","topic":"3.2","href":"unit-3/3.2/3.2.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.2/3.2.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every control studied so far in Unit 3, DNS protections (3.1.b), ARP/MITM defenses (3.1.c),\nencryption against packet sniffing (3.1.d), strong Wi-Fi protocols (3.2.a), and managerial policy\n(3.2.b), addresses a specific threat at a specific point. None of them, alone, protects an\norganization completely.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Walk into this skill already knowing its punchline, because it's worth having ahead of time: every control this unit has covered so far, DNSSEC, ARP inspection, encryption, WPA3, an AUP, only stops <em>one specific kind</em> of attack, and none of them overlaps with any of the others. WPA3 does nothing whatsoever against DNS poisoning. DNSSEC does nothing whatsoever against a socially engineered password handover. That's not a weakness in any individual control; it's the reason defense in depth exists as a principle at all, and this skill is where all of Unit 3's separate pieces finally get organized into one picture instead of feeling like a list of unrelated topics.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"The strongest single control is better than several weaker ones.\" → Different controls defend against different, non-overlapping attack vectors; an attacker only needs to find the one gap a single-control strategy leaves open. Depth matters more than any individual control's strength.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain the principle of defense in depth, place a given security control at the correct layer of a layered network defense model, and evaluate why relying on any single control creates a single point of failure.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.2.c","kind":"post","title":"Layered network defense overview","topic":"3.2","href":"unit-3/3.2/3.2.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.2/3.2.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The layer model here is worth memorizing by function, not just by name, because the exam wants you to <em>place</em> a novel control correctly, not recite the list: perimeter stops obviously bad traffic at the edge, network limits how far an attacker can move once they're past the edge, host protects the individual device assuming the network layer failed, application protects the logic running on that device, data protects the payload itself as the innermost and last line of defense, and human/managerial doesn't occupy a physical location at all, it spans and reinforces every other layer, which is exactly why 3.2.b's AUP and awareness training get filed here rather than off in their own silo. The genuinely important idea underneath all six layers is this: depth doesn't mean more security products, it means covering <em>non-overlapping</em> attack vectors, so that an attacker who defeats one layer's control still has to separately defeat the next layer's completely different control. Every remaining atom in this unit, VLANs, zero trust, firewalls, IDS/IPS, monitoring, incident response, is really just this skill's model getting one layer at a time filled in with concrete machinery.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Defense in depth (layered defense)</strong>: the principle of using multiple independent security layers so that failure of any single control does not result in full compromise.</li>\n<li><strong>Perimeter layer</strong>: the boundary between an internal network and the public internet (firewall, IPS, DNS filtering).</li>\n<li><strong>Network layer</strong>: internal segmentation and traffic control (VLANs, internal firewalls, Wi-Fi security).</li>\n<li><strong>Host layer</strong>: controls on individual devices (endpoint antivirus/EDR, host firewalls, patching).</li>\n<li><strong>Data layer</strong>: controls protecting data itself (encryption at rest/in transit, DLP).</li>\n<li><strong>Single point of failure</strong>: any control whose failure alone results in a complete compromise, which defense in depth is specifically designed to avoid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"The strongest single control is better than several weaker ones.\" → Different controls defend against different, non-overlapping attack vectors; an attacker only needs to find the one gap a single-control strategy leaves open. Depth matters more than any individual control's strength.</li>\n<li>\"Managerial controls (policy, training) aren't really part of network defense.\" → The human/managerial layer spans and reinforces every technical layer, it is what keeps technical controls correctly configured and catches what automated tools miss, making it a genuine and necessary layer, not an afterthought.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.2.B, 3.2.C</p>"},{"id":"3.3.a","kind":"pre","title":"VLANs and network segmentation","topic":"3.3","href":"unit-3/3.3/3.3.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.3/3.3.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Networks that treat every connected device as equally trusted are far more dangerous than they appear. When an attacker compromises one machine on a flat network, a network where every device can freely reach every other device, they inherit access to printers, file servers, cameras, payroll databases, and anything else plugged into the same broadcast domain.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The image worth holding onto walking in: a flat network is a building with no interior walls, where a fire that starts in the lobby reaches the server room with nothing in its way. VLANs are those interior walls, built in switch software rather than drywall, and the entire justification for segmentation is that a compromise of one device should never automatically mean a compromise of everything plugged into the same switch. That's the direct payoff of 3.1.a's flat-network warning from the start of this unit, this skill is where you finally get the tool that actually builds the walls it warned you were missing.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A VLAN is the same as a separate physical network.\" A VLAN is a logical separation on shared hardware. Physical separation (an air gap) provides stronger isolation, but VLANs are practical and sufficient for most environments when firewall rules are enforced at the routing boundary.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain what a VLAN is, describe how network segmentation limits the spread of an attack, and assign hosts to VLANs to enforce separation between different trust zones.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.3.a","kind":"post","title":"VLANs and network segmentation","topic":"3.3","href":"unit-3/3.3/3.3.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.3/3.3.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The distinction worth locking in for the exam is that a VLAN is a <em>logical</em> separation on <em>shared</em> physical hardware, not an air gap, which means the wall only holds if the router and firewall rules governing inter-VLAN traffic are actually configured to deny what they should deny. A misconfigured router that permits all inter-VLAN traffic doesn't just weaken segmentation, it defeats it completely, which is the same lesson 3.2.b taught about policy without enforcement, now showing up as a technical-control version of the same failure mode. Keep trunk ports and access ports straight too: an access port is invisible-tag, single-VLAN, end-user-facing; a trunk port carries multiple VLANs' tagged traffic between switches and routers, and it's precisely the kind of link an attacker would want to reach if they were trying to jump VLANs illegitimately. This skill is also your bridge into 3.3.b, VLANs get you zone-level isolation (guests can't reach servers), but notice the gap already lurking in this skill's four-VLAN design: nothing stops two devices <em>inside</em> the same VLAN from talking to each other freely, which is exactly the weakness the next atom exists to close.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>VLAN (Virtual Local Area Network)</strong>: a logical broadcast domain created in switch software, isolating traffic between groups of ports without requiring separate physical switches.</li>\n<li><strong>Network segmentation</strong>: dividing a network into zones with controlled, limited connectivity between them to reduce the blast radius of a breach.</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcast domain</strong>: the set of devices that receive a broadcast frame; VLANs confine broadcasts to only the members of that VLAN.</li>\n<li><strong>Trunk port</strong>: a switch port that carries tagged frames for multiple VLANs, used on uplinks between network devices.</li>\n<li><strong>Access port</strong>: a switch port assigned to exactly one VLAN, used for end-user devices.</li>\n<li><strong>Inter-VLAN routing</strong>: the process of forwarding traffic between VLANs through a router or layer-3 switch, where firewall rules can be applied.</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral movement</strong>: the technique by which an attacker hops from one compromised host to another within a network.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A VLAN is the same as a separate physical network.\" A VLAN is a logical separation on shared hardware. Physical separation (an air gap) provides stronger isolation, but VLANs are practical and sufficient for most environments when firewall rules are enforced at the routing boundary.</li>\n<li>\"Setting up VLANs means attackers definitely cannot cross them.\" VLANs stop direct layer-2 traffic. If the router/firewall rules are misconfigured to allow all inter-VLAN traffic, segmentation is defeated. The firewall policy at the routing layer is what enforces the separation.</li>\n<li>\"The management VLAN is fine to share with users for convenience.\" Placing user devices on the management VLAN exposes switch and router administration interfaces. The management VLAN must always be isolated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.3.A</p>"},{"id":"3.3.b","kind":"pre","title":"Traffic isolation and zero-trust segmentation","topic":"3.3","href":"unit-3/3.3/3.3.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.3/3.3.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Traditional network security relies on a \"perimeter\" model, often compared to a castle with a moat. Under this design, the organization builds strong defenses at the network perimeter (using firewalls and intrusion prevention systems) to keep attackers out.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Before this lecture, sit with the uncomfortable fact this skill opens with: a VLAN-segmented network still runs on implicit trust, once you're inside a zone, or inside the perimeter at all, older security models simply assume you belong there. Zero Trust's whole premise is refusing that assumption entirely, treating a device on the corporate Wi-Fi with exactly as much suspicion as a device on the open internet, because getting in has never actually meant you should be trusted with everything inside.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Zero Trust means we don't trust our employees.\" → Zero Trust is a technical posture, not a human resource policy. It protects employees' credentials from being abused by automating checks and preventing compromised devices from spreading malware.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain the limitations of traditional network segmentation, define the core principles of Zero Trust Network Architecture (ZTNA), and apply traffic isolation and microsegmentation strategies to prevent lateral movement.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.3.b","kind":"post","title":"Traffic isolation and zero-trust segmentation","topic":"3.3","href":"unit-3/3.3/3.3.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.3/3.3.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The connection worth making explicit: 3.3.a's VLANs solved the flat-network problem from 3.1.a by drawing walls between zones, but this skill shows you the gap VLANs leave behind, devices <em>inside</em> the same zone can still scan and attack each other freely, and that gap is exactly what let the ransomware scenario in this skill's Scenario A spread to fifty machines even on a segmented network. Microsegmentation and PVLANs close that gap by treating every single host as its own isolated segment rather than trusting the zone as a whole, and the never-trust-always-verify mantra means even a successful login or a healthy-looking device gets continuously re-evaluated rather than granted a blanket pass once admission happens. Keep the assume-breach principle especially close, because it's the throughline connecting this skill back to defense in depth in 3.2.c: Zero Trust isn't a claim that breaches won't happen, it's a design philosophy built around the assumption that they <em>will</em>, engineered so a single compromised machine's blast radius stops at exactly one machine instead of cascading through an entire trust zone.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zero Trust</strong>: A cybersecurity paradigm centered on the belief that organizations should not automatically trust anything inside or outside its perimeters; instead, they must verify anything and everything trying to connect.</li>\n<li><strong>Implicit trust</strong>: The practice of automatically trusting a device or user based on their network location, IP address, or initial login.</li>\n<li><strong>Microsegmentation</strong>: A method of creating secure zones in data centers and cloud environments to isolate workloads from one another and secure them individually.</li>\n<li><strong>Private VLAN (PVLAN)</strong>: A layer-2 isolation mechanism that divides a VLAN into sub-domains, preventing devices in the same subnet from communicating directly.</li>\n<li><strong>Isolated port</strong>: A PVLAN port that is completely blocked from talking to any other host in the same VLAN, except the gateway (promiscuous port).</li>\n<li><strong>Promiscuous port</strong>: A PVLAN port that can communicate with all other ports (isolated, community, promiscuous) in the VLAN, typically reserved for gateways.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Zero Trust means we don't trust our employees.\" → Zero Trust is a technical posture, not a human resource policy. It protects employees' credentials from being abused by automating checks and preventing compromised devices from spreading malware.</li>\n<li>\"Once we set up VLANs, we have achieved Zero Trust.\" → VLANs are a tool for traditional segmentation. Zero Trust requires continuous verification of identity and device health, not just IP address routing. A flat VLAN network still relies on implicit trust within the subnet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.3.B</p>"},{"id":"3.4.a","kind":"pre","title":"Firewall types (stateful vs. stateless)","topic":"3.4","href":"unit-3/3.4/3.4.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.4/3.4.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every network sits behind some form of gatekeeper, a device that decides which packets may enter\nor leave. The firewall is the most fundamental of those gatekeepers. Before you can configure one,\nyou need to understand how it thinks about traffic.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The question to carry into this lecture: does the firewall remember anything, or does it wake up with total amnesia before every single packet? That's the entire stateless-versus-stateful distinction in one sentence, and it's worth having ready because the exam scenario that trips students up almost always hinges on exactly this, a packet that looks perfectly legitimate in isolation (correct port, correct protocol, an ACK bit set) but that no real connection ever actually started.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A stateful firewall is always more secure than a stateless one.\" → Stateful is more aware of connection context, but security also depends on the rules written. A stateful firewall with a rule that permits all inbound traffic is still insecure.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Distinguish stateless from stateful firewalls, explain how each inspects traffic, and identify which type is better suited to a given threat scenario.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.4.a","kind":"post","title":"Firewall types (stateful vs. stateless)","topic":"3.4","href":"unit-3/3.4/3.4.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.4/3.4.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The scenario worth remembering by name is the spoofed RST packet: a stateless firewall sees TCP, port 80, allowed and waves it through, because a stateless firewall genuinely cannot ask whether this is part of a connection that actually exists, it has no memory to ask that question with. A stateful firewall's connection-tracking table is what makes that question askable at all, and that's precisely why stateful inspection defeats a whole category of spoofing and session-hijacking attacks that a stateless ruleset simply cannot see. Don't let stateful expand in your head into smart, though, this skill is explicit that stateful inspection tracks <em>connection state</em>, not <em>payload content</em>; malware riding inside an already-permitted HTTPS session sails straight through a stateful firewall because the firewall's only question is whether the session is established, not what's actually inside it. That gap, connection-aware but content-blind, is exactly the opening the next atom's ACL precision starts to narrow, and it's also the reason 3.5's IDS/IPS atom needs to exist at all: something has to inspect what a stateful firewall, by design, does not.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stateless (packet-filter) firewall</strong>: examines each packet independently against a fixed rule set; no memory of prior packets or connection state.</li>\n<li><strong>Stateful (stateful-inspection) firewall</strong>: maintains a connection-tracking table; permits return traffic for established sessions and drops packets that do not match any tracked session.</li>\n<li><strong>Connection-tracking table</strong>: data structure the firewall uses to record active sessions (source IP, destination IP, ports, protocol, and connection state).</li>\n<li><strong>Packet header</strong>: the metadata portion of a packet (IP addresses, ports, flags, protocol) that both firewall types examine.</li>\n<li><strong>TCP three-way handshake</strong>: the SYN / SYN-ACK / ACK exchange that establishes a TCP session; stateful firewalls use this to know when a connection is legitimately open.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A stateful firewall is always more secure than a stateless one.\" → Stateful is more aware of connection context, but security also depends on the rules written. A stateful firewall with a rule that permits all inbound traffic is still insecure.</li>\n<li>\"Stateful firewalls inspect the content of packets.\" → Stateful inspection tracks connection state, not application payload. Content inspection is a separate, application-layer function.</li>\n<li>\"Stateless firewalls are obsolete.\" → They are still used where performance and simplicity matter, such as high-throughput edge routers or embedded devices with limited CPU.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.4.A</p>"},{"id":"3.4.b","kind":"pre","title":"Firewall rules and access control lists (ACLs)","topic":"3.4","href":"unit-3/3.4/3.4.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.4/3.4.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> A firewall's decisions are only as good as the rule list it is given. That rule list is called an\naccess control list (ACL): an ordered sequence of statements, each specifying an action (allow or\ndeny) and a set of matching conditions, source address, destination address, destination port,\nand protocol.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The one fact worth having memorized before you even open this lecture: order is not a formatting choice in an ACL, it is the entire logic of the ACL. First-match-wins means a firewall stops reading the instant a packet matches any rule, so the identical set of rules written in a different order can produce a completely different, and completely wrong, security outcome, with zero code changed, only the order of lines on a page.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"The order of ACL rules doesn't matter as long as all the right rules exist.\" → Order is everything under first-match-wins; a correct rule placed after a broader matching rule never fires.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Write and evaluate an ordered firewall access control list (ACL), correctly apply first-match-wins evaluation, and identify when a rule's placement creates a security gap by shadowing a more specific rule below it.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.4.b","kind":"post","title":"Firewall rules and access control lists (ACLs)","topic":"3.4","href":"unit-3/3.4/3.4.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.4/3.4.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Rule shadowing is the concept to walk away from this skill actually able to <em>diagnose</em>, not just define: a broad allow rule sitting above a narrow, more careful deny rule doesn't conflict with that deny rule, it makes it unreachable dead code, and the fix is never to argue about which rule is more correct, it's purely about which one is physically listed first. That's a sharper, more mechanical version of the same lesson 3.3.a taught about misconfigured inter-VLAN routing defeating segmentation: a security boundary that looks correct on paper can be silently defeated by one ordering mistake nobody would notice just by reading the rules in isolation. Tie the implicit deny-all back to 3.2.b's least-privilege framing too, default-deny is least privilege applied to an entire network boundary, permitting only what's explicitly needed and rejecting everything else by default rather than trying to enumerate every possible bad packet, which is a losing game against an adversary who only needs to find the one case you forgot to write a rule for.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Access control list (ACL)</strong>: an ordered list of allow/deny rules a firewall applies to traffic, matched on source, destination, port, and protocol.</li>\n<li><strong>First-match-wins</strong>: the evaluation model where a packet is judged by the first rule it matches, top to bottom; later rules are never consulted for that packet.</li>\n<li><strong>Implicit deny (default deny)</strong>: the unwritten or explicit final ACL rule that drops any packet matching nothing above it.</li>\n<li><strong>Rule shadowing</strong>: when a broad rule earlier in the list matches everything a more specific rule below it would match, making the lower rule unreachable.</li>\n<li><strong>Rule specificity</strong>: how narrowly a rule's conditions match traffic; a rule scoped to one host and one port is more specific than one that matches \"any\" for those fields.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"The order of ACL rules doesn't matter as long as all the right rules exist.\" → Order is everything under first-match-wins; a correct rule placed after a broader matching rule never fires.</li>\n<li>\"If there's no explicit deny-all at the end, unmatched traffic is allowed.\" → Real firewall ACLs are default-deny; unmatched traffic is dropped by the implicit deny even if no rule states it. (Some routers default to allow, the AP framework assumes default-deny for security devices.)</li>\n<li>\"A more specific deny rule automatically overrides a broader allow rule.\" → Nothing \"overrides\" anything in a first-match-wins engine. The only thing that matters is which rule is physically listed first.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.4.B, 3.4.C, 3.4.D</p>"},{"id":"3.5.a","kind":"pre","title":"Intrusion detection and prevention systems (IDS/IPS)","topic":"3.5","href":"unit-3/3.5/3.5.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.5/3.5.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> A firewall decides what traffic is allowed in and out. An intrusion detection or prevention system watches traffic that has already been permitted and asks a harder question: does this allowed traffic actually look like an attack? The difference matters because many attacks travel over ports and protocols that firewalls must leave open, HTTP on port 80, DNS on port 53, encrypted traffic on port 443.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The question worth carrying into lecture: what happens to traffic a firewall already decided to let through? That's the gap this skill lives in, a firewall answers whether a port is allowed, but plenty of real attacks travel over ports that have to stay open (HTTP, DNS, HTTPS), which means allowed and safe are two different questions, and IDS/IPS exists specifically to keep asking the second one after the firewall has already answered the first.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"IDS and IPS are the same thing.\" → IDS only alerts; IPS actively blocks. A misconfigured IPS with a high false-positive rate will drop legitimate traffic and cause outages.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Distinguish between intrusion detection systems (IDS) and intrusion prevention systems (IPS), explain how signature-based and anomaly-based detection work, and identify which alert type (true positive, false positive, true negative, false negative) a given scenario represents.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.5.a","kind":"post","title":"Intrusion detection and prevention systems (IDS/IPS)","topic":"3.5","href":"unit-3/3.5/3.5.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.5/3.5.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Keep signature-based and anomaly-based detection sorted by what each one trades away: signatures are precise and fast but structurally blind to anything new, since a zero-day by definition has no entry in the database yet; anomaly detection can catch a never-before-seen attack precisely because it doesn't need a signature at all, but it pays for that with a much higher false-positive rate, since unusual and malicious are not the same thing. The TP/FP/FN/TN grid is worth being fluent in cold, because the exam's favorite trap is treating false positives as merely annoying, they're actually dangerous precisely because enough of them causes analysts to start tuning out real alerts, and an ignored true positive is functionally identical to a system that never detected anything at all. That alert-fatigue idea is the direct throughline into 3.5.b: a signature engine only catches what it already recognizes, but broader traffic monitoring, watching volume, timing, and destination rather than matching known patterns, is what catches everything a signature-based IDS was never going to see coming.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IDS (Intrusion Detection System)</strong>: a passive sensor that copies and analyzes traffic, generating alerts without blocking.</li>\n<li><strong>IPS (Intrusion Prevention System)</strong>: an inline sensor that can drop or block malicious traffic in real time.</li>\n<li><strong>Signature-based detection</strong>: matching traffic against a database of known attack patterns; fast but blind to zero-days.</li>\n<li><strong>Anomaly-based detection</strong>: flagging deviations from a learned behavioral baseline; catches novel attacks but produces more false positives.</li>\n<li><strong>True positive (TP)</strong>: the sensor correctly identified an actual attack.</li>\n<li><strong>False positive (FP)</strong>: the sensor flagged benign traffic as an attack (wasted analyst effort, alert fatigue).</li>\n<li><strong>False negative (FN)</strong>: the sensor missed a real attack (most dangerous outcome).</li>\n<li><strong>NIDS / HIDS</strong>: network-level vs. host-level placement of the sensor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"IDS and IPS are the same thing.\" → IDS only alerts; IPS actively blocks. A misconfigured IPS with a high false-positive rate will drop legitimate traffic and cause outages.</li>\n<li>\"Signature-based IDS catches all attacks.\" → Only attacks with existing signatures are caught. Zero-days and novel variants pass through undetected unless anomaly or behavioral detection is also in place.</li>\n<li>\"More alerts = better security.\" → Alert fatigue from excessive false positives degrades security by causing analysts to stop investigating alerts. Tuning and prioritization matter as much as detection capability.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.5.A, 3.5.D</p>"},{"id":"3.5.b","kind":"pre","title":"Network monitoring and traffic analysis","topic":"3.5","href":"unit-3/3.5/3.5.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.5/3.5.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> An IDS signature engine (3.5.a) catches attacks it already knows about. Network monitoring is the\nbroader discipline of continuously watching traffic itself, volumes, ports, destinations, timing, so that an analyst can notice when something looks wrong even without a matching signature. It\nis the practice of knowing what \"normal\" looks like well enough to recognize \"not normal.\"</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The habit to bring into this lecture, carried straight over from 3.5.a's anomaly-detection idea: you cannot know what's abnormal until you've actually measured what's normal first. That's not a throwaway line, it's the entire justification for baselining, and it reframes network monitoring as fundamentally a measurement discipline before it's ever a detection discipline. Skip the baseline and every alert is just a guess dressed up as a finding.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"You need full packet capture to detect an attack.\" → Most anomalies (volume spikes, beaconing, unusual ports) are visible in flow records alone, without ever inspecting payload content.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how baseline traffic profiles support anomaly detection, read basic flow-record and packet-capture data, and identify network-monitoring indicators of compromise such as beaconing, unusual port usage, and abnormal data-volume spikes.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.5.b","kind":"post","title":"Network monitoring and traffic analysis","topic":"3.5","href":"unit-3/3.5/3.5.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.5/3.5.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The three indicators worth being able to name cold, unusual port usage, beaconing, and volume spikes, share a single underlying idea worth remembering as one thing rather than three: none of them require reading a single byte of payload to be useful. A host beaconing to a command-and-control server at suspiciously regular intervals is detectable purely from <em>timing</em>, a data-exfiltration spike is detectable purely from <em>volume and direction</em>, and that's precisely why flow records (cheap, payload-free summaries) can catch what a full packet capture would only confirm after the fact at far greater storage cost. That's also the connective tissue linking this skill back to 3.1.d's packet-sniffing atom from earlier in the unit: encryption defeats an attacker trying to <em>read</em> your payload, but it does absolutely nothing to hide the <em>pattern</em> of your traffic, which is exactly the signal network monitoring is built to exploit on defense instead. Layer this skill on top of 3.5.a and 3.4's firewall atoms and you get the complete three-tier picture this unit has been building toward the whole time: the firewall decides what's allowed to start, the IDS matches what's already known to be bad, and monitoring catches everything left over, attacks using fully permitted ports and protocols, with no matching signature, that only give themselves away through volume, timing, or destination drifting off the baseline.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Packet capture (pcap)</strong>: full-fidelity recording of every packet, including payload; precise but storage- and search-expensive at scale.</li>\n<li><strong>Flow record (NetFlow)</strong>: a summarized record of a single conversation (endpoints, ports, protocol, byte/packet counts, duration) without payload; the common basis for large-scale monitoring.</li>\n<li><strong>Baseline</strong>: the recorded profile of \"normal\" traffic (volume, ports, timing, destinations) that current traffic is compared against.</li>\n<li><strong>Beaconing</strong>: regular, machine-timed outbound connections characteristic of malware command-and-control check-ins.</li>\n<li><strong>Data exfiltration (volume-based indicator)</strong>: an abnormal spike in outbound data volume, often the clearest sign that data is actively leaving the network.</li>\n<li><strong>SIEM (Security Information and Event Management)</strong>: a platform that aggregates and correlates logs and flow data from many sources for centralized analyst query and alerting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"You need full packet capture to detect an attack.\" → Most anomalies (volume spikes, beaconing, unusual ports) are visible in flow records alone, without ever inspecting payload content.</li>\n<li>\"A baseline is set once and never changes.\" → Legitimate traffic patterns shift (new applications, seasonal usage, staff changes); baselines must be re-established periodically or monitoring drifts into constant false positives.</li>\n<li>\"Any traffic on an unusual port is malicious.\" → Unusual-port traffic is a prompt for investigation, not automatic proof of compromise, new legitimate software can also introduce new ports. Volume, destination, and timing context matter together.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.5.B, 3.5.E</p>"},{"id":"3.5.c","kind":"pre","title":"Incident response sequence","topic":"3.5","href":"unit-3/3.5/3.5.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.5/3.5.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Detecting an attack, via a firewall log, an IDS alert, or a traffic anomaly, is only the\nbeginning. What an organization does in the minutes, hours, and days after detection determines\nwhether the incident is a contained inconvenience or a catastrophic breach.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The single sentence worth memorizing before this lecture, because it's the atom's entire argument in miniature: detecting an attack is the beginning of the story, not the end of it, and what an organization does in the next few hours is what actually determines whether this becomes a contained inconvenience or a catastrophe. Every detection tool this unit has built up, firewalls, IDS/IPS, traffic monitoring, exists to trigger this skill's process, not to replace it.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"You should remove the malware as soon as you find it.\" → Removing malware before containment can tip off the attacker or allow reinfection; contain first, then eradicate.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Name the six phases of the incident-response lifecycle in order, classify a given response action into its correct phase, and explain why performing actions out of order (for example, eradicating before containing) can make an incident worse.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"3.5.c","kind":"post","title":"Incident response sequence","topic":"3.5","href":"unit-3/3.5/3.5.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-3/3.5/3.5.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The ordering logic is the part worth being able to defend, not just recite: containment has to come before eradication because cleaning a still-connected host gives the attacker a chance to notice and either react or simply reinfect a moment later, and recovery has to wait for eradication to be verified complete because bringing a system back online before the root cause is actually gone just restarts the incident from the same starting point. That's not bureaucratic sequencing for its own sake, each phase is a genuine precondition for the next one succeeding at all, the same each-layer-assumes-the-one-before-it-held logic that ran through 3.2.c's defense-in-depth model, just applied to a timeline instead of a network diagram. And don't let Lessons Learned read as an afterthought: it's the phase that closes the loop back to Preparation, the very first phase, which is precisely why skipping it is how the <em>same</em> incident keeps recurring, the technical fix in Eradication handles this specific instance, but the process gap that allowed it in the first place (a missing patch policy, an unmonitored segment, exactly the kind of managerial-control failure 3.2.b warned about) goes unaddressed until someone deliberately writes it down and fixes it. This skill is a fitting closer for Unit 3 because it's where every technical control from 3.1 through 3.5, spoofing defenses, segmentation, firewalls, IDS/IPS, monitoring, finally meets the organizational process that decides whether any of it actually mattered when a real incident happens.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Incident response (IR)</strong>: the structured process an organization follows to detect, contain, and recover from a security incident.</li>\n<li><strong>Six-phase IR lifecycle (SANS PICERL)</strong>: Preparation, Identification, Containment, Eradication, Recovery, Lessons Learned. NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2 groups the same steps into four phases.</li>\n<li><strong>Containment (short-term / long-term)</strong>: actions that stop an incident from spreading, performed before the root cause is removed.</li>\n<li><strong>Eradication</strong>: removing the actual cause of the incident (malware, backdoor accounts, unpatched vulnerability) after containment.</li>\n<li><strong>Lessons Learned (Post-Incident Activity)</strong>: the closing phase where the team documents the incident and updates its plan, tools, or training to prevent recurrence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"You should remove the malware as soon as you find it.\" → Removing malware before containment can tip off the attacker or allow reinfection; contain first, then eradicate.</li>\n<li>\"Incident response starts when the incident happens.\" → Preparation is itself a phase, performed continuously before any incident, and is what determines how effective the rest of the response will be.</li>\n<li>\"Recovery means the incident is over.\" → Recovery restores operations, but Lessons Learned is still required, without it, the organizational gap that allowed the incident often persists.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 3.5.C</p>"},{"id":"4.1.a","kind":"pre","title":"Malware types (viruses, worms, trojans, ransomware)","topic":"4.1","href":"unit-4/4.1/4.1.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.1/4.1.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> The word \"malware\" is a contraction of <em>malicious software</em>: any program intentionally designed\nto cause harm, steal data, disrupt operations, or give an attacker unauthorized control. Malware\nis not a single thing, it is a family of distinct weapon types, each with different mechanics.\nThe AP framework focuses on four categories that account for the vast majority of real-world\ndevice compromises.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Before you memorize four malware names, notice the one question that sorts all of them: does this thing need a human to help it move, or does it move on its own? A virus and a trojan both need you, you open the infected file, you install the fake app. A worm needs nobody; it finds the next machine itself. That single fork explains why the defenses look so different: user education stops the first group, network segmentation stops the second, and no amount of \"don't click suspicious links\" training will slow down a worm that never asked you to click anything.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Antivirus software catches all malware.\" → Signature-based tools miss new variants; behavioral detection helps but zero-day malware can evade it. Defense-in-depth (backups, patching, user awareness) is essential.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify the four major malware categories, viruses, worms, trojans, and ransomware, describe how each spreads and causes harm, and classify a described infection by its behavioral signature.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.1.a","kind":"post","title":"Malware types (viruses, worms, trojans, ransomware)","topic":"4.1","href":"unit-4/4.1/4.1.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.1/4.1.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The detail worth keeping long after the quiz is that these four categories describe <em>mechanism</em>, not damage, and real attacks freely mix them, a worm that carries a trojan payload that later fetches ransomware isn't a contradiction, it's the normal case. That should reframe how you read the ransomware section, too: the popular image of ransomware as digital vandalism is wrong in a way that matters. It doesn't destroy your files, it locks them, which is precisely why the single most effective countermeasure isn't a smarter antivirus, it's an offline backup that makes the attacker's leverage worthless. Keep that distinction between destruction and encryption in mind, because the rest of Unit 4 is really the story of what you build to keep that leverage from ever existing: hardened devices (4.3.a) so the infection vector narrows, endpoint protection (4.3.b) so the payload gets caught mid-execution, and detection (4.4.a-c) so that even a successful infection gets noticed before it finishes its job.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Malware</strong>: malicious software intentionally designed to harm, steal from, or take control of a device or network.</li>\n<li><strong>Virus</strong>: malware that attaches to a host file and spreads when the host is executed or shared; requires user action to propagate.</li>\n<li><strong>Worm</strong>: self-replicating malware that spreads autonomously across networks by exploiting vulnerabilities; no host file or user action needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Trojan</strong>: malware disguised as legitimate software; relies on social deception for installation and does not self-replicate.</li>\n<li><strong>Ransomware</strong>: malware that encrypts victim files and demands payment for the decryption key.</li>\n<li><strong>Infection vector</strong>: the initial path or method by which malware enters a device or network.</li>\n<li><strong>Payload</strong>: the harmful action malware performs after infecting a system (beyond spreading).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Antivirus software catches all malware.\" → Signature-based tools miss new variants; behavioral detection helps but zero-day malware can evade it. Defense-in-depth (backups, patching, user awareness) is essential.</li>\n<li>\"Worms and viruses are the same thing.\" → Viruses need a host file and user action; worms are self-contained and spread without user interaction. The distinction changes which defenses matter most.</li>\n<li>\"Ransomware destroys your files.\" → Ransomware typically encrypts files, leaving them intact but inaccessible; the attacker can decrypt them if paid. The files are not gone, they are locked. Backups break this leverage entirely.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.1.A, 4.1.B</p>"},{"id":"4.1.b","kind":"pre","title":"Device tampering and physical compromise","topic":"4.1","href":"unit-4/4.1/4.1.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.1/4.1.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> The fundamental rule of hardware security is simple: <em>if an attacker has physical access to your device, it is no longer your device.</em> Logical controls, such as passwords, firewalls, and network access lists, assume that the physical chassis of the machine is secure. If an adversary can touch a computer, plug a device into it, or open its case, they can bypass almost every software-based defense.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The sentence to actually internalize here is blunt on purpose: if an attacker has physical access to your device, it is no longer your device. Everything you've learned so far about strong passwords and clever authentication schemes quietly assumes the chassis in front of you is trustworthy, that nobody has opened the case, swapped the RAM, or plugged something into a port while you weren't looking. Unit 2 spent a lot of time on physical security for buildings; this skill is the same argument scaled down to a single laptop, and the punchline is identical: no software control, however well designed, survives an attacker who can touch the hardware it runs on.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Operating system passwords protect my data if my laptop is stolen.\" → Bypassed. An attacker can remove the hard drive and read it on another system, or boot from a Linux USB to browse files, unless Full Disk Encryption (FDE) is enabled.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify physical security threats (including hardware keyloggers, malicious USB drop attacks, and unauthorized physical access), explain how physical compromise bypasses logical network defenses, and analyze strategies to mitigate device tampering.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.1.b","kind":"post","title":"Device tampering and physical compromise","topic":"4.1","href":"unit-4/4.1/4.1.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.1/4.1.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> What makes the USB Rubber Ducky and the hardware keylogger worth remembering together is that they attack from opposite directions of the exact same blind spot: operating systems are built to implicitly trust anything that looks like a keyboard, and antivirus software only ever inspects files and running code, never raw electrical signals arriving before the OS gets involved. Neither attack is a software bug; both are a category of threat that pure software defense literally cannot see, which is exactly why the fixes in this skill (BIOS passwords, disabled boot-from-USB, port blockers) are physical and firmware-level rather than another antivirus signature. Full-disk encryption deserves special attention here because it's your first real preview of Unit 4.2's authentication material: FDE bound to a TPM is really a possession-based unlock gate for an entire drive, and the cold-boot attack is a reminder, one you'll see again with session tokens in 4.2.c, that a cryptographically sound control can still fail because of exactly <em>when</em> it's active, not because the crypto itself is weak.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Device tampering</strong>: unauthorized physical modification or interception of a device to compromise its security.</li>\n<li><strong>USB Rubber Ducky</strong>: a keystroke injection tool disguised as a standard USB flash drive that executes pre-programmed commands by mimicking a keyboard.</li>\n<li><strong>Hardware keylogger</strong>: a physical device connected between a keyboard and computer that records all keystrokes.</li>\n<li><strong>Full Disk Encryption (FDE)</strong>: a cryptographic method that encrypts all data on a storage drive, including the operating system, protecting it from offline reading if the drive is stolen.</li>\n<li><strong>Trusted Platform Module (TPM)</strong>: a dedicated microchip on the motherboard designed to secure hardware by integrating cryptographic keys and verifying boot sequence integrity.</li>\n<li><strong>Port blockers</strong>: physical inserts placed into unused external ports (like USB or Ethernet) to prevent unauthorized devices from being plugged in.</li>\n<li><strong>Chassis intrusion detection</strong>: a switch inside a device's case that logs or alerts when the case is opened, providing after-the-fact evidence of physical tampering.</li>\n<li><strong>Tamper-evident seal</strong>: a sticker or label placed over a device's case or port that visibly tears, discolors, or breaks if removed, letting staff visually confirm a device has not been opened.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Operating system passwords protect my data if my laptop is stolen.\" → Bypassed. An attacker can remove the hard drive and read it on another system, or boot from a Linux USB to browse files, unless Full Disk Encryption (FDE) is enabled.</li>\n<li>\"Antivirus software will block malicious USB keyboard emulators.\" → Antivirus scans files, but a USB Rubber Ducky acts as a keyboard. The OS trusts it as a typing human, meaning it bypasses software-level malware scans unless specific USB device whitelisting is active.</li>\n<li>\"A locked office building means the devices inside are physically secure.\" → Building access controls only stop outsiders; they do nothing against a malicious insider, a visitor left unattended, or a device sitting in an unlocked room within an otherwise secure building. Physical security requires layered controls at the building, room, and device level.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.1.B</p>"},{"id":"4.1.c","kind":"pre","title":"IoT device vulnerabilities","topic":"4.1","href":"unit-4/4.1/4.1.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.1/4.1.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> The Internet of Things (IoT) is the broad category of everyday physical devices, cameras,\nrouters, thermostats, door locks, baby monitors, medical sensors, industrial controllers, that\nconnect to a network and, usually, to the internet. There are tens of billions of these devices\nin use worldwide, and from a security standpoint they are collectively the softest target on the\nmodern network.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Don't memorize the IoT vulnerability list as five unrelated facts, notice they're all downstream of the same root cause: nobody thinks of a light bulb, thermostat, or doorbell as \"a computer that needs security,\" so it never gets treated like one. The device has no screen to prompt you for a password change, no icon in your mental model of \"things I should patch,\" and often no vendor still supporting it a few years in. Every weakness in this skill, default credentials nobody changes, firmware nobody updates, ports nobody closes, traces back to that one blind spot in how people categorize what counts as a computer.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"IoT devices are too small or simple to be worth attacking.\" → False. Attackers rarely care about the device itself; they want it as a botnet node, a pivot point into the rest of the network, or a source of sensitive data (camera feeds, sensor logs). Scale, not sophistication, is what makes IoT attractive.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain why Internet of Things (IoT) devices carry unusually high risk, identify the specific vulnerability categories that make them easy targets, and classify a described smart device by its dominant weakness.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.1.c","kind":"post","title":"IoT device vulnerabilities","topic":"4.1","href":"unit-4/4.1/4.1.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.1/4.1.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The Mirai botnet is worth remembering not as a scary anecdote but as proof of a genuinely counterintuitive idea: individually worthless devices become collectively catastrophic purely through scale, with no sophistication required at all, just a scanner trying the same three or four factory passwords against every IP address on the internet. That should reshape how you think about \"shadow IoT\" as a risk category: it's not dangerous because any one unapproved smart plug is powerful, it's dangerous because attack surface is additive and invisible devices can't be hardened by a team that doesn't know they exist. Hold onto that connection into 4.3.a, where device hardening turns out to be the direct answer to exactly the first two bullet points here, change every default credential, close every unnecessary port, applied as a discipline to <em>every</em> device, not just the traditional laptops and servers a security team is used to thinking about.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IoT (Internet of Things)</strong>: everyday physical devices that connect to a network/internet (cameras, sensors, appliances, controllers) beyond traditional computers and phones.</li>\n<li><strong>Default credentials</strong>: the factory-set username/password shipped on a device, often never changed by the owner, and widely known/scanned for by attackers.</li>\n<li><strong>Firmware</strong>: the low-level software embedded in a device that controls its core operation; often lacks an automatic update mechanism on IoT hardware.</li>\n<li><strong>Attack surface</strong>: the complete set of points where an attacker could try to get into or extract data from a system; more exposed devices/services/ports means a larger attack surface.</li>\n<li><strong>Botnet</strong>: a network of compromised devices remotely controlled by an attacker as a group, often used for large-scale DDoS attacks.</li>\n<li><strong>Mirai</strong>: a 2016 botnet malware that hijacked IoT devices via default Telnet credentials and launched record-setting DDoS attacks; the canonical real-world IoT botnet example.</li>\n<li><strong>UPnP (Universal Plug and Play)</strong>: a protocol that lets devices automatically open ports on a router, which can unintentionally expose management interfaces to the internet.</li>\n<li><strong>Shadow IoT</strong>: devices connected to a network without IT's knowledge, approval, or security oversight, silently expanding the attack surface.</li>\n<li><strong>EOL (end-of-life) device</strong>: a device whose manufacturer has stopped issuing updates or support, leaving any undiscovered or unpatched vulnerabilities permanently exploitable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"IoT devices are too small or simple to be worth attacking.\" → False. Attackers rarely care about the device itself; they want it as a botnet node, a pivot point into the rest of the network, or a source of sensitive data (camera feeds, sensor logs). Scale, not sophistication, is what makes IoT attractive.</li>\n<li>\"If a device isn't connected to the internet, it's safe.\" → A device on a local network can still be reached by anything else already on that network (a compromised laptop, a malicious guest device), and many \"local-only\" devices still phone home to a cloud API. Network placement reduces but does not eliminate risk.</li>\n<li>\"Changing the Wi-Fi password protects every device on the network.\" → A Wi-Fi password controls who can join the network, not whether devices on that network trust each other. A device with default admin credentials or an exposed Telnet port is just as exploitable by another device already inside the network (or through UPnP-punched holes from outside it).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.1.A, 4.1.C, 4.1.D</p>"},{"id":"4.2.a","kind":"pre","title":"Passwords and password policies","topic":"4.2","href":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Authentication is the process of proving you are who you claim to be. The oldest and most\nwidespread mechanism is the password, a shared secret known only to the user and the system.\nDespite decades of criticism, passwords remain ubiquitous, which makes understanding them\nfoundational to any cybersecurity curriculum.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The intuition to carry in is that length beats cleverness, and the math is not close: adding four characters to a password multiplies the attacker's search space by roughly 81 million, while swapping an \"a\" for an \"@\" barely inconveniences a dictionary attack that already expects that substitution. If you remember only one thing from the old-school password rules you grew up with, remember that NIST spent 2017 walking almost all of them back, mandatory symbols, forced 90-day rotation, not because security stopped mattering, but because those specific rules were quietly making passwords <em>worse</em> by pushing everyone toward predictable patterns like Summer2024!.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Complexity rules (uppercase + symbol) make passwords strong.\" -&gt; Complexity rules push users toward predictable patterns (P@ssw0rd1). Length and unpredictability matter more. NIST removed mandatory complexity in 2017.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Evaluate a password against a formal policy (length, complexity, history, lockout) and explain why each rule reduces the risk of unauthorized access.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.2.a","kind":"post","title":"Passwords and password policies","topic":"4.2","href":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The throughline worth naming explicitly is that every rule in a password policy is a direct, traceable answer to a named attack: minimum length answers brute-force, banning breached passwords answers dictionary attacks and credential stuffing, salted slow hashing (bcrypt/Argon2, never MD5 or SHA-1) answers what happens <em>after</em> a database is stolen, and lockout thresholds answer online guessing specifically, with duration itself becoming a tradeoff between stopping an attacker and accidentally letting an attacker lock out a legitimate user as a denial-of-service move. That's the pattern to keep applying for the rest of Unit 4.2: authentication controls always come in this shape, a defense mapped tightly to one specific attack, which is exactly why 4.2.b exists at all, passwords, however well policed, are still just one factor, and a single leaked secret (however strong) grants full access forever until someone notices and changes it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Authentication</strong>: verifying the identity of a user or system before granting access.</li>\n<li><strong>Brute-force attack</strong>: systematically trying every possible password until one succeeds.</li>\n<li><strong>Dictionary attack</strong>: trying passwords from a list of common words, names, and patterns.</li>\n<li><strong>Salt</strong>: a unique random value added to a password before hashing to defeat precomputed attacks.</li>\n<li><strong>Account lockout</strong>: temporarily blocking an account after a threshold of failed login attempts.</li>\n<li><strong>Password policy</strong>: a set of rules an organization enforces on the creation and management of passwords.</li>\n<li><strong>NIST SP 800-63B</strong>: the US federal password guideline that underpins most modern enterprise policy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Complexity rules (uppercase + symbol) make passwords strong.\" -&gt; Complexity rules push users toward predictable patterns (P@ssw0rd1). Length and unpredictability matter more. NIST removed mandatory complexity in 2017.</li>\n<li>\"Changing your password every 90 days improves security.\" -&gt; Forced rotation produces predictable families (\"Spring2024!\", \"Summer2024!\"). Rotate only when there is evidence of compromise.</li>\n<li>\"Blocking copy-paste in the password field is a security feature.\" -&gt; It forces shorter, weaker passwords and prevents password manager use. It is an anti-pattern.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.2.A, 4.2.D</p>"},{"id":"4.2.b","kind":"pre","title":"Multi-factor authentication","topic":"4.2","href":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> A password is a single piece of evidence: if an attacker learns it, through phishing, a data\nbreach, or a guessed weak password, they can log in exactly as the real user, with no further\nobstacle. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) closes this gap by requiring proof from two or more\nindependent <em>categories</em> of evidence before granting access.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Before the lecture, fix this trap in your head so it doesn't catch you on the exam: two passwords is not two-factor authentication, no matter how many boxes you type into. What makes something MFA is that the factors come from different <em>categories</em>, something you know, something you have, something you are, because the whole point is forcing an attacker to succeed at two structurally different kinds of theft simultaneously, not just guess twice. A stolen password plus a stolen second password is still just one skill (guessing or phishing secrets) executed twice.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Entering two different passwords is two-factor authentication.\" → Both are \"something you know\", the same factor category twice. True MFA requires two <em>different</em> categories.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain why single-factor (password-only) login is insufficient, classify a described authentication method into its factor category, judge whether a login attempt satisfies true multi-factor authentication, and identify which MFA methods are phishing-resistant versus phishable.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.2.b","kind":"post","title":"Multi-factor authentication","topic":"4.2","href":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The ranking from SMS codes up through FIDO2 hardware keys is really a story about which attacks each method was never designed to survive, and it's worth tracing the causal chain rather than memorizing the order: SMS fails because a phone <em>number</em> is a carrier-controlled abstraction that can be SIM-swapped away from the phone itself; TOTP fixes that by binding the secret to the device, but a human is still capable of typing a valid code into a fake site, so phishing-relay attacks still work; push notifications fix the typing problem but open a new one, MFA fatigue, where the attacker just spams approval requests until an exhausted user taps yes. FIDO2/WebAuthn is the only method here that closes all three gaps at once, and it does it structurally, not through user vigilance: the cryptographic handshake is bound to the exact domain, so the key simply refuses to respond to a perfect-looking fake page. That's worth connecting back to 4.1.b's evil-maid-style lesson about <em>when</em> a control is active, MFA methods aren't ranked by how strong the secret is, they're ranked by how many different tricks a human being can be fooled into cooperating with.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Multi-factor authentication (MFA)</strong>: requiring proof from two or more distinct factor categories before granting access.</li>\n<li><strong>Factor category</strong>: one of: knowledge (something you know), possession (something you have), inherence (something you are).</li>\n<li><strong>TOTP / HOTP</strong>: Time-based / HMAC-based One-Time Password; authenticator-app codes generated from a shared secret.</li>\n<li><strong>FIDO2 / WebAuthn</strong>: an open standard for hardware/platform security keys that cryptographically bind authentication to the requesting website domain, making it phishing-resistant.</li>\n<li><strong>SIM swapping</strong>: an attacker fraudulently transfers a victim's phone number to a attacker-controlled SIM card, intercepting SMS-based codes.</li>\n<li><strong>MFA fatigue (push bombing)</strong>: repeatedly sending push-approval prompts until the user approves one out of annoyance or confusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Phishing-resistant authentication</strong>: a factor that cannot be captured and replayed by a fake login page, such as a FIDO2/WebAuthn hardware key.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Entering two different passwords is two-factor authentication.\" → Both are \"something you know\", the same factor category twice. True MFA requires two <em>different</em> categories.</li>\n<li>\"SMS-based MFA is just as strong as an authenticator app or hardware key.\" → SMS codes travel through the mobile carrier network and are vulnerable to SIM swapping and relay/phishing attacks that authenticator apps avoid, and that hardware keys are specifically designed to resist.</li>\n<li>\"Approving a push notification always means the real user is logging in.\" → MFA fatigue attacks exploit the fact that a user may approve a prompt they did not initiate, just to stop repeated notifications; approval is not proof of intent unless the prompt shows context the user verifies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.2.A, 4.2.B</p>"},{"id":"4.2.c","kind":"pre","title":"Session security","topic":"4.2","href":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Authentication (checking a username and password) happens once, but a user typically stays\n\"logged in\" across many page loads and API calls. Re-entering a password on every click would be\nunusable.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Here's the mental model to hold before the lecture starts: a session token is a temporary password that gets reissued every time you log in, and whoever holds a copy of it <em>is</em> you, as far as the server is concerned. That single fact reframes everything else in this skill, hijacking isn't about breaking your real password at all, it's about stealing the token that already proves you logged in, which means every defense here is really about protecting a piece of data, not a secret you memorized.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Deleting the cookie on logout fully logs the user out.\" → False. If the server does not invalidate the session record, a copy of the token captured earlier (e.g., via network sniffing) remains valid and can still be replayed after the user thinks they logged out.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how session tokens keep a user authenticated across requests, identify session hijacking and session fixation from a described scenario, and recommend the specific control (secure cookie flag, timeout, or session regeneration) that mitigates each.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.2.c","kind":"post","title":"Session security","topic":"4.2","href":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The distinction the exam will test hardest is fixation versus hijacking, and the cleanest way to keep them straight is by timing: fixation plants a known ID <em>before</em> you ever log in and just waits for you to authenticate into it, while hijacking steals a live ID <em>after</em> you're already authenticated, which is exactly why they need different fixes. Session regeneration (a fresh ID issued at the moment of login) defeats fixation because the attacker's pre-planted ID never becomes valid; secure cookie flags and encrypted transport defeat hijacking because they close the specific paths, XSS reading JavaScript-accessible cookies, plaintext sniffing on public Wi-Fi, that let an attacker steal a token that already works. The detail most students miss, and that connects directly back to 4.2.b's MFA fatigue lesson, is that logout has to be a server-side action: deleting a cookie from your browser is cosmetic if the server-side session record is still alive, because a token an attacker copied five minutes ago is just as valid after you click \"logout\" as it was before, the button gave you the feeling of safety without the substance of it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Session</strong>: a server-side record that a specific browser has successfully authenticated.</li>\n<li><strong>Session token / session cookie</strong>: the random string identifying a session, sent by the browser on every request.</li>\n<li><strong>Session hijacking</strong>: stealing and reusing a victim's already-authenticated session token.</li>\n<li><strong>Session fixation</strong>: forcing a victim to authenticate under a session ID the attacker already controls.</li>\n<li><strong>Idle timeout</strong>: session expiration triggered by a period of no activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Absolute timeout</strong>: session expiration triggered after a fixed total duration.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure flag</strong>: cookie attribute restricting transmission to HTTPS only.</li>\n<li><strong>HttpOnly flag</strong>: cookie attribute hiding the cookie from JavaScript, blunting XSS theft.</li>\n<li><strong>SameSite</strong>: cookie attribute limiting cross-site sending, mitigating CSRF.</li>\n<li><strong>Session regeneration</strong>: issuing a fresh session ID at login / privilege change, defeating fixation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Deleting the cookie on logout fully logs the user out.\" → False. If the server does not invalidate the session record, a copy of the token captured earlier (e.g., via network sniffing) remains valid and can still be replayed after the user thinks they logged out.</li>\n<li>\"HTTPS alone makes sessions un-hijackable.\" → False. HTTPS protects the token in transit, but if the cookie is missing the HttpOnly flag, a successful XSS injection can still read it directly from the page's JavaScript context and send it to the attacker, no network sniffing required.</li>\n<li>\"Session fixation and session hijacking are the same attack.\" → False. Fixation plants a known ID <em>before</em> login; hijacking steals a live ID <em>after</em> login. The defenses differ: regeneration stops fixation, while secure flags and encrypted transport stop hijacking.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.2.B</p>"},{"id":"4.2.d","kind":"pre","title":"Least privilege and access control models","topic":"4.2","href":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.d/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.d/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every account, process, and service on a system needs <em>some</em> level of access to do its job, a\nhelp-desk technician needs to reset passwords, a database service needs to read certain tables, a\nweb server needs to write to a log directory. The <strong>principle of least privilege (PoLP)</strong> says\nthat every subject should be granted the <em>minimum</em> access required to perform its function, and\nnothing more.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Walk into this skill with a single sharp question ready: if this account gets phished tomorrow, what's the worst thing the attacker inherits? The principle of least privilege isn't a compliance checkbox, it's a bet that you can't stop every credential from eventually leaking, so the only lever left is making sure each individual leak is cheap. A marketing intern's read-only drive access being compromised is an inconvenience; the same intern's account having write access to production is a company-wide incident, and the only difference between those two outcomes is a permission that never should have been granted in the first place.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Giving admin rights to everyone is more convenient and low-risk if you trust your employees.\" → Trust does not shrink blast radius. A trusted employee's account can still be phished or infected by malware, and malware inherits the privileges of whoever runs it, over-provisioning turns any single compromise into an organization-wide one.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain the principle of least privilege, describe why over-privileged accounts increase risk, distinguish DAC, MAC, RBAC, and ABAC access control models, and recognize privilege creep and the controls that mitigate it.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.2.d","kind":"post","title":"Least privilege and access control models","topic":"4.2","href":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.d/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.2/4.2.d/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The four access-control models (DAC, MAC, RBAC, ABAC) are easiest to keep straight if you ask who's making the decision and when: DAC lets the resource owner decide, case by case, which is why it scales badly, permissions sprawl with nobody enforcing consistency. MAC decides centrally with fixed labels, trading flexibility for near-total resistance to insider misconfiguration. RBAC decides by role, which is why it dominates enterprise IT, changing someone's job means reassigning a role, not hand-editing dozens of permissions. ABAC decides dynamically, at the moment of the request, using context like time of day or device posture, trading simplicity for real-time responsiveness. Notice that privilege creep is really the same silent-decay problem you saw with configuration drift already waiting for you in 4.3.a, access that was correct on day one degrades into a liability because revoking permissions requires someone to notice and act, while granting them is just a routine part of onboarding. That's exactly why periodic access review, separation of duties, and just-in-time access all exist: not to get the permission model right once, but to keep re-proving it's still right as roles quietly change underneath it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Least privilege (PoLP)</strong>: grant a subject only the minimum access needed to perform its function, nothing more.</li>\n<li><strong>DAC (Discretionary Access Control)</strong>: resource owner decides and grants access; e.g. Unix file permissions.</li>\n<li><strong>MAC (Mandatory Access Control)</strong>: access decided centrally via fixed classification labels; e.g. government/military systems, SELinux.</li>\n<li><strong>RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)</strong>: permissions attached to roles; users assigned to roles. Most common model in enterprise IT.</li>\n<li><strong>ABAC (Attribute-Based Access Control)</strong>: access decided dynamically from attributes of user, resource, and environment.</li>\n<li><strong>Privilege creep</strong>: the silent accumulation of unnecessary access over time as roles change.</li>\n<li><strong>Separation of duties</strong>: structuring a sensitive process so no single person controls it end-to-end.</li>\n<li><strong>Access review</strong>: a periodic recertification where unused or unjustified access is revoked.</li>\n<li><strong>Just-in-time (JIT) access</strong>: temporary, time-bound elevated access granted only for the duration it is needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Giving admin rights to everyone is more convenient and low-risk if you trust your employees.\" → Trust does not shrink blast radius. A trusted employee's account can still be phished or infected by malware, and malware inherits the privileges of whoever runs it, over-provisioning turns any single compromise into an organization-wide one.</li>\n<li>\"RBAC and ABAC are the same thing.\" → RBAC assigns static permissions to a role and a user inherits that role's access; ABAC evaluates dynamic attributes (user, resource, environment) at the moment of the request. RBAC answers \"what role are you,\" ABAC answers \"do current conditions satisfy the policy.\"</li>\n<li>\"Once access is granted correctly, it stays correct.\" → Access correctness decays over time as people change roles (privilege creep). Least privilege requires ongoing periodic access review, not a one-time setup.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.2.B, 4.2.C</p>"},{"id":"4.3.a","kind":"pre","title":"Device hardening","topic":"4.3","href":"unit-4/4.3/4.3.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.3/4.3.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every device shipped from the factory arrives with a default configuration designed to maximize\ncompatibility and ease of setup, not security. Default administrator passwords are identical\nacross thousands of units. Unneeded services run because the manufacturer assumed the buyer might\nwant them.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The image worth carrying into this skill: every device ships from the factory optimized for the easiest possible setup, not for security, which means \"default configuration\" and \"maximally exploitable configuration\" are often the same thing. Hardening isn't adding new protection so much as it's <em>removing</em> invitations that were switched on by default, the open port nobody uses, the guest account nobody disabled, the admin password that's identical across ten thousand identical devices sitting on shelves. Before you can defend a device, you have to first stop advertising it as an easy target.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Only servers need hardening.\" → Every device with a network connection, laptops, printers, phones, cameras, has an attack surface and must be hardened.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Describe the core device-hardening controls, apply a hardening checklist to a sample configuration, and explain why reducing a device's attack surface lowers organizational risk.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.3.a","kind":"post","title":"Device hardening","topic":"4.3","href":"unit-4/4.3/4.3.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.3/4.3.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The four-part checklist, change defaults, remove what's unneeded, enable protective features, lock down accounts, is really least privilege from 4.2.d applied to a machine instead of a person, and it's worth seeing them as the same discipline wearing two hats: a device that only runs the services its job requires has a smaller blast radius for exactly the same reason an account with only the access it needs does. The idea that should stick longest, though, is configuration drift: hardening is not a one-time checklist you complete and move on from, it's a state that constantly decays as administrators install software, open a port \"temporarily,\" or reset something during troubleshooting, which is precisely why mature programs pair hardening with automated tools (Ansible, Puppet, Group Policy) that continuously re-check the live device against a known-good baseline. Keep that idea active through the rest of 4.3: hardening removes unnecessary risk <em>before</em> an incident, endpoint protection (4.3.b) catches malicious activity that a hardened device still lets through, and patching (4.3.c) closes the vulnerabilities that hardening alone can't touch because they're bugs in code the device actually needs to run.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Device hardening</strong>: the process of systematically removing or disabling unnecessary features, services, and accounts to reduce a device's attack surface before deployment.</li>\n<li><strong>Attack surface</strong>: the total set of points where an unauthorized user could attempt to interact with or exploit a system.</li>\n<li><strong>Default credentials</strong>: factory-set usernames and passwords that are identical across many units of the same device model and are publicly known.</li>\n<li><strong>Principle of least privilege</strong>: every account and process should operate with only the minimum permissions required for its legitimate function.</li>\n<li><strong>Configuration baseline</strong>: a documented record of the approved secure state of a device, used as a reference to detect and correct drift.</li>\n<li><strong>Configuration drift</strong>: the gradual accumulation of unauthorized or unintended changes to a device's configuration over time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Only servers need hardening.\" → Every device with a network connection, laptops, printers, phones, cameras, has an attack surface and must be hardened.</li>\n<li>\"Changing the password is enough.\" → Changing the default password is the single most important first step, but a hardened device also disables unneeded services, enables protective features, and restricts account privileges.</li>\n<li>\"Hardening is a one-time task.\" → Configuration drift means hardening must be monitored continuously; a hardened device today may have re-opened attack surfaces tomorrow.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.3.A</p>"},{"id":"4.3.b","kind":"pre","title":"Antivirus and endpoint protection","topic":"4.3","href":"unit-4/4.3/4.3.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.3/4.3.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Even on a fully hardened device, users must interact with files, websites, and external media. This interaction introduces the risk of malware, malicious software designed to disrupt, damage, or gain unauthorized access to a computer system. To mitigate this risk, security teams deploy antivirus and endpoint protection systems.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The frame to hold going in: a hardened device still has to let users open files, click links, and plug in that USB drive from the front office, hardening shrinks the target, it doesn't eliminate the need for something to be watching what happens <em>after</em> a user interacts with something risky. That's the job endpoint protection exists to do, and the single most important idea in this skill is the shift from asking \"does this file match something bad I've already seen\" to asking \"is this program currently doing something a legitimate program wouldn't do.\"</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Having an antivirus means I don't need a firewall.\" → Antivirus blocks malicious files and code execution, whereas a firewall regulates network traffic. You need both to protect against different attack vectors.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain the difference between signature-based and heuristic/behavioral malware detection, describe the function of host-based firewalls, and explain how endpoint protection platforms (EPP) safeguard devices in an enterprise.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.3.b","kind":"post","title":"Antivirus and endpoint protection","topic":"4.3","href":"unit-4/4.3/4.3.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.3/4.3.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> Signature-based and behavioral detection aren't competing approaches, they're a deliberate tradeoff pairing, and understanding <em>why</em> each one exists where it does matters more than memorizing their definitions: signatures are fast and nearly false-positive-free but entirely reactive, a brand-new or polymorphic variant sails right past a database built from what's already been seen, while behavioral analysis catches genuinely novel attacks by watching what a process <em>tries to do</em>, at the cost of being computationally heavier and more prone to flagging legitimate administrative tools as suspicious. That's exactly the same signature-versus-behavior tension you're about to meet again in EDR (4.4.a), just applied one layer up, from a single device to a security team's fleet-wide visibility. The host-based firewall piece deserves its own attention because it closes a gap signature and behavioral scanning both miss: even malware that successfully executes needs to <em>phone home</em> to a command-and-control server to be useful to an attacker, and blocking that outbound connection can neutralize a payload that already got past every other layer, a reminder that in security, stopping the attacker's exit is sometimes just as valuable as stopping their entry.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature-based detection</strong>: a malware detection method that matches files or memory patterns against a static database of known malware fingerprints.</li>\n<li><strong>Heuristic / Behavioral analysis</strong>: threat detection methods that analyze software code patterns or monitor active processes in real-time to identify suspicious actions, catching zero-day threats.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero-day threat</strong>: a computer threat or vulnerability that is unknown to the software developers or security community, meaning no signature or patch exists yet.</li>\n<li><strong>Host-based firewall</strong>: security software running on a single device that controls inbound and outbound network traffic based on predefined security rules.</li>\n<li><strong>Endpoint Protection Platform (EPP)</strong>: a centralized enterprise solution that deploys a security agent to all user devices to provide antivirus, firewall, and policy management from a single console.</li>\n<li><strong>False positive</strong>: an alert that incorrectly flags a benign file, process, or network packet as malicious.</li>\n<li><strong>Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR)</strong>: a tool that continuously records endpoint activity and gives responders tools to investigate, isolate, and remediate threats, complementing preventive antivirus.</li>\n<li><strong>Living-off-the-land</strong>: an attack technique that abuses legitimate, pre-installed system tools (e.g., PowerShell) instead of custom malware, to evade detection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Having an antivirus means I don't need a firewall.\" → Antivirus blocks malicious files and code execution, whereas a firewall regulates network traffic. You need both to protect against different attack vectors.</li>\n<li>\"Antivirus will catch 100% of malware.\" → No security control is perfect. Zero-day exploits and highly customized, targeted attacks can bypass signature-based and even heuristic detections. Defense-in-depth is always required.</li>\n<li>\"Disabling the antivirus makes my computer faster and is safe if I'm careful.\" → Modern malware can infect devices through compromised advertising networks on reputable sites (malvertising) or software updates, without direct user action. Speed gains are negligible compared to the risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.3.A, 4.3.B, 4.3.D</p>"},{"id":"4.3.c","kind":"pre","title":"Patching and regular updates","topic":"4.3","href":"unit-4/4.3/4.3.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.3/4.3.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Antivirus and firewalls (4.3.a, 4.3.b) defend a device from the outside, they watch for known-bad\nfiles and traffic. But many of the most damaging breaches in history did not require tricking a\nuser or evading a signature at all. They exploited a <strong>known, published vulnerability</strong> in\nsoftware that the victim organization had simply not yet fixed.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The sentence worth sitting with before the lecture: publishing a patch also publishes a roadmap of exactly what was broken, because attackers can reverse-engineer the diff between the old code and the new code to see precisely what the vendor just fixed. That single fact is why \"patch promptly\" isn't just good hygiene, it's a race that starts the moment the fix ships, and it reframes the whole atom around a genuinely surprising idea: the vulnerability becomes <em>more</em> dangerous, not less, in the hours right after it gets patched, because that's exactly when weaponized exploit code starts circulating against everyone who hasn't updated yet.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Zero-days are the main way organizations get breached.\" → Most real-world breaches exploit n-day vulnerabilities, known flaws with an available patch that simply was not applied in time. Zero-days are rare and expensive for attackers by comparison.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain why unpatched software is a top attack vector, describe how the vulnerability-disclosure-to-exploit timeline works, and prioritize which patches an organization should deploy first using severity, exploitation status, and exposure.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.3.c","kind":"post","title":"Patching and regular updates","topic":"4.3","href":"unit-4/4.3/4.3.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.3/4.3.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The zero-day versus n-day distinction is the one piece of vocabulary here worth never confusing, because it flips the popular narrative about breaches on its head: the dramatic, expensive zero-day, exploited before any fix exists, is actually rare, while the overwhelming majority of real breaches exploit n-day vulnerabilities, flaws with a perfectly good patch sitting unapplied for months. That should change how you read the \"why organizations delay patching\" section, compatibility risk, downtime, scale, legacy systems, not as excuses but as the genuine engineering costs that make the patch gap a real tradeoff rather than pure negligence, which is exactly why mature patch management runs as a formal process (inventory, prioritize, test, deploy, verify) rather than an instinct to patch everything instantly. Notice, too, that the CVSS severity score reappears almost unchanged as the \"weight\" concept in 4.4.c's compromise scoring, this course keeps reaching for the same idea, turning fuzzy risk into a number you can set a threshold against, because \"patch everything with equal urgency\" is exactly as unworkable here as \"defend against every threat equally\" was back in Unit 2's risk-management atom.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Patch</strong>: a vendor-released software update that fixes a bug, often including a security fix.</li>\n<li><strong>CVE (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures)</strong>: a public, unique identifier assigned to a disclosed vulnerability.</li>\n<li><strong>CVSS (Common Vulnerability Scoring System)</strong>: a 0-10 standardized severity score for a vulnerability, based on exploitability and impact.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero-day</strong>: a vulnerability being exploited before any patch exists.</li>\n<li><strong>N-day</strong>: a vulnerability for which a patch exists but has not yet been applied to the target.</li>\n<li><strong>Patch gap</strong>: the time between a patch's release and an organization actually deploying it.</li>\n<li><strong>Patch management</strong>: the formal process of inventorying, prioritizing, testing, deploying, and verifying patches across an organization's assets.</li>\n<li><strong>End-of-life (EOL) software</strong>: software no longer receiving vendor patches, requiring compensating controls instead.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Zero-days are the main way organizations get breached.\" → Most real-world breaches exploit n-day vulnerabilities, known flaws with an available patch that simply was not applied in time. Zero-days are rare and expensive for attackers by comparison.</li>\n<li>\"You should always apply every patch the instant it's released.\" → Untested patches carry real compatibility and downtime risk. Mature patch management tests and prioritizes by severity and exploitation status rather than patching reflexively, while still moving fast on critical, actively-exploited flaws.</li>\n<li>\"If a system still works fine, it doesn't need patching.\" → A system can function perfectly while still containing a serious, publicly known, unpatched vulnerability. Functionality and security posture are independent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.3.B, 4.3.C</p>"},{"id":"4.4.a","kind":"pre","title":"Endpoint detection and response (EDR)","topic":"4.4","href":"unit-4/4.4/4.4.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.4/4.4.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every device on a network, laptop, server, or workstation, is a potential entry point for an\nattacker. A firewall guards the boundary, but once malicious code is running inside a machine, a\nboundary control cannot see it. <strong>Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR)</strong> is the category of\nsecurity software that watches activity <em>on the device itself</em> rather than at the network edge.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The reframe to make before this lecture starts: a firewall is a guard standing at the front door, and EDR is a guard who already lives inside the building, the moment malicious code is actually running on a device, a boundary control can no longer see it, because the code isn't crossing the boundary anymore, it's already past it. Everything interesting about EDR follows from that one positional difference: it has to watch behavior happening <em>on</em> the machine, in real time, which is a fundamentally different job than inspecting traffic as it crosses a perimeter.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"EDR is just better antivirus.\" -&gt; Antivirus primarily checks files against known signatures at rest. EDR monitors continuous runtime behavior across process, file, network, and registry streams and can respond by isolating, killing, or quarantining, not just scanning.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Describe how an EDR platform collects telemetry from a device, explain how it classifies process and file activity as suspicious or clean, and identify the core actions an EDR takes when it detects a threat.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.4.a","kind":"post","title":"Endpoint detection and response (EDR)","topic":"4.4","href":"unit-4/4.4/4.4.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.4/4.4.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The Meridian scenario, Office spawning PowerShell, PowerShell making an unusual outbound connection, a new executable landing in a temp folder, is worth remembering as a worked example of \"living off the land,\" and it's the cleanest illustration in this unit of why signature-based defense (4.3.b) structurally cannot catch everything: PowerShell itself is completely legitimate software that ships with Windows, so no signature will ever flag it, and the only way to catch this attack chain is to notice the <em>sequence</em> is abnormal even though every individual step used trusted tools. That's the same behavioral-baseline logic from endpoint protection, just extended across four different telemetry streams, process, file, registry, network, instead of one, and it directly sets up the next two atoms: EDR gives you a live, single-device view, log analysis (4.4.b) extends that same pattern-recognition instinct across many systems and across time, and indicators of compromise (4.4.c) formalizes how you combine clues from both into a confident verdict. Keep the response half in mind too, collect-detect-respond in one agent is specifically what separates EDR from a SIEM (which correlates but can't act) and from antivirus (which detects but rarely isolates), and isolation is worth remembering precisely: it cuts network access to stop lateral movement, it does not wipe or destroy anything.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endpoint</strong>: any device (laptop, server, workstation) that is an origin or destination for network traffic and hosts a security agent.</li>\n<li><strong>EDR (Endpoint Detection and Response)</strong>: security software that collects device telemetry, detects malicious behavior using signatures and behavioral models, and can respond automatically to contain threats.</li>\n<li><strong>Telemetry</strong>: the stream of raw event data (process, file, network, registry events) that the EDR agent reports from the device.</li>\n<li><strong>Behavioral detection</strong>: flagging activity that deviates from a learned baseline, even when no matching signature exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Isolation</strong>: the EDR response action that cuts a device's network connections to prevent lateral movement while preserving remote analyst access.</li>\n<li><strong>Living off the land</strong>: attacker technique of using legitimate OS tools (PowerShell, WMI, certutil) to carry out malicious actions, making detection harder for signature-based tools.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"EDR is just better antivirus.\" -&gt; Antivirus primarily checks files against known signatures at rest. EDR monitors continuous runtime behavior across process, file, network, and registry streams and can respond by isolating, killing, or quarantining, not just scanning.</li>\n<li>\"If nothing alerts, the device is clean.\" -&gt; EDR relies on behavioral baselines and signatures that may not cover every possible attack. No detection tool guarantees completeness; layered defenses and regular threat-hunting are still required.</li>\n<li>\"Isolation means the device is wiped.\" -&gt; Isolation cuts network access but does not destroy data. The analyst can still connect to the isolated device through the EDR console to investigate and recover.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.4.A</p>"},{"id":"4.4.b","kind":"pre","title":"Log analysis","topic":"4.4","href":"unit-4/4.4/4.4.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.4/4.4.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every meaningful action a device or application takes can be recorded as a <strong>log entry</strong>, a\ntimestamped record of an event. A failed login, a file being opened, a firewall blocking a\nconnection, a new process starting: each of these can generate a line in a log file. Individually,\na single log line rarely proves an attack happened.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The habit worth building before this lecture: never judge a single log line. One failed login is background noise, everyone mistypes a password sometimes, and the entire skill this skill teaches is refusing to draw a conclusion until you've looked at <em>several</em> entries linked by the same user, the same IP, or a tight time window. A log analyst's job isn't reading logs, it's reading patterns that only exist once you stop looking at any one line in isolation.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A single failed login is proof of an attack.\" → One failed login is ordinary human error. Attacks are recognized through correlated patterns, multiple entries linked by user, IP, and tight time windows, not a single log line in isolation.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain what a security log records and why it matters, identify the common log sources security analysts review, and detect a brute-force login pattern by correlating multiple log entries over time.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.4.b","kind":"post","title":"Log analysis","topic":"4.4","href":"unit-4/4.4/4.4.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.4/4.4.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The Meridian scenario is worth remembering in exact structure, because it contains a trap the exam loves: six failed logins followed immediately by a success, from the same IP, in under a minute. Read the final line alone and it looks like someone finally got their password right, completely unremarkable. Read the whole sequence as a correlated unit and it's a textbook brute-force success. That gap between what one line suggests and what the sequence proves is the entire argument for correlation, and it's the same argument you'll meet again in 4.4.c when individual IOCs get combined into a compromise score, a single artifact is ambiguous, several artifacts pointing the same direction are not. The threshold-tuning discussion (5 failures in 60 seconds, not 2, not 50) deserves equal weight: it's the same false-positive-versus-false-negative tradeoff you saw in 4.3.b's behavioral detection, and \"alert fatigue\" is worth naming explicitly as a security failure in its own right, a detection rule tuned so sensitively that analysts start ignoring it is functionally the same as having no detection rule at all, just with extra noise and false confidence layered on top.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Log entry</strong>: a single timestamped record of an event (e.g., a login attempt, a blocked connection).</li>\n<li><strong>Authentication log</strong>: a log recording every login attempt, its result, source, and account.</li>\n<li><strong>Correlation</strong>: linking multiple log entries together (by user, IP, or time) to reveal a pattern that no single entry shows alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Brute-force attack</strong>: repeated, rapid login attempts (often automated) trying to guess a valid password.</li>\n<li><strong>Detection rule</strong>: a formal, repeatable condition (e.g., \"5+ failures in 60 seconds\") used to flag a pattern automatically.</li>\n<li><strong>SIEM (Security Information and Event Management)</strong>: a platform that ingests logs from many systems and applies correlation rules at scale to raise alerts.</li>\n<li><strong>Alert fatigue</strong>: the desensitization that occurs when detection thresholds are too sensitive and generate too many false alarms, causing analysts to start ignoring real alerts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A single failed login is proof of an attack.\" → One failed login is ordinary human error. Attacks are recognized through correlated patterns, multiple entries linked by user, IP, and tight time windows, not a single log line in isolation.</li>\n<li>\"More sensitive detection rules are always better.\" → Overly sensitive thresholds generate false positives that cause alert fatigue, where analysts start ignoring alerts altogether, potentially missing a real attack buried in the noise.</li>\n<li>\"Logs are only useful in real time.\" → Logs are equally critical after an incident, for forensic reconstruction of what an attacker did, especially once the attacker is no longer active on the system.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.4.B, 4.4.D</p>"},{"id":"4.4.c","kind":"pre","title":"Identifying compromised devices (indicators of compromise)","topic":"4.4","href":"unit-4/4.4/4.4.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.4/4.4.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> EDR (4.4.a) watches live behavior on a device, and log analysis (4.4.b) correlates events over\ntime. Both techniques ultimately produce the same kind of output: individual clues that something\nis wrong. An <strong>indicator of compromise (IOC)</strong> is a piece of forensic evidence, an artifact left\nbehind on a system or network, that suggests a device has been breached.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Carry this question into the lecture: if you found just <em>one</em> of these clues on a device, a new admin account, say, would you be confident it was an attack? Almost certainly not; an IT technician doing routine maintenance produces the exact same artifact. That ambiguity is the whole reason this skill exists: an indicator of compromise is evidence, not proof, and the skill being taught is exactly the same one from log analysis one atom ago, refusing to trust a single data point and instead asking what the <em>combination</em> of data points implies.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Any single suspicious artifact proves a device is compromised.\" → One IOC alone is usually ambiguous and may have an innocent explanation. Confident verdicts come from combining multiple independent IOCs, similar to correlating multiple log entries in 4.4.b.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Define an indicator of compromise (IOC), classify common IOC categories, and combine multiple IOCs on a single device into an overall compromise verdict using weighted scoring.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"4.4.c","kind":"post","title":"Identifying compromised devices (indicators of compromise)","topic":"4.4","href":"unit-4/4.4/4.4.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-4/4.4/4.4.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The Meridian scenario ties this entire unit together on purpose: a new unauthorized admin account (a 4.2.d least-privilege violation), a disabled antivirus service (defeating 4.3.b's endpoint protection), and an outbound connection to a known-malicious IP (the exact network-based IOC signature EDR watches for in 4.4.a), three artifacts that are individually explainable but collectively describe one coherent attack story: gain access, disable the tooling that might catch you, establish persistence, phone home. Weighted scoring is worth connecting explicitly back to CVSS from 4.3.c, because it's the same underlying move applied to a different question, instead of \"how severe is this vulnerability,\" it's \"how strongly does this specific artifact suggest compromise\", and both exist for the identical reason: fuzzy, instinctive risk judgment doesn't scale, but a number compared against a threshold does. Last, keep IOC and IOA cleanly separated, because it's a distinction about <em>timing</em>, not severity: an IOC is evidence a step already happened (a file with a <code>.locked</code> extension already sitting on disk), while an IOA tries to catch the attacker's technique while it's still unfolding (a process starting to enumerate files by extension, which is exactly what ransomware does in the seconds before encryption begins), and that's a fitting place to end Unit 4, because it's the same idea the whole unit has been building toward: the earlier in the attack chain you can reliably recognize what's happening, the more of the damage in 4.1's malware section never gets the chance to occur at all.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Indicator of compromise (IOC)</strong>: forensic evidence (a file, network connection, account, configuration change, or behavior) suggesting a device has been breached.</li>\n<li><strong>Persistence</strong>: an attacker technique for maintaining access to a compromised system even after the initial foothold is discovered or the system is rebooted.</li>\n<li><strong>Threat intelligence feed</strong>: a shared, continuously updated source of known-malicious indicators (hashes, IPs, domains) that organizations subscribe to.</li>\n<li><strong>Compromise score</strong>: a numeric value produced by summing the weights of every IOC observed on a device, used to classify overall risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Indicator of attack (IOA)</strong>: evidence of an attacker's intent or technique while an attack is actively in progress, as opposed to after-the-fact evidence (IOC).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Any single suspicious artifact proves a device is compromised.\" → One IOC alone is usually ambiguous and may have an innocent explanation. Confident verdicts come from combining multiple independent IOCs, similar to correlating multiple log entries in 4.4.b.</li>\n<li>\"All IOCs are equally serious.\" → A confirmed known-malicious file hash is far stronger evidence than a single failed login. Weighted scoring reflects that different IOC types carry very different levels of severity.</li>\n<li>\"IOCs and IOAs are the same thing.\" → An IOC is typically after-the-fact evidence that something already happened; an IOA focuses on catching the attacker's technique while it is still in progress.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 4.4.B, 4.4.C</p>"},{"id":"5.1.a","kind":"pre","title":"SQL Injection","topic":"5.1","href":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Web applications that store data almost always use a relational database behind the scenes. When a user logs in, searches for a product, or retrieves a record, the application typically builds a SQL query using whatever the user typed. SQL injection (SQLi) happens when an attacker types SQL syntax instead of ordinary data, and the application blindly pastes it into the query without checking.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Carry this framing into the lecture: SQL injection is not really a database problem, it's a language problem, the application asked a user for a piece of data and got back a piece of code instead, and never noticed the swap. Every payload family in this skill, from admin'-- to UNION SELECT, is the same trick wearing different clothes: sneak syntax into a slot the program assumed would only ever hold values. Once you see it that way, the fix stops being a filter you bolt on afterward and becomes obvious from the start, never let user data and query syntax share the same string in the first place.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Encoding the output prevents injection\", output encoding prevents cross-site scripting; SQL injection is an input parsing problem and requires parameterized queries or strict input validation, not output encoding.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Identify SQL injection attack patterns in web application input, explain how unsanitized queries expose database contents, and apply detection logic to flag malicious parameter strings.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.1.a","kind":"post","title":"SQL Injection","topic":"5.1","href":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The throughline worth keeping from this skill is the gap between detecting and preventing. The lesson spends real time on payload signatures because a defender scanning traffic needs to recognize SQLi in the wild, but it is explicit that pattern-matching a WAF against known strings is a second-line control, not the fix, an attacker who varies capitalization, encoding, or comment style slips past a signature list that a parameterized query would have made irrelevant regardless of how the payload was spelled. That distinction, detect versus structurally prevent, is going to recur constantly for the rest of this unit, and it is worth pre-loading now: 5.1.b's escaping, 5.2's authorization checks, and 5.5's parameterized queries are all examples of closing the door at the level where the trust boundary actually lives, rather than posting a guard in front of it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SQL injection</strong>: an attack that inserts SQL syntax into application input fields so the database executes attacker-controlled commands</li>\n<li><strong>parameterized query</strong>: a query template with typed placeholders for data values, preventing user input from being parsed as SQL</li>\n<li><strong>UNION SELECT</strong>: a SQL construct that appends a second SELECT to a query; used in injection to extract data from other tables</li>\n<li><strong>comment truncation</strong>: using SQL comment characters (<code>--</code> or <code>/* */</code>) in an injected payload to neutralize the rest of the intended query</li>\n<li><strong>blind injection</strong>: an injection technique used when the application returns no visible data; time delays or boolean responses leak information indirectly</li>\n<li><strong>WAF (Web Application Firewall)</strong>: an application-layer (layer 7) filter that inspects HTTP traffic for known attack patterns including SQLi payloads</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Encoding the output prevents injection\", output encoding prevents cross-site scripting; SQL injection is an input parsing problem and requires parameterized queries or strict input validation, not output encoding.</li>\n<li>\"Only login forms are vulnerable\", any page that uses user-supplied data to build a query (search boxes, URL parameters, HTTP headers, JSON fields) can be an injection point.</li>\n<li>\"A WAF is enough\", WAFs block known signatures but cannot catch all variants; parameterized queries must be the primary control.</li>\n<li>\"Attackers need to know the database schema\", error messages and blind-injection techniques allow schema enumeration; the attacker discovers the structure as part of the attack.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.1.A, 5.1.B</p>"},{"id":"5.1.b","kind":"pre","title":"Cross-site scripting (XSS)","topic":"5.1","href":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> A web application usually takes input from one user (a comment, a search term, a profile field)\nand displays it back, to that user, to an admin, or to every visitor. <strong>Cross-site scripting\n(XSS)</strong> happens when an application includes untrusted input in a page it sends to a browser\n<em>without properly neutralizing it first</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The mental model to bring into this lecture: XSS is SQL injection's twin, just aimed at the browser instead of the database. In both cases the root failure is identical, data and code sharing one channel, so a program that expected to receive text ends up executing instructions instead. Once you already understand that a database can't tell apart \"the string admin'--\" from \"a SQL comment,\" it should feel natural that a browser can't tell apart \"the string <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>\" from \"an instruction to run JavaScript.\" The specific fix differs, escaping output for HTML instead of parameterizing a query, but the diagnosis is the same question asked in a new context: where did code and data get mixed?</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Filtering <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags is enough.\" → Attackers use countless other vectors (<code>onerror=</code>, <code>javascript:</code> URLs, SVG, <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tags), deny-listing tags is easily bypassed; escaping output is the reliable fix.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how cross-site scripting (XSS) lets an attacker inject and run malicious script in another user's browser, identify the three main XSS variants, and recognize the input-handling mistakes that make an application vulnerable.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.1.b","kind":"post","title":"Cross-site scripting (XSS)","topic":"5.1","href":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The detail worth remembering longest here is that the three XSS variants are really differentiated by one question: does the malicious payload have to survive a trip to the server, or not? Reflected XSS needs the victim to click something, because the payload lives only in that one request. Stored XSS is scarier precisely because it removes that requirement, once it's saved in a comment or profile field, every visitor including an administrator is compromised just by loading the page, no phishing step needed. DOM-based XSS removes the server from the picture entirely, which is exactly why a server-side filter can look completely clean while the page is still vulnerable. That's the exam trap: students who memorize \"XSS = unescaped input\" without sorting by <em>where</em> the unescaped step happens will misclassify DOM-based cases as safe because the server-side log shows nothing wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cross-site scripting (XSS)</strong>: injecting attacker-controlled script into a page viewed by other users, by exploiting unescaped output of untrusted input.</li>\n<li><strong>Reflected XSS</strong>: payload delivered via the request (e.g., URL parameter) and echoed immediately in the response; requires tricking the victim into a crafted link.</li>\n<li><strong>Stored XSS</strong>: payload saved server-side and served to every subsequent viewer.</li>\n<li><strong>DOM-based XSS</strong>: payload never reaches the server; client-side script writes untrusted data into an unsafe DOM sink.</li>\n<li><strong>Output encoding / escaping</strong>: converting special characters (<code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>\"</code>, <code>'</code>, <code>&amp;</code>) to their literal-text equivalents before placing data into HTML.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Filtering <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags is enough.\" → Attackers use countless other vectors (<code>onerror=</code>, <code>javascript:</code> URLs, SVG, <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tags), deny-listing tags is easily bypassed; escaping output is the reliable fix.</li>\n<li>\"XSS only matters if the attacker can guess a password.\" → XSS runs in the <em>victim's already authenticated</em> browser session, no password is needed, it hijacks trust that already exists.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.1.A, 5.1.B</p>"},{"id":"5.1.c","kind":"pre","title":"API vulnerabilities","topic":"5.1","href":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> An <strong>API (application programming interface)</strong> is the set of URLs, request formats, and response\nformats a client application uses to talk to a server. Modern apps, mobile, web, IoT, are almost\nentirely built on APIs: the mobile banking app calls <code>GET /api/accounts/482</code> to show your balance,\na shopping site calls <code>GET /api/orders/9931</code> to show your order status.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Hold onto this distinction walking in: authentication answers \"who are you,\" authorization answers \"what are you allowed to touch\", and BOLA exists in the gap between an API asking the first question and forgetting to ask the second. A valid login token is not a blank check; it's proof of identity, and every single request still needs its own separate check of whether <em>this</em> identity is allowed to touch <em>this</em> specific object. That's a genuinely different mental habit than the input-sanitization mindset from 5.1.a/b, here the data going in is perfectly well-formed, the attacker just changed a number in a URL, so no amount of escaping or query parameterization would have caught it.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"If the request needs a valid login token, it's secure.\" → Authentication only proves identity; BOLA happens precisely when authorization (per-object permission) is never checked afterward.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how broken object-level authorization (BOLA/IDOR) and excessive-data-exposure flaws let an attacker abuse an application programming interface (API) to access data or objects they should not be able to reach, and can identify the missing check that causes it.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.1.c","kind":"post","title":"API vulnerabilities","topic":"5.1","href":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The idea worth keeping past this lecture is that BOLA is what happens when a UI's convenience gets mistaken for a security boundary. Hiding the \"view account 483\" button for a user who's logged in as account 482 feels like it should be protective, but an attacker who calls the API directly with curl never sees that button in the first place, the missing check was never really enforced, only politely suggested by the interface. That's the same lesson 5.5.a will drive home about client-side validation being worthless for security, just applied one layer earlier: whatever the client shows or hides is a UX decision, and every security-relevant decision has to be re-verified on the server, on every request, with no exceptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>API (application programming interface)</strong>: the defined set of endpoints/requests a client uses to interact with a server's data and functionality.</li>\n<li><strong>Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA) / IDOR</strong>: an API accepts a client-supplied object ID and returns/modifies that object without verifying the caller is authorized for that specific object.</li>\n<li><strong>Authentication vs. authorization</strong>: authentication proves <em>who you are</em>; authorization checks <em>what that identity is allowed to do/see</em>. BOLA is an authorization failure, not an authentication failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Excessive data exposure</strong>: an endpoint returns more fields/data than the client needs, relying on the front-end (not the server) to hide sensitive fields.</li>\n<li><strong>Broken function-level authorization</strong>: an endpoint intended for a privileged role is reachable by any authenticated user because the server never checks role/permission.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"If the request needs a valid login token, it's secure.\" → Authentication only proves identity; BOLA happens precisely when authorization (per-object permission) is never checked afterward.</li>\n<li>\"Hiding a button in the UI prevents unauthorized access.\" → Attackers call the API directly with tools like curl/Postman; hiding a button does not stop a raw HTTP request.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.1.B</p>"},{"id":"5.1.d","kind":"pre","title":"Data breaches and their impact","topic":"5.1","href":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.d/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.d/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> A <strong>data breach</strong> is any incident in which sensitive, protected, or confidential data is accessed,\ndisclosed, or exfiltrated by someone who is not authorized to have it.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The habit worth building before this lecture: stop asking \"did a breach happen\" and start asking \"how bad is this specific breach,\" because the AP framework wants you to reason quantitatively, not just spot the incident. Sensitivity, scale, and time-to-detect are three independent dials, and a breach can be severe by maxing out just one of them, forty leaked health records can outrank four million leaked newsletter emails even though the second number is a thousand times bigger. That's the same risk-equals-likelihood-times-impact logic from way back in Unit 2, just now applied to data instead of physical assets.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A breach only matters if passwords were stolen.\" → Exposure of PII, financial, or health data alone (without passwords) can enable identity theft and fraud and often carries stronger legal notification obligations.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain what a data breach is, identify the categories of data whose exposure causes the most harm, and estimate the severity of a breach from the type of data exposed, the number of records affected, and how quickly it was detected/contained.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.1.d","kind":"post","title":"Data breaches and their impact","topic":"5.1","href":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.d/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.1/5.1.d/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The connective tissue worth noticing is that this skill is the unifying finish line for everything in 5.1: SQL injection, XSS, and BOLA are three different <em>causes</em>, and a data breach is the <em>consequence</em> they all funnel toward, alongside plenty of non-technical causes like a lost laptop or a misconfigured bucket. That's worth sitting with, because it means the attack technique and the resulting harm are separate axes you have to reason about independently, the same breach severity can follow from a highly technical SQLi dump or a simple human mistake, and the response arc (contain, assess, notify, remediate) doesn't change based on how sophisticated the entry point was. Keep the time-to-detect factor especially close, because it's the bridge into 5.6: everything this unit builds toward, logging, DLP, evidence analysis, exists specifically to shrink that number before it becomes the deciding factor in how bad a breach turns out to be.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Data breach</strong>: unauthorized access, disclosure, or exfiltration of sensitive/protected data.</li>\n<li><strong>PII (personally identifiable information)</strong>: data that can identify a specific individual (name, SSN, address, date of birth).</li>\n<li><strong>PHI (protected health information)</strong>: health-related records protected by regulation (e.g., HIPAA in the U.S.).</li>\n<li><strong>Breach notification</strong>: the legal/regulatory requirement to inform affected individuals and authorities within a defined window after a confirmed breach.</li>\n<li><strong>Time to detect / contain</strong>: the elapsed time between a breach starting and it being discovered/stopped; a major driver of total breach impact.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A breach only matters if passwords were stolen.\" → Exposure of PII, financial, or health data alone (without passwords) can enable identity theft and fraud and often carries stronger legal notification obligations.</li>\n<li>\"Small breaches don't need a formal response.\" → Even a small breach of highly sensitive data (e.g., 40 patients' health records) can be more severe than a large breach of low-sensitivity data (e.g., 4 million newsletter emails); severity depends on data type and scale together.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.1.C</p>"},{"id":"5.2.a","kind":"pre","title":"Access control models (RBAC, DAC, MAC)","topic":"5.2","href":"unit-5/5.2/5.2.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.2/5.2.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every application stores data that should not be readable or modifiable by everyone. An\norganization's payroll file should be visible to HR managers, not to interns. A classified\ngovernment document should only reach employees with the correct clearance.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The frame to carry in: none of DAC, MAC, or RBAC is \"the secure one\", they're three different answers to \"who gets to decide access,\" and the right answer depends entirely on whether you trust individual owners, need centrally enforced rules that nobody can override, or need something that scales past a handful of people. Resist the urge to rank them by strength; the AP exam is testing whether you can match a scenario to a model, not whether you can recite that MAC is \"the most secure.\" A consumer photo-sharing app that used MAC would be unusable, and a intelligence agency that used DAC would be reckless, same three tools, opposite right answers.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"DAC is weak, so only MAC is secure.\", Each model fits different contexts. MAC overhead is impractical for consumer software; DAC is appropriate when owners are trusted to manage their own data correctly.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain what access control is, distinguish between the three primary access control models (DAC, MAC, RBAC), and select the appropriate model for a given organizational scenario.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.2.a","kind":"post","title":"Access control models (RBAC, DAC, MAC)","topic":"5.2","href":"unit-5/5.2/5.2.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.2/5.2.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The concept worth keeping longest is least privilege as the thread that runs underneath all three models rather than being a fourth option. DAC, MAC, and RBAC are just different <em>mechanisms</em> for granting access; least privilege is the <em>design principle</em> that tells you how narrowly to grant it regardless of which mechanism you're using. That distinction is exactly what the exam-trap misconception in this skill is pointing at, an RBAC system with one bloated \"superuser\" role is still RBAC, it just violates least privilege badly, which means the model you chose and the discipline you applied inside it are two separate things you have to evaluate separately. Hold onto that pairing, because 5.2.b spends its entire atom on exactly this gap: how a perfectly reasonable role structure quietly drifts into a least-privilege violation over time.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Access control</strong>: the set of policies and mechanisms that determine which subjects can perform which actions on which objects.</li>\n<li><strong>Subject</strong>: the entity requesting access (user, process, service).</li>\n<li><strong>Object</strong>: the resource being protected (file, database, endpoint).</li>\n<li><strong>DAC (Discretionary Access Control)</strong>: owner-controlled access; flexible but inconsistent.</li>\n<li><strong>MAC (Mandatory Access Control)</strong>: centrally enforced label-based access; strong but rigid.</li>\n<li><strong>RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)</strong>: permission-via-role assignment; scalable for organizations.</li>\n<li><strong>Least privilege</strong>: granting only the minimum access necessary for a subject to function.</li>\n<li><strong>Role</strong>: a named collection of permissions assigned to users as a unit.</li>\n<li><strong>Label / classification</strong>: a MAC tag attached to subjects and objects (e.g., UNCLASSIFIED, SECRET).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"DAC is weak, so only MAC is secure.\", Each model fits different contexts. MAC overhead is impractical for consumer software; DAC is appropriate when owners are trusted to manage their own data correctly.</li>\n<li>\"RBAC and least privilege are the same thing.\", RBAC is a mechanism; least privilege is a design principle. RBAC supports least privilege only if roles are defined narrowly. An RBAC system with an over-broad \"superuser\" role violates least privilege.</li>\n<li>\"Once an access model is chosen, it handles everything automatically.\", Models define the structure; administrators must still configure policies correctly. Misconfigured RBAC (too many permissions in a role) is a common real-world vulnerability.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.2.A, 5.2.D</p>"},{"id":"5.2.b","kind":"pre","title":"Roles, permissions, and least privilege","topic":"5.2","href":"unit-5/5.2/5.2.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.2/5.2.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> The previous atom (5.2.a) introduced the three access control models. This skill goes one level\ndeeper into the model organizations use most: <strong>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)</strong>, and the design\nprinciple that should guide every role you define, <strong>least privilege</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The asymmetry worth internalizing before this lecture: under-granting access is loud (someone complains immediately) and over-granting is silent (nothing breaks, nobody notices), and that single asymmetry is the entire engine behind privilege creep. It's not that organizations decide to be reckless with permissions; it's that the feedback loop is broken in exactly one direction, so access accumulates by default unless someone deliberately fights against it. Once you see privilege creep as a natural consequence of which errors get noticed rather than a failure of vigilance, the fix, scheduled role recertification, stops looking like bureaucratic overhead and starts looking like the only thing capable of counteracting a bias that's baked into how humans notice problems.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Giving extra access 'just in case' is harmless if it's never used.\" → Unused excess permission is exactly what increases blast radius if that account is ever compromised, it is a silent risk, not a neutral one.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how permissions are bundled into roles, apply the principle of least privilege to evaluate whether a role assignment is over-permissioned, and identify the risks of privilege creep and excessive standing access.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.2.b","kind":"post","title":"Roles, permissions, and least privilege","topic":"5.2","href":"unit-5/5.2/5.2.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.2/5.2.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The phrase worth keeping is blast radius, because it reframes why least privilege matters in a way that connects straight back to 5.2.a and forward to 5.2.c. A role isn't just a convenience for onboarding new hires, it's a pre-committed answer to \"how much damage does one phished account cause,\" decided in advance, before any attack happens. A tightly scoped support-agent role turns a phishing success into a contained incident; a role padded with \"just in case\" permissions turns the exact same phishing email into a much larger breach. That's not a hypothetical, it's literally the mechanism 5.2.c is about to walk through in detail: lateral movement is what happens when blast radius wasn't actually contained, and every silent excess permission this skill warns about is a potential hop in that chain.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Permission</strong>: a single narrow grant to perform one action on one resource type.</li>\n<li><strong>Role</strong>: a named bundle of permissions assigned to users as a unit (RBAC).</li>\n<li><strong>Least privilege</strong>: granting a subject only the permissions it needs, nothing more.</li>\n<li><strong>Privilege creep</strong>: the gradual, unnoticed accumulation of unnecessary permissions over time.</li>\n<li><strong>Role review / recertification</strong>: periodic re-confirmation that role assignments are still justified, used to counteract privilege creep.</li>\n<li><strong>Separation of duties</strong>: splitting a sensitive process across multiple roles so no single role can complete it alone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Giving extra access 'just in case' is harmless if it's never used.\" → Unused excess permission is exactly what increases blast radius if that account is ever compromised, it is a silent risk, not a neutral one.</li>\n<li>\"Once a role is set up correctly, it stays correct.\" → Job functions and org structure change continuously; without periodic review, roles drift away from least privilege via creep.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.2.A, 5.2.B</p>"},{"id":"5.2.c","kind":"pre","title":"Credential theft prevention and lateral movement","topic":"5.2","href":"unit-5/5.2/5.2.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.2/5.2.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Most large breaches do not end where they begin. An attacker rarely gets everything they want from\nthe very first account they compromise, a phished marketing employee's laptop, for instance,\nusually has no access to the finance database or the domain controller.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The frame worth walking in with: almost no breach ends where it starts, so the interesting security question is rarely \"how did they get in\" but \"how far did they get once they were in.\" A phished marketing laptop is a nuisance by itself; it only becomes a catastrophe if it can reach the finance database from there. That reframes least privilege and network segmentation from 5.2.a/b, they're not abstract hygiene, they're literally the walls that determine whether an attacker's first foothold stays a foothold or becomes the whole building.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A strong password alone stops credential theft.\" → Many lateral-movement techniques replay a stolen hash or ticket without ever needing the plaintext password, MFA and hygiene practices matter more than password strength alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how stolen credentials enable an attacker to move laterally across a network, identify the detection signals that indicate lateral movement is happening, and describe the access-control practices (MFA, credential hygiene, network segmentation) that limit its spread.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.2.c","kind":"post","title":"Credential theft prevention and lateral movement","topic":"5.2","href":"unit-5/5.2/5.2.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.2/5.2.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The detail worth remembering past this lecture is pass-the-hash: an attacker moving laterally frequently never needs your actual password at all, because many authentication protocols will accept a stolen hash or ticket as proof of identity just as readily as the plaintext would have been. That single fact should reshape how you think about password strength, a 20-character unguessable password does nothing to stop an attacker who scraped its hash straight out of memory on a compromised machine and replayed it. This is exactly why MFA earns its place as the standout defense in this skill: it's the one control that breaks the assumption underlying every credential-theft technique here, because a stolen credential, hashed, plaintext, or ticketed, is worthless without the second factor. Keep this skill in mind through the rest of the unit as the connective tissue to Unit 4's detection material: the behavioral signals described here (unusual host counts, atypical hours, first-time admin-tool use) are the same anomaly-detection logic as 5.6's log analysis, just applied to authentication events specifically.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lateral movement</strong>: an attacker using a compromised account/host to reach additional systems within a network after an initial foothold.</li>\n<li><strong>Pass-the-hash / pass-the-ticket</strong>: authenticating using a stolen password hash or session ticket without ever knowing the plaintext password.</li>\n<li><strong>Privilege escalation</strong>: increasing the level of access an attacker holds, often paired with lateral movement toward higher-value accounts/systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Multi-factor authentication (MFA)</strong>: requiring a second proof of identity beyond a password, which neutralizes most stolen-credential replay attempts.</li>\n<li><strong>Network segmentation</strong>: dividing a network so that compromise of one segment does not grant direct reach into another (e.g., isolating finance systems from general workstations).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A strong password alone stops credential theft.\" → Many lateral-movement techniques replay a stolen hash or ticket without ever needing the plaintext password, MFA and hygiene practices matter more than password strength alone.</li>\n<li>\"Lateral movement is only detectable after the fact.\" → Behavioral signals (unusual host counts, atypical hours, first-time admin-tool use) can flag lateral movement while it is in progress, which is exactly what the detection tooling covered in Unit 4 is built to surface.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.2.B, 5.2.C</p>"},{"id":"5.3.a","kind":"pre","title":"Encryption vs. hashing","topic":"5.3","href":"unit-5/5.3/5.3.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.3/5.3.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> When a system needs to protect stored data, engineers reach for two very different tools:\nencryption and hashing. On the surface both produce an unreadable-looking blob of bytes from\nreadable input. Below the surface they have opposite goals, and choosing the wrong one is a\ncommon and costly mistake.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The single question worth carrying into this lecture: do you ever need the original value back? If yes, encrypt. If no, hash. That one question does almost all the work in this skill, and it's worth testing against every scenario you meet from here forward, a payroll system needs to read salaries back out, so it encrypts; a login system never legitimately needs to see your actual password again, only whether what you typed matches, so it hashes. Confusing the two isn't a minor mistake, it's storing the <em>wrong kind of secret</em> for the job, and the cost of getting it backwards (plaintext-recoverable passwords sitting in a breached database) is exactly the kind of high-severity data breach 5.1.d taught you to score.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"I can hash a file and recover it later.\" → Hashing is one-way; the digest cannot be reversed.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Distinguish encryption from hashing, explain the one-way property that makes them suited for different jobs, and choose the correct control when a scenario requires either confidentiality or integrity verification.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.3.a","kind":"post","title":"Encryption vs. hashing","topic":"5.3","href":"unit-5/5.3/5.3.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.3/5.3.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The idea worth keeping longest is the avalanche effect and what it actually buys you: a hash function is designed so that two inputs differing by a single character produce digests that look nothing alike, which is what makes a digest useful as a tripwire for even the tiniest tampering. Hold that property in your head, because 5.3.c is going to build its entire argument for HMAC on top of it, and 5.4.c's digital signatures reuse the exact same property one layer up the stack. Also keep the salting story close, it's the clearest illustration in this unit of a general principle that recurs everywhere in cryptography: an attacker doesn't need to break your algorithm if they can out-plan you, and a rainbow table is just precomputation defeating a system that never varied its inputs. The fix is never \"a stronger hash function,\" it's \"stop giving the attacker a predictable target to precompute against.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Encryption</strong>: a reversible transformation of data using a key; protects confidentiality.</li>\n<li><strong>Decryption</strong>: the reverse of encryption; requires the correct key.</li>\n<li><strong>Hash function</strong>: a one-way function that maps any input to a fixed-size digest.</li>\n<li><strong>Digest</strong>: the fixed-size output of a hash function (also called a hash or checksum).</li>\n<li><strong>Salt</strong>: a random value mixed into input before hashing to defeat precomputed table attacks.</li>\n<li><strong>Rainbow table</strong>: a precomputed table mapping common inputs to their digests, used for hash cracking.</li>\n<li><strong>Avalanche effect</strong>: property where a tiny input change produces a drastically different digest.</li>\n<li><strong>Collision</strong>: two distinct inputs that produce the same digest; a fatal weakness in a hash function.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"I can hash a file and recover it later.\" → Hashing is one-way; the digest cannot be reversed.</li>\n<li>\"Encoding (Base64) is a form of encryption.\" → Encoding is a reversible format change with no key; it provides zero confidentiality.</li>\n<li>\"MD5 is fine for passwords.\" → MD5 has known collision weaknesses and is very fast to brute-force; use bcrypt/scrypt/Argon2 for passwords.</li>\n<li>\"Encryption and hashing both protect the same thing.\" → Encryption protects confidentiality (you need the original back); hashing protects integrity/authentication (you never need the original back).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.3.A</p>"},{"id":"5.3.b","kind":"pre","title":"Symmetric encryption and key management","topic":"5.3","href":"unit-5/5.3/5.3.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.3/5.3.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> When organizations need to protect the confidentiality of stored data (data-at-rest), they use symmetric cryptography. <strong>Symmetric encryption</strong> uses a single secret key for both encryption and decryption. Because the same key does both jobs, it must be kept secret; if an unauthorized party obtains the key, the protection is completely lost.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The image worth holding onto before this lecture: ECB mode is what happens when you encrypt each block of a message completely independently, and independence is exactly the problem, identical plaintext blocks produce identical ciphertext blocks, so the <em>shape</em> of your data leaks straight through the encryption, like tracing a photograph through frosted glass. That single image (the ECB-encrypted bitmap where you can still see the outline of the picture) is worth more than the definition, because it makes viscerally clear that \"encrypted\" is not a binary property, a cipher can be mathematically unbreakable and still leak everything that matters if it's chained together wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Hardcoding a key in a private Git repository is safe.\" → Private repositories can be compromised, and any developer with clone access can copy the key. Keys should be stored in environment variables or secrets managers.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain the mechanics of symmetric encryption, differentiate between ECB and secure modes of operation (like CBC and GCM), and describe the key lifecycle management principles necessary to protect keys from compromise.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.3.b","kind":"post","title":"Symmetric encryption and key management","topic":"5.3","href":"unit-5/5.3/5.3.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.3/5.3.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The concept worth keeping past this lecture is envelope encryption, because it's the practical answer to a problem you'd hit the moment you tried to deploy any of this at scale: you can't re-encrypt a terabyte database every time you rotate a key, so instead you rotate a small key that only protects other keys. DEK encrypts the data, KEK encrypts the DEK, and the expensive operation (touching the actual data) never has to happen during rotation, only the cheap operation (re-wrapping a tiny key) does. That's a pattern worth generalizing: whenever a system needs frequent rotation of something expensive to change, look for a cheaper intermediary you can rotate instead. And notice the throughline back to 5.2's least privilege: an HSM that never releases a key in plaintext is least privilege applied to the key itself, even the system using the key never actually holds it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Symmetric encryption</strong>: a cryptographic system where the same key is used for both encryption and decryption.</li>\n<li><strong>Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)</strong>: the standard symmetric block cipher used globally, supporting 128, 192, and 256-bit keys.</li>\n<li><strong>Block cipher</strong>: a cipher that encrypts data in fixed-size blocks (e.g., 128 bits for AES).</li>\n<li><strong>Mode of operation</strong>: an algorithm that defines how a block cipher encrypts data larger than a single block.</li>\n<li><strong>Initialization Vector (IV)</strong>: a random block of data used to seed the first block of encryption in modes like CBC, ensuring unique ciphertexts.</li>\n<li><strong>CSPRNG</strong>: Cryptographically Secure Pseudo-Random Number Generator; a generator that produces unpredictable numbers suitable for cryptography.</li>\n<li><strong>Key wrapping</strong>: the process of encrypting a Data Encryption Key (DEK) with a Key Encryption Key (KEK) for secure storage.</li>\n<li><strong>HSM (Hardware Security Module)</strong>: a physical device designed to securely generate, store, and manage cryptographic keys.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Hardcoding a key in a private Git repository is safe.\" → Private repositories can be compromised, and any developer with clone access can copy the key. Keys should be stored in environment variables or secrets managers.</li>\n<li>\"Reusing an IV is fine as long as the key is secret.\" → Reusing an IV allows attackers to perform frequency analysis or detect patterns, undermining confidentiality.</li>\n<li>\"AES-ECB is secure because AES is strong.\" → Even with a strong cipher like AES, ECB mode encrypts identical plaintext blocks into identical ciphertext blocks, exposing data patterns.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.3.A, 5.3.B</p>"},{"id":"5.3.c","kind":"pre","title":"Integrity and authenticity (HMAC)","topic":"5.3","href":"unit-5/5.3/5.3.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.3/5.3.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> 5.3.a established that a hash lets you detect whether data changed: store a digest, recompute\nit later, and compare. That is enough when the only threat is accidental corruption, a bad\ndisk sector, a dropped byte in transit. It is not enough when the threat is an intelligent\nattacker who can intercept and rewrite the data.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The gap worth naming before this lecture starts: 5.3.a taught you that a hash detects tampering, but only if the attacker can't also update the hash after they tamper, and a plain hash is public and keyless, so any attacker who can rewrite your message can just as easily recompute a matching digest for their rewritten version. That's not a hash function being broken; it's a hash function doing exactly what it was designed to do, applied in a context where the design assumption (an untampered channel for the digest) silently failed. HMAC exists because \"the data matches its digest\" and \"the data came from someone who knew a secret\" are two completely different claims, and only the second one survives an active attacker.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A matching hash proves the message wasn't tampered with.\" → Only if the attacker could not also recompute the hash after tampering. A plain, keyless hash gives no such guarantee once an attacker can rewrite both the message and its accompanying hash.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain why a plain hash cannot prove who sent a message, describe how HMAC combines a secret key with a hash function to provide both integrity and authenticity, and classify a given integrity-check design as secure or vulnerable to tampering.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.3.c","kind":"post","title":"Integrity and authenticity (HMAC)","topic":"5.3","href":"unit-5/5.3/5.3.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.3/5.3.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The detail worth remembering longest is why HMAC doesn't just do the obvious thing, hash the secret and message concatenated together, and it comes down to length-extension attacks on Merkle-Damgard hashes like SHA-256: the digest exposes exactly the internal state an attacker needs to keep hashing from, letting them extend a message and produce a valid tag for it without ever learning the key. HMAC's double-nested construction specifically closes that leak. You don't need the ipad/opad constants memorized, but you should walk away able to state precisely what integrity means versus what authenticity means, because that distinction is the whole atom in miniature, and it's the exact gap that 5.4.c's digital signatures close one level further, HMAC proves authenticity to anyone who shares the secret, but can't prove <em>which</em> of the two secret-holders actually sent a message; a signature, built on an asymmetric key pair instead of one shared secret, finally closes that last gap too.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MAC (Message Authentication Code)</strong>: a keyed hash/tag that proves both integrity and authenticity.</li>\n<li><strong>HMAC</strong>: the standardized (NIST FIPS 198-1) hash-based MAC construction using nested, keyed hashing.</li>\n<li><strong>Tag</strong>: the output of a MAC/HMAC computation, compared during verification (analogous to a digest, but keyed).</li>\n<li><strong>Integrity</strong>: assurance that data was not altered.</li>\n<li><strong>Authenticity</strong>: assurance that data really came from the claimed, key-holding sender.</li>\n<li><strong>Length-extension attack</strong>: exploiting a Merkle-Damgard hash's exposed internal state to extend <code>H(secret||message)</code> without knowing the secret.</li>\n<li><strong>ipad / opad</strong>: fixed padding constants XORed with the key to derive HMAC's two nested keyed inputs.</li>\n<li><strong>Digital signature</strong>: the asymmetric analog to a MAC, using a private/public key pair instead of a shared secret (see 5.4.c).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A matching hash proves the message wasn't tampered with.\" → Only if the attacker could not also recompute the hash after tampering. A plain, keyless hash gives no such guarantee once an attacker can rewrite both the message and its accompanying hash.</li>\n<li>\"<code>H(key + message)</code> is basically the same as HMAC.\" → Naive concatenation is vulnerable to length-extension attacks on Merkle-Damgard hashes (MD5/SHA-1/SHA-256); HMAC's nested construction is specifically designed to prevent this.</li>\n<li>\"HMAC encrypts the message.\" → HMAC produces a tag for verification; it does not hide or encrypt the message content at all. Confidentiality requires separate encryption.</li>\n<li>\"Integrity and authenticity are the same thing.\" → Integrity means unaltered; authenticity means it really came from the claimed sender. A plain hash can offer integrity (over a trusted channel) without authenticity; HMAC offers both because only the key holder can produce a valid tag.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.3.B, 5.3.C</p>"},{"id":"5.4.a","kind":"pre","title":"Public/private key pairs","topic":"5.4","href":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every secure interaction on the internet, HTTPS, email signing, SSH login, ultimately depends on a single elegant idea: two mathematically linked numbers, one of which you can hand to anyone in the world, and one of which you keep to yourself forever. This is asymmetric cryptography, and the foundation of the whole system is the key pair.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The bootstrap problem worth sitting with before this lecture: symmetric cryptography (5.3) is strong once both sides share a key, but it never actually explains how two strangers agree on a secret over a network where anyone could be listening, asymmetric cryptography is the answer to that exact gap. The elegant part is that one of your two keys is designed to be handed to literally anyone, posted publicly, shouted across an open channel, and none of that weakens your security at all, because possessing the public key was never the thing protecting you.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"If I encrypt with the private key, it is more secure.\" → Encrypting with the private key does not provide confidentiality; anyone holding the public key can decrypt. Sign-with-private-key is used for authenticity, not secrecy.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how a public/private key pair is generated and used, describe the mathematical relationship between the two keys, and identify which key is used for encryption versus decryption in common asymmetric scenarios.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.4.a","kind":"post","title":"Public/private key pairs","topic":"5.4","href":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The image worth keeping is the one-way trapdoor: easy to fall through, computationally infeasible to climb back up without the specific private key that makes the climb possible. Multiplying two large primes together is trivial; factoring their product back into those primes, without already knowing one of them, is what real-world security rests on. Also lock in the two use cases now, because every atom for the rest of this section is just one of them elaborated: encrypt with the public key so only the private-key holder can read it (confidentiality, 5.4.a/b), or sign with the private key so anyone can verify only that key-holder could have produced it (authenticity, 5.4.c). Getting those two directions backwards is the single most common conceptual error students make in this unit, and it's worth drilling until \"encrypt-with-public, sign-with-private\" is automatic.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Asymmetric cryptography</strong>: a cryptographic system that uses two mathematically linked keys: a public key and a private key.</li>\n<li><strong>Public key</strong>: the key that can be freely distributed; used to encrypt messages or verify signatures.</li>\n<li><strong>Private key</strong>: the key kept secret by its owner; used to decrypt messages or create signatures.</li>\n<li><strong>Key pair</strong>: the matched (public key, private key) set derived together from the same mathematical relationship.</li>\n<li><strong>One-way trapdoor function</strong>: a function easy to compute in one direction but infeasible to reverse without special information (the trapdoor = private key).</li>\n<li><strong>Key size</strong>: the bit length of the key; larger sizes increase the difficulty of breaking the underlying mathematical problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"If I encrypt with the private key, it is more secure.\" → Encrypting with the private key does not provide confidentiality; anyone holding the public key can decrypt. Sign-with-private-key is used for authenticity, not secrecy.</li>\n<li>\"The two keys are interchangeable.\" → They are not: the mathematical relationship is asymmetric. What one key does, only its partner can undo.</li>\n<li>\"Publishing your public key lets attackers impersonate you.\" → Knowing your public key lets an attacker send you encrypted messages or verify your signatures, both harmless. Only possessing your private key would allow impersonation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.4.A</p>"},{"id":"5.4.b","kind":"pre","title":"RSA algorithm","topic":"5.4","href":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> 5.4.a introduced the key pair, (e, n) public, (d, n) private, both built from two primes p and q. This skill is about the actual algorithm that uses those numbers to scramble and unscramble a message: RSA, named for its inventors Rivest, Shamir, and Adleman. Everything RSA does boils down to one operation, applied twice: modular exponentiation.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> Walk into this lecture ready to do actual arithmetic, RSA is one of the few places in this course where the security concept and the math are the same object, not just a metaphor for it. Everything reduces to modular exponentiation done twice, once with each exponent, and the entire security guarantee rests on one asymmetry: computing <code>m^e mod n</code> is fast in both directions if you know the right exponent, but recovering d from e and n alone requires factoring n, which is the one direction nobody has found a fast way to do at real key sizes. Small numbers like p=3, q=11 make the steps legible; they don't make RSA weak, the operations at 2048 bits are identical, just computationally hopeless to reverse without d.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Encryption and decryption use different algorithms.\" → They use the exact same operation, modular exponentiation; only the exponent (e vs. d) and which key it comes from differ.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Carry out the RSA encrypt and decrypt operations by hand on small numbers (including deriving the private exponent d), and explain why modular exponentiation with a public exponent is easy to compute but infeasible to reverse without the private key.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.4.b","kind":"post","title":"RSA algorithm","topic":"5.4","href":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The connection worth keeping past this lecture is that encryption and signing (5.4.c) are the exact same modular-exponentiation machinery, just with the two exponents swapped in role: encrypting uses the public exponent first, decrypting uses the private one to undo it; signing uses the private exponent first, and anyone can verify with the public one. If the arithmetic in this skill feels solid, 5.4.c is not new math, it's the same math pointed the other direction for a different goal. Also worth remembering: real systems use e=65537 specifically because it's prime, computationally cheap to exponentiate with (thanks to its binary form), and large enough to dodge low-exponent attacks, a reminder that even in cryptography, the \"obvious\" choice (e=3, the smallest valid option) is often the wrong one once you account for how attackers actually behave.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>RSA</strong>: the asymmetric cryptosystem (Rivest-Shamir-Adleman) built on modular exponentiation and the difficulty of factoring large integers.</li>\n<li><strong>Modular exponentiation</strong>: computing <code>base^exponent mod modulus</code>; the single operation underlying both RSA encryption and decryption.</li>\n<li><strong>Ciphertext (c)</strong>: the encrypted integer produced by <code>c = m^e mod n</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Modular inverse</strong>: the value d such that <code>e * d ≡ 1 (mod phi(n))</code>; computed via the extended Euclidean algorithm.</li>\n<li><strong>Extended Euclidean algorithm</strong>: an efficient method for computing modular inverses (and greatest common divisors) without brute-force search.</li>\n<li><strong>e = 65537</strong>: the near-universal choice of public exponent in real RSA implementations, chosen for primality, computational efficiency, and resistance to low-exponent attacks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Encryption and decryption use different algorithms.\" → They use the exact same operation, modular exponentiation; only the exponent (e vs. d) and which key it comes from differ.</li>\n<li>\"You can pick any e and d as long as they're different.\" → e and d must satisfy <code>e * d ≡ 1 (mod phi(n))</code>; d is mathematically determined by e, p, and q, not chosen freely.</li>\n<li>\"Small numbers in the worked example mean RSA is easy to break.\" → The arithmetic is identical at any size; security comes from using primes hundreds of digits long, where factoring n becomes computationally infeasible, not from the operations themselves being complex.</li>\n<li>\"A message can be any size.\" → In real RSA, m must be a numeric value smaller than n; larger content is chunked or (more commonly) RSA encrypts a symmetric key rather than the full message.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.4.A, 5.4.B</p>"},{"id":"5.4.c","kind":"pre","title":"Digital signatures","topic":"5.4","href":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> In 5.4.a you learned that a key pair can be used two ways: encrypt with the public key so only the\nprivate-key holder can decrypt, or sign with the private key so anyone with the public key can\nverify. This skill is about that second use case, the digital signature, which is the asymmetric\nanalogue of the HMAC you studied in 5.3.c.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The upgrade worth anticipating before this lecture: everything HMAC (5.3.c) gave you, proof the data wasn't altered, proof it came from someone who held a secret, a digital signature gives you too, plus one thing HMAC structurally cannot: non-repudiation. The reason is almost embarrassingly simple once you see it, HMAC's two parties share one secret, so either one of them could have produced any given tag, and there's no way to prove after the fact which one actually did. A signature uses two different keys for the two different actions, so only the signer's own private key could have produced it, and nobody, not even the verifier, could have forged it themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A digital signature encrypts the message for confidentiality.\" → It does not provide secrecy at all; the message is often sent in the clear alongside the signature. Signing proves authenticity and integrity, not confidentiality, that requires separately encrypting with the recipient's public key (5.4.a).</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how a digital signature is created and verified using an asymmetric key pair and a cryptographic hash, and articulate why a valid signature provides authenticity, integrity, and non-repudiation in a way that a shared-secret MAC (5.3.c) cannot.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.4.c","kind":"post","title":"Digital signatures","topic":"5.4","href":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The detail worth remembering longest is why you hash before you sign rather than signing the raw message directly: it keeps every signature a fixed, small size regardless of how long the original document is, it keeps the expensive RSA operation to a single pass, and it inherits the avalanche effect from 5.3.a so that a one-bit change anywhere in the message produces a completely different digest, meaning tampering is caught the instant verification recomputes the hash. Hold onto the three guarantees (authenticity, integrity, non-repudiation) as a set, because 5.4.d is about to show you the one gap none of this solves on its own: even a perfect signature only proves the person who holds a particular private key signed something, it says nothing about whether that person is who they claim to be. That identity-binding problem is exactly what PKI exists to close.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Digital signature</strong>: a value computed from a message digest and a private key that lets anyone with the matching public key verify the message's authenticity and integrity.</li>\n<li><strong>Signing</strong>: computing <code>signature = hash(message)^d mod n</code> with the signer's private exponent.</li>\n<li><strong>Verification</strong>: recomputing <code>hash(message)</code> and comparing it to <code>signature^e mod n</code>, computed with the signer's public exponent.</li>\n<li><strong>Authenticity</strong>: assurance that a message originated from the claimed sender.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-repudiation</strong>: the signer cannot plausibly deny having signed, since only they hold the private key; a property HMAC (shared-secret) schemes cannot provide.</li>\n<li><strong>Message digest</strong>: the fixed-size hash output that is actually signed, rather than signing the raw message.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A digital signature encrypts the message for confidentiality.\" → It does not provide secrecy at all; the message is often sent in the clear alongside the signature. Signing proves authenticity and integrity, not confidentiality, that requires separately encrypting with the recipient's public key (5.4.a).</li>\n<li>\"HMAC and digital signatures provide the same guarantees.\" → Both give integrity and authenticity, but only digital signatures give non-repudiation, because HMAC's shared secret means either party could have produced a valid tag.</li>\n<li>\"You sign the message directly with RSA.\" → Real schemes hash the message first and sign the fixed-size digest, both for performance and to avoid structural weaknesses in signing large raw inputs directly.</li>\n<li>\"If verification succeeds, the message was never read by anyone else in transit.\" → Verification proves integrity and authenticity, not secrecy; an unencrypted signed message can still be read by anyone who intercepts it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.4.B</p>"},{"id":"5.4.d","kind":"pre","title":"PKI and digital certificates","topic":"5.4","href":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.d/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.d/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> 5.4.a ended with a bootstrap problem it deliberately left unsolved: asymmetric cryptography lets you\nhand your public key to anyone, but that only helps if the person receiving it can be sure the key\nreally belongs to who it claims.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The unresolved thread worth carrying in from 5.4.a-c: you now know how to encrypt with someone's public key and verify their signature, but none of that math tells you the public key you received actually belongs to who it claims to. An attacker who swaps in their own key while claiming to be your bank makes every cryptographic guarantee from the last three atoms useless, you'd faithfully encrypt your password for the attacker, or faithfully \"verify\" a signature they forged with a key you never actually confirmed was your bank's. PKI is not new cryptography; it's the trust infrastructure that makes the cryptography you already learned actually mean something in the real world.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"A certificate proves the website is trustworthy/legitimate in a business sense.\" → It only proves that a CA verified the binding between the stated subject (usually just a domain name) and the public key presented. It says nothing about the site's content, intent, or safety.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain what a digital certificate contains and why, describe how a client walks a certificate chain up to a trusted root to decide whether to trust a public key, and identify why and how certificates get revoked before their stated expiration.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.4.d","kind":"post","title":"PKI and digital certificates","topic":"5.4","href":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.d/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.4/5.4.d/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The concept worth keeping longest is that PKI just pushes the trust question up a level, again and again, until it hits a point you agreed to trust out-of-band, a root CA pre-installed in your OS's trust store, vetted by the OS vendor rather than by you personally. Every certificate in the chain is really just 5.4.c's sign/verify operation reused: the issuer hashes the certificate's fields and signs the digest with its own private key, and the next link up verifies it the same way you'd verify any signature. Keep the revocation-versus-expiration distinction sharp for the exam, expiration is scheduled and built in, revocation is an emergency withdrawal of trust issued when the situation changes unexpectedly, most often because a private key was compromised. And notice the full-circle moment this skill represents: it closes out the cryptography arc (5.3-5.4) by tying keys, hashes, and signatures together into the one system, HTTPS, that quietly runs underneath almost every interaction covered earlier in this course, from the login forms of 5.1 to the credential theft of 5.2.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Digital certificate</strong>: a signed document binding a public key to a subject identity, issued and signed by a Certificate Authority.</li>\n<li><strong>Certificate Authority (CA)</strong>: an organization that verifies identities and signs certificates vouching for the binding between a subject and its public key.</li>\n<li><strong>Root CA</strong>: a CA whose self-signed certificate is pre-installed in an OS/browser trust store and trusted as the ultimate anchor of a chain.</li>\n<li><strong>Intermediate CA</strong>: a CA whose certificate is signed by a root (or another intermediate) and which signs end-entity (leaf) certificates.</li>\n<li><strong>Chain of trust</strong>: the sequence of signed certificates from a leaf up through intermediates to a trusted root, each link verified with the next certificate's public key.</li>\n<li><strong>Trust store</strong>: the curated set of root CA public keys an OS or browser trusts out of the box.</li>\n<li><strong>Certificate Revocation List (CRL)</strong>: a CA-signed, periodically updated list of revoked certificate serial numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>OCSP</strong>: Online Certificate Status Protocol; a real-time query/response check of one certificate's revocation status against a CA-operated responder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A certificate proves the website is trustworthy/legitimate in a business sense.\" → It only proves that a CA verified the binding between the stated subject (usually just a domain name) and the public key presented. It says nothing about the site's content, intent, or safety.</li>\n<li>\"Any self-signed certificate is inherently insecure.\" → Self-signed certificates use the same cryptography and can be perfectly secure once distributed and trusted through some other channel (e.g. manually installed on an internal network); the issue is that a random visitor's browser has no independent reason to trust it.</li>\n<li>\"Revocation and expiration are the same thing.\" → Expiration is a scheduled, built-in end to a certificate's validity window. Revocation is an unscheduled, active withdrawal of trust before that date, typically because the private key was compromised.</li>\n<li>\"The whole chain is checked with one signature.\" → Each certificate in the chain is signed and verified independently, using the public key of the certificate directly above it in the chain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.4.B, 5.4.C</p>"},{"id":"5.5.a","kind":"pre","title":"Input validation and sanitization","topic":"5.5","href":"unit-5/5.5/5.5.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.5/5.5.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Every application is, at its core, a machine that takes input from a user and does something with\nit: stores it, displays it, uses it to build a database query, or passes it to another program.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The rule worth memorizing cold before this lecture: client-side validation is a user-experience feature, not a security control, full stop, an attacker who disables JavaScript or sends a request with curl never touches your browser-side checks at all. That single fact rhymes directly with 5.1.c's BOLA lesson about hidden UI buttons: anything enforced only in the client was never actually enforced. Everything security-relevant in this skill has to happen again, independently, on the server, on every request, because the server is the one place the attacker can't reach in and edit.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Client-side (JavaScript) validation is good enough security.\" → It improves user experience but provides zero security guarantee, since an attacker fully controls the client and can bypass it entirely; all security-relevant validation must be re-checked on the server.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain why every application must treat user input as untrusted, distinguish allowlist from denylist validation, describe where validation must occur to be trustworthy, and identify how input validation prevents injection classes like SQL injection, cross-site scripting, and command injection.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.5.a","kind":"post","title":"Input validation and sanitization","topic":"5.5","href":"unit-5/5.5/5.5.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.5/5.5.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The idea worth keeping longest is that this skill is the unifying prevention layer for practically everything in 5.1: SQL injection, XSS, and command injection are three different <em>symptoms</em> of the exact same root failure, an application trusted that input was just data when a downstream interpreter (a SQL engine, a browser, a shell) was ready to treat some of those characters as instructions. Allowlisting flips the entire defensive posture from \"try to anticipate every attack\" to \"define what correct looks like and reject everything else,\" which is a fundamentally smaller and more stable problem to solve than enumerating attacker creativity. Keep canonicalization close too, validating before decoding is a classic bypass, because a filter checking the raw string never sees what the string actually becomes after the browser or server normalizes it, which is precisely how encoded path-traversal and injection payloads slip past naive filters.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Allowlist (whitelist) validation</strong>: accepting only input that matches a known-good pattern, rejecting everything else by default.</li>\n<li><strong>Denylist (blocklist) validation</strong>: rejecting input that matches known-bad patterns; weaker because it must anticipate every attack variant.</li>\n<li><strong>Canonicalization</strong>: reducing input to one normalized form before validating it, so encoded or obfuscated variants cannot slip past a filter that only checks the raw string.</li>\n<li><strong>Sanitization</strong>: actively stripping or rewriting dangerous content out of accepted input, rather than just accepting or rejecting it wholesale.</li>\n<li><strong>Output encoding</strong>: transforming data at the point it is inserted into a new context (HTML, SQL, shell) so special characters are treated as literal data, not executable syntax.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Client-side (JavaScript) validation is good enough security.\" → It improves user experience but provides zero security guarantee, since an attacker fully controls the client and can bypass it entirely; all security-relevant validation must be re-checked on the server.</li>\n<li>\"Sanitizing input once when it's received protects it everywhere it's later used.\" → The correct encoding depends on the output context (HTML vs. SQL vs. shell vs. JSON); a single upstream pass is not a substitute for context-specific output encoding at each use.</li>\n<li>\"A denylist that blocks the obvious bad keywords is enough.\" → Attackers routinely bypass denylists through case changes, encoding, and alternate syntax; allowlisting known-good shapes is far more robust than trying to enumerate every bad one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.5.A</p>"},{"id":"5.5.b","kind":"pre","title":"Secure coding practices","topic":"5.5","href":"unit-5/5.5/5.5.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.5/5.5.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Input validation (5.5.a) stops one specific failure, trusting untrusted data, but it is only\none piece of a much larger discipline called <strong>secure coding</strong>: the set of habits and defaults a\ndevelopment team builds into <em>every</em> line of code so that vulnerabilities are prevented by\nconstruction rather than caught after the fact.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The reframe worth making before this lecture: input validation (5.5.a) closes one specific door, but secure coding is the discipline of never building the other doors in the first place. Parameterized queries don't depend on catching a malicious string at all, they make it structurally impossible for user data to ever be parsed as SQL syntax, which is a stronger guarantee than any filter, because a filter can miss a variant it wasn't designed to catch and a parameterized query has nothing to miss.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"Secure coding is just input validation done well.\" → Input validation is one practice among several; parameterized queries, secrets management, least privilege, and dependency management each close a different, independent class of vulnerability.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> List the core secure-coding practices that prevent common vulnerability classes, explain why each practice works, and audit a piece of application configuration or code against those practices to identify violations.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.5.b","kind":"post","title":"Secure coding practices","topic":"5.5","href":"unit-5/5.5/5.5.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.5/5.5.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The connective thread worth keeping is that every practice in this skill is least privilege (5.2.a) applied to code instead of people: a database connection scoped to only the tables it needs, a web server process that never runs as root, an error handler that fails closed by default rather than accidentally granting access when something goes wrong. That's the same blast-radius logic from 5.2.b, just moved from human roles to running processes, if an attacker exploits a bug in code running with excessive privilege, the bug becomes a full system compromise instead of a contained one. Also worth remembering: a verbose error message leaking a stack trace or internal file path is not a cosmetic issue, it's free reconnaissance handed straight to an attacker, which connects forward to 5.6.a's point that what an application logs (and what it exposes) shapes how much an attacker can learn before you ever notice them.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Least privilege (in code)</strong>: running application processes and database connections with only the minimum access they need to function.</li>\n<li><strong>Parameterized query</strong>: a database query where user-supplied values are passed as separate, typed parameters rather than concatenated into the query string, preventing SQL injection.</li>\n<li><strong>Secrets management</strong>: storing credentials, API keys, and encryption keys outside source code (environment variables, a secrets manager) so they can be rotated without a code change.</li>\n<li><strong>Fail securely</strong>: designing error/exception paths to deny access by default rather than accidentally granting it when something unexpected happens.</li>\n<li><strong>Dependency management</strong>: pinning, tracking, and patching third-party library versions so a known vulnerability in a dependency does not become an unpatched vulnerability in the app.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Secure coding is just input validation done well.\" → Input validation is one practice among several; parameterized queries, secrets management, least privilege, and dependency management each close a different, independent class of vulnerability.</li>\n<li>\"A verbose error message is only a usability issue.\" → Detailed stack traces and internal paths shown to an attacker are reconnaissance information that materially helps them plan an attack.</li>\n<li>\"As long as the code works and passes QA, it's secure enough.\" → Functional correctness and security are different properties; code can pass every functional test while still building queries insecurely or running with excessive privilege.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.5.A, 5.5.B</p>"},{"id":"5.5.c","kind":"pre","title":"Application security testing (SAST/DAST)","topic":"5.5","href":"unit-5/5.5/5.5.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.5/5.5.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Writing secure code is not the same as knowing your code is secure. Every application security\nprogram eventually asks the same question: how do we find the vulnerabilities before an attacker\ndoes? Two complementary families of automated testing answer that question from opposite\ndirections, <strong>SAST</strong> (static application security testing) and <strong>DAST</strong> (dynamic application\nsecurity testing).</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The distinction worth carrying into this lecture: SAST reads code without running it, DAST attacks a running system without reading its code, and they are blind to exactly each other's territory, which is why the AP framework wants you to be able to sort a given finding into one bucket or the other, not just recognize that \"testing happened.\" SAST can see every code path, including ones nobody has exercised yet, but it can't tell whether a flagged pattern actually reaches attacker-controlled input, so it over-flags. DAST only knows what it actually attacked, but when it finds something, that something is almost always real.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"SAST and DAST find the same bugs, so you only need one.\" → They find different, mostly non-overlapping classes of issues, SAST sees the code but not runtime behavior; DAST sees runtime behavior but not the code. Mature pipelines run both.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain how static application security testing (SAST) and dynamic application security testing (DAST) find different classes of vulnerabilities, describe the tradeoffs of each, and classify a given finding as something SAST would catch, something DAST would catch, or neither.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.5.c","kind":"post","title":"Application security testing (SAST/DAST)","topic":"5.5","href":"unit-5/5.5/5.5.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.5/5.5.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The idea worth remembering longest is that neither SAST nor DAST, nor both together, is a substitute for a skilled human thinking like an attacker. Automated tools are exhaustive over known patterns and completely blind to business-logic flaws, the kind of thing a penetration tester spots by asking \"can a regular user manipulate this parameter to view someone else's invoice\", which, notice, is exactly the BOLA scenario from 5.1.c, a vulnerability class that neither a static scanner reading source code nor a dynamic scanner fuzzing inputs would reliably flag, because nothing about the request itself looks malformed. That's the clean argument for why mature programs run continuous automated scanning for volume and reserve human pentesting for depth, and it sets up 5.6 perfectly: everything from here forward in this unit shifts from <em>preventing</em> vulnerabilities before release to <em>detecting</em> attacks against whatever slipped through anyway.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SAST (static application security testing)</strong>: analyzing source code without executing it to find patterns such as unsafe SQL construction, hardcoded secrets, or dangerous function calls.</li>\n<li><strong>DAST (dynamic application security testing)</strong>: testing a running application from the outside by sending real requests and observing responses for vulnerabilities like XSS or auth bypass.</li>\n<li><strong>False positive</strong>: a tool flagging something as a vulnerability that is not actually exploitable; SAST tools tend to produce more of these than DAST tools.</li>\n<li><strong>CI/CD pipeline integration</strong>: running SAST/DAST automatically on every commit or deployment so vulnerabilities are caught continuously rather than only at a scheduled audit.</li>\n<li><strong>Penetration testing</strong>: human-driven security testing that chains findings and business-logic reasoning together in ways automated SAST/DAST scanners cannot.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"SAST and DAST find the same bugs, so you only need one.\" → They find different, mostly non-overlapping classes of issues, SAST sees the code but not runtime behavior; DAST sees runtime behavior but not the code. Mature pipelines run both.</li>\n<li>\"A clean SAST/DAST scan means the application has no vulnerabilities.\" → Both tools only find what they are built to look for; business-logic flaws and novel attack chains typically require human penetration testing to uncover.</li>\n<li>\"DAST can run as early as SAST in the pipeline.\" → DAST requires a running, reachable instance of the application, so it necessarily happens later than source-level SAST scanning.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.5.B</p>"},{"id":"5.6.a","kind":"pre","title":"Application monitoring and logging","topic":"5.6","href":"unit-5/5.6/5.6.a/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.6/5.6.a/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Applications are not silent. Every time a user logs in, a form is submitted, a file is opened, or an error is thrown, the application can record a structured line in its log, a timestamped, machine-readable trace of what just happened. Application monitoring is the practice of collecting and watching those traces in real time; logging is the act of writing them down so they can be examined later.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The line worth holding onto walking in: you cannot detect an attack you never recorded, which means logging isn't a forensic afterthought, it's the precondition for every detection technique the rest of this skill and the next two are about to cover. A single failed login is meaningless noise, people mistype passwords constantly. Fifty failed logins across twenty usernames from one IP in two minutes is an unmistakable pattern. The shift from \"reading individual events\" to \"reading the shape of many events together\" is the entire skill this skill is building.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"If no alarm fired, nothing bad happened.\" → Alarms only fire on configured rules; a misconfigured or absent rule creates a silent blind spot. Periodic manual log review is still necessary.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain what application logging captures, describe how security teams use log analysis to detect attacks in progress, and identify the key log fields and patterns that signal brute-force, injection, and account-takeover activity.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.6.a","kind":"post","title":"Application monitoring and logging","topic":"5.6","href":"unit-5/5.6/5.6.a/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.6/5.6.a/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The concept worth keeping past this lecture is that this skill's detection logic is the exact same move as 5.2.c's lateral-movement detection and 5.6.b's DLP behavior analysis, just aimed at a different signal: define what normal looks like, then flag whatever falls outside it, whether that's login frequency, host-hopping, or data-transfer volume. Once you notice that pattern repeating, detection engineering across this entire unit stops looking like a pile of unrelated tools and starts looking like one recurring template applied to authentication events, network hops, and outbound transfers respectively. Also worth remembering: logs are themselves an attack target, an attacker who achieves code execution will often try to erase their tracks, which is why the atom insists on append-only, remotely streamed logs. That single design choice is what makes chain-of-custody in 5.6.c even possible; evidence that could have been quietly edited by the attacker who generated it isn't evidence at all.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Application log</strong>: a time-ordered record of events inside an application, including authentication attempts, errors, and resource accesses.</li>\n<li><strong>Brute-force detection</strong>: identifying repeated, failed authentication attempts from a single source that suggest automated password guessing.</li>\n<li><strong>SIEM</strong>: Security Information and Event Management; a platform that centralizes logs and applies detection rules to generate security alerts.</li>\n<li><strong>Log aggregation</strong>: collecting logs from multiple sources into one place for unified analysis and long-term storage.</li>\n<li><strong>HTTP status code</strong>: a three-digit code in web server logs indicating request outcome (e.g., 200 = OK, 401 = unauthorized, 403 = forbidden, 500 = server error).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"If no alarm fired, nothing bad happened.\" → Alarms only fire on configured rules; a misconfigured or absent rule creates a silent blind spot. Periodic manual log review is still necessary.</li>\n<li>\"Logging is a performance problem, so we keep it minimal.\" → The security cost of a blind spot far exceeds the overhead of structured logging. Modern log pipelines handle high throughput efficiently.</li>\n<li>\"Deleting the logs covers an attacker's tracks completely.\" → If logs stream to a remote aggregator in real time, deletion of local copies does not erase the forensic record.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.6.A</p>"},{"id":"5.6.b","kind":"pre","title":"Data loss detection (DLP)","topic":"5.6","href":"unit-5/5.6/5.6.b/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.6/5.6.b/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> An organization can defend its perimeter, patch its applications, and monitor its logs, and still\nlose its most valuable asset: the data itself, quietly walking out the door.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The pairing worth carrying into this lecture: DLP fights the exact same battle as 5.6.a's log analysis, but content-based detection and volume/behavior-based detection split the work the way SAST and DAST split application testing in 5.5.c, one looks at <em>what</em> is moving, the other at <em>how much and how oddly</em> it's moving, and each catches what the other misses. An attacker who encrypts stolen data before exfiltrating it defeats content inspection completely, but a 3 AM upload of two gigabytes to a domain nobody's ever used before still lights up a behavior-based system, because that system never needed to understand the content at all.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"DLP only needs to scan for keywords like 'confidential'.\" → Content inspection is only half the picture; volume/behavior-based detection catches exfiltration that has no recognizable content pattern, such as encrypted or compressed data.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain what data loss prevention (DLP) systems detect and why, describe the difference between content-based and volume/behavior-based detection, and evaluate a stream of outbound data-transfer events to flag likely data exfiltration.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.6.b","kind":"post","title":"Data loss detection (DLP)","topic":"5.6","href":"unit-5/5.6/5.6.b/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.6/5.6.b/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> The idea worth keeping longest is that DLP is one of the few controls in this entire unit that can act preventively in real time rather than only forensically after the fact, it can block the email or quarantine the upload mid-attempt, not just record that it happened. That puts DLP in an interesting position relative to 5.1.d's severity scoring: time-to-detect was one of the three factors driving how bad a breach turns out to be, and a DLP system that catches exfiltration in progress is directly attacking that variable, potentially turning what would have been a severe, fully-completed breach into a contained, mostly-prevented one. Also keep the approved-destination-list detail close, it's the same allowlist-over-denylist philosophy from 5.5.a, just applied to network destinations instead of form-field values: defining what's known-good is more stable and less noisy than trying to flag every possible bad destination.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Data loss prevention (DLP)</strong>: tools and processes that detect and block sensitive data from leaving an organization's control without authorization.</li>\n<li><strong>Content-based detection</strong>: inspecting the actual data (patterns like credit-card numbers, SSNs, document classification labels) to recognize sensitive information leaving the network.</li>\n<li><strong>Volume/behavior-based detection</strong>: flagging anomalies in how much data moves, when, and to where, without needing to understand the data's content.</li>\n<li><strong>Egress monitoring</strong>: watching outbound network traffic at the perimeter for signs of data leaving the organization.</li>\n<li><strong>Approved destination list</strong>: a maintained allowlist of known-good transfer destinations (partners, sanctioned cloud storage) that DLP rules use to suppress false positives.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"DLP only needs to scan for keywords like 'confidential'.\" → Content inspection is only half the picture; volume/behavior-based detection catches exfiltration that has no recognizable content pattern, such as encrypted or compressed data.</li>\n<li>\"A large data transfer is always suspicious.\" → Legitimate large transfers (backups, sanctioned partner data sharing) happen constantly; effective DLP relies on an approved-destination list and account baselines, not raw size alone.</li>\n<li>\"DLP is purely detective, like a log review.\" → Well-integrated DLP can act preventively in real time, blocking or quarantining a transfer before it completes, not just recording it afterward.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.6.B</p>"},{"id":"5.6.c","kind":"pre","title":"Digital evidence analysis and attack classification","topic":"5.6","href":"unit-5/5.6/5.6.c/pre-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.6/5.6.c/banner.png","html":"<p><strong>Before class today.</strong> Detecting that <em>something</em> happened is only the first half of incident response.</p>\n<p><strong>The intuition to hold onto:</strong> The habit worth building before this lecture, and really before the rest of your life in this field: an alert is a hypothesis, not a verdict. A burst of failed logins suggests brute-force, but it could just as easily be a broken script retrying a stale password, the analyst's job starts, not ends, when the alert fires, and jumping straight to a conclusion without checking the underlying evidence is the single most common way a real incident gets mishandled: wrong IP blocked, wrong password reset, real attack vector missed entirely.</p>\n<p><strong>A myth worth busting before we start:</strong> \"One suspicious indicator is enough to declare the attack type.\" → A single IOC is rarely conclusive; classification should weigh the full set of collected evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Today's goal:</strong> Explain the chain-of-custody principles that make digital evidence trustworthy, describe the difference between an indicator of compromise (IOC) and a conclusion about attack type, and classify a set of collected evidence into the attack category it most strongly supports.\nHandout: <code>pre-lecture-handout.docx</code>.</p>"},{"id":"5.6.c","kind":"post","title":"Digital evidence analysis and attack classification","topic":"5.6","href":"unit-5/5.6/5.6.c/post-lecture-post.html","banner":"unit-5/5.6/5.6.c/banner.png","html":"<p>Nice work today. Here's what to lock in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap handout: <code>post-lecture-handout.docx</code></li>\n<li>Simulation: SimLab</li>\n<li>Self-check quiz: due before next class</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The big picture:</strong> As the final atom of the final unit, this is a genuinely fitting place to zoom out. Every detection tool this course has built, 5.6.a's log patterns, 5.6.b's DLP alerts, 5.2.c's lateral-movement flags, even 2.4's physical access-log anomalies from Unit 2, produces exactly the same kind of output: an indicator of compromise, a single observable fact that is rarely conclusive by itself. What separates a mature security program from an alert-driven panic is this skill's whole point, collecting multiple IOCs, weighing how strongly each one supports a given attack category, and reasoning to the best-supported classification rather than the first plausible one. Chain of custody matters for the same reason parameterized queries mattered back in 5.5.b: it's a structural guarantee (a hash taken at collection time, a documented access log) rather than a promise to be careful, which is exactly the pattern this whole course keeps returning to, trust the mechanism, not the good intentions. And notice the last thread this skill pulls taut: MITRE ATT&amp;CK's shared vocabulary is what lets the risk-triage instinct from Unit 2's very first atom (name the threat, then reason about it) scale from one analyst's judgment call to an entire organization's, and an entire industry's, shared understanding of what actually happened.</p>\n<p><strong>Key terms to lock in:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Indicator of compromise (IOC)</strong>: a single observable artifact (IP, file hash, unusual log entry) suggesting a security incident may be occurring; rarely conclusive on its own.</li>\n<li><strong>Attack classification</strong>: reasoning from a collection of IOCs to the attack category they collectively best support.</li>\n<li><strong>Chain of custody</strong>: a documented record of who collected, accessed, and stored evidence, so its integrity can be trusted (or challenged) later.</li>\n<li><strong>MITRE ATT&amp;CK</strong>: a widely-used framework providing a shared vocabulary for attack techniques, letting teams classify and compare incidents consistently.</li>\n<li><strong>Hash verification</strong>: computing a cryptographic hash of evidence at collection time so any later modification is detectable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common misconceptions to watch for:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"One suspicious indicator is enough to declare the attack type.\" → A single IOC is rarely conclusive; classification should weigh the full set of collected evidence.</li>\n<li>\"Chain of custody only matters for evidence going to court.\" → Internal incident reports and post-mortems also depend on evidence integrity for their conclusions to be trusted.</li>\n<li>\"Classification frameworks are just paperwork.\" → A shared vocabulary (like MITRE ATT&amp;CK) is what lets different analysts and teams compare and aggregate incidents meaningfully.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning objective(s):</strong> 5.6.B, 5.6.C, 5.6.D, 5.6.E</p>"}];
